{"cards":[
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.def-a-ratio","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"definition","front":"What is a ratio?","back":"A ratio is a mathematical number calculated as a reference to relationship of two or more numbers and can be expressed as a fraction, proportion, percentage and a number of times.","quote":"A ratio is a mathematical number calculated as a reference to relationship of two or more numbers and can be expressed as a fraction, proportion, percentage and a number of times.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":194,"section":"5.1 Meaning of Accounting Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["ratio"],"order":10},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.ex-an-inventory-turnover-ratio-is-6-what-does-i","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"example","front":"An inventory turnover ratio is 6. What does it imply?","back":"inventory turnover ratio may be 6 which implies that inventory turns into `Revenue from Operations' six times in a year.","quote":"inventory turnover ratio may be 6 which implies that inventory turns into `Revenue from Operations' six times in a year.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":195,"section":"5.1 Meaning of Accounting Ratios"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["inventory-turnover"],"order":50},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.def-ratio-analysis-ncert","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"definition","front":"What is ratio analysis (NCERT)?","back":"Ratio analysis is a technique which involves regrouping of data by application of arithmetical relationships, though its interpretation is a complex matter.","quote":"Ratio analysis is a technique which involves regrouping of data by application of arithmetical relationships, though its interpretation is a complex matter.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":195,"section":"5.2 Objectives of Ratio Analysis"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["ratio-analysis"],"order":70},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.list-name-the-5-objectives-of-ratio-analysis","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"list","front":"Name the 5 objectives of ratio analysis.","back":"1. To know the areas of the business which need more attention;\n2. To know about the potential areas which can be improved with the effort in the desired direction;\n3. To provide a deeper analysis of the profitability, liquidity, solvency and efficiency levels in the business;\n4. To provide information for making cross-sectional analysis by comparing the performance with the best industry standards; and\n5. To provide information derived from financial statements useful for making projections and estimates for the future.","quote":"1. To know the areas of the business which need more attention;\n2. To know about the potential areas which can be improved with the effort in the desired direction;\n3. To provide a deeper analysis of the profitability, liquidity, solvency and efficiency levels in the business;\n4. To provide information for making cross-sectional analysis by comparing the performance with the best industry standards; and\n5. To provide information derived from financial statements useful for making projections and estimates for the future.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":195,"section":"5.2 Objectives of Ratio Analysis"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["objectives"],"order":80},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.list-name-the-advantages-of-ratio-analysis","v":2,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"list","front":"Name the advantages of ratio analysis.","back":"1. Helps to understand efficacy of decisions\n2. Simplify complex figures and establish relationships\n3. Helpful in comparative analysis\n4. Identification of problem areas\n5. Enables SWOT analysis\n6. Various comparisons","quote":"1. Helps to understand efficacy of decisions: The ratio analysis helps you to understand whether the business firm has taken the right kind of operating, investing and financing decisions. It indicates how far they have helped in improving the performance. 2. Simplify complex figures and establish relationships: Ratios help in simplifying the complex accounting figures and bring out their relationships. They help summarise the financial information effectively and assess the managerial efficiency, firm’s credit worthiness, earning capacity, etc. 3. Helpful in comparative analysis: The ratios are not be calculated for one year only. When many year figures are kept side by side, they help a great deal in exploring the trends visible in the business. The knowledge of trend helps in making projections about the business which is a very useful feature. 4. Identification of problem areas: Ratios help business in identifying the problem areas as well as the bright areas of the business. Problem areas would need more attention and bright areas will need polishing to have still better results. 5. Enables SWOT analysis: Ratios help a great deal in explaining the changes occurring in the business. The information of change helps the management a great deal in understanding the current threats and opportunities and allows business to do its own SWOT (Strength-Weakness-Opportunity-Threat) analysis. 6. Various comparisons:","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":196,"section":"5.3 Advantages of Ratio Analysis"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["advantages"],"order":90},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.key-their-role-is-essentially-indicative-and-tha","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"key_line","front":"Their role is essentially indicative and that of a ____.","back":"Their role is essentially indicative and that of a whistle blower.","quote":"Their role is essentially indicative and that of a whistle blower.","blanks":["whistle blower"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":196,"section":"5.3 Advantages of Ratio Analysis"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["advantages"],"order":110},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.types-ratios-help-comparison-in-which-three-ways","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"types","front":"Ratios help comparison in which three ways?","back":"(i) over a number of accounting periods with itself (Intra-firm Comparison/Time Series Analysis), (ii) with other business enterprises (Inter-firm Comparison/Cross-sectional Analysis) and (iii) with standards set for that firm/industry","quote":"(i) over a number of accounting periods with itself (Intra-firm Comparison/Time Series Analysis), (ii) with other business enterprises (Inter-firm Comparison/Cross-sectional Analysis) and (iii) with standards set for that firm/industry","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":196,"section":"5.3 Advantages of Ratio Analysis"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["comparison"],"order":130},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.list-ratio-comparisons","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"list","front":"Name the three comparisons that ratios help to make against benchmarks.","back":"(i) over a number of accounting periods with itself (Intra-firm Comparison/Time Series Analysis)\n(ii) with other business enterprises (Inter-firm Comparison/Cross-sectional Analysis)\n(iii) with standards set for that firm/industry (comparison with standard (or industry expectations)","quote":"(i) over a number of accounting periods with itself (Intra-firm Comparison/Time Series Analysis), (ii) with other business enterprises (Inter-firm Comparison/Cross-sectional Analysis) and (iii) with standards set for that firm/industry (comparison with standard (or industry expectations).","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":196,"section":"5.3 Advantages of Ratio Analysis"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["comparison"],"order":140},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.def-why-do-weaknesses-in-financial-statements-li","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"definition","front":"Why do weaknesses in financial statements limit ratio analysis (NCERT)?","back":"Since the ratios are derived from the financial statements, any weakness in the original financial statements will also creep in the derived analysis in the form of ratio analysis.","quote":"Since the ratios are derived from the financial statements, any weakness in the original financial statements will also creep in the derived analysis in the form of ratio analysis.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":197,"section":"5.4 Limitations of Ratio Analysis"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["limitations"],"order":160},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.list-name-the-limitations-of-ratio-analysis-that","v":2,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"list","front":"Name the limitations of ratio analysis that arise from the nature of financial statements.","back":"1. Limitations of Accounting Data\n2. Ignores Price-level Changes\n3. Ignore Qualitative or Non-monetary Aspects\n4. Variations in Accounting Practices\n5. Forecasting","quote":"1. Limitations of Accounting Data: Accounting data give an unwarranted impression of precision and finality. In fact, accounting data “reflect a combination of recorded facts, accounting conventions and personal judgements which affect them materially. For example, profit of the business is not a precise and final figure. It is merely an opinion of the accountant based on application of accounting policies. The soundness of the judgement necessarily depends on the competence and integrity of those who make them and on their adherence to Generally Accepted Accounting Principles and Conventions”. Thus, the financial statements may not reveal the true state of affairs of the enterprises and so the ratios will also not give the true picture. 2. Ignores Price-level Changes: The financial accounting is based on stable money measurement principle. It implicitly assumes that price level changes are either non-existent or minimal. But the truth is otherwise. We are normally living in inflationary economies where the power of money declines constantly. A change in the price-level makes analysis of financial statement of different accounting years meaningless because accounting records ignore changes in value of money. 3. Ignore Qualitative or Non-monetary Aspects: Accounting provides information about quantitative (or monetary) aspects of business. Hence, the ratios also reflect only the monetary aspects, ignoring completely the non-monetary (qualitative) factors. 4. Variations in Accounting Practices: There are differing accounting policies for valuation of inventory, calculation of depreciation, treatment of intangibles Assets definition of certain financial variables etc., available for various aspects of business transactions. These variations leave a big question mark on the cross-sectional analysis. As there are variations in accounting practices followed by different business enterprises, a valid comparison of their financial statements is not possible. 5. Forecasting:","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":197,"section":"5.4 Limitations of Ratio Analysis"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["limitations"],"order":170},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.list-name-the-limitations-of-the-ratios-themselve","v":2,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"list","front":"Name the limitations of the ratios themselves.","back":"1. Means and not the End\n2. Lack of ability to resolve problems\n3. Lack of standardised definitions\n4. Lack of universally accepted standard levels\n5. Ratios based on unrelated figures","quote":"1. Means and not the End: Ratios are means to an end rather than the end by itself. 2. Lack of ability to resolve problems: Their role is essentially indicative and of whistle blowing and not providing a solution to the problem. 3. Lack of standardised definitions: There is a lack of standardised definitions of various concepts used in ratio analysis. For example, there is no standard definition of liquid liabilities. Normally, it includes all current liabilities, but sometimes it refers to current liabilities less bank overdraft. 4. Lack of universally accepted standard levels: There is no universal yardstick which specifies the level of ideal ratios. There is no standard list of the levels universally acceptable, and, in India, the industry averages are also not available. 5. Ratios based on unrelated figures:","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":198,"section":"5.4 Limitations of Ratio Analysis"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["limitations"],"order":180},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.key-the-financial-accounting-is-based-on-stable","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"key_line","front":"The financial accounting is based on ____ principle.","back":"The financial accounting is based on stable money measurement principle.","quote":"The financial accounting is based on stable money measurement principle.","blanks":["stable money measurement"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":197,"section":"5.4 Limitations of Ratio Analysis"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["limitations","price-level"],"order":210},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.key-accounting-provides-information-about-quanti","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"key_line","front":"Accounting provides information about ____ (or monetary) aspects of business.","back":"Accounting provides information about quantitative (or monetary) aspects of business.","quote":"Accounting provides information about quantitative (or monetary) aspects of business.","blanks":["quantitative"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":197,"section":"5.4 Limitations of Ratio Analysis"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["limitations"],"order":220},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.key-forecasting-of-future-trends-based-only-on-h","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"key_line","front":"Forecasting of future trends based only on ____ analysis is not feasible.","back":"Forecasting of future trends based only on historical analysis is not feasible.","quote":"Forecasting of future trends based only on historical analysis is not feasible.","blanks":["historical"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":197,"section":"5.4 Limitations of Ratio Analysis"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["limitations"],"order":230},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.key-there-is-no-universal-yardstick-which-specif","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"key_line","front":"There is no ____ which specifies the level of ideal ratios.","back":"There is no universal yardstick which specifies the level of ideal ratios.","quote":"There is no universal yardstick which specifies the level of ideal ratios.","blanks":["universal yardstick"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":198,"section":"5.4 Limitations of Ratio Analysis"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["limitations"],"order":240},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.types-the-two-classifications-of-ratios","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"types","front":"What are the two classifications of ratios?","back":"There is a two way classification of ratios: (1) traditional classification, and (2) functional classification.","quote":"There is a two way classification of ratios: (1) traditional classification, and (2) functional classification.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":198,"section":"5.5 Types of Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["types"],"order":280},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.def-ratio-analysis","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"definition","front":"What is ratio analysis?","back":"It describes the significant relationship which exists between various items of a balance sheet and a statement of profit and loss of a firm.","quote":"It describes the significant relationship which exists between various items of a balance sheet and a statement of profit and loss of a firm.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy : Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac204","chapterNo":4,"page":175,"section":"4.4 Tools of Analysis of Financial Statements"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["ratio analysis"],"order":290},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.types-types-of-ratios-in-the-traditional-classific","v":2,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"types","front":"Types of ratios in the traditional classification.","back":"1. Statement of Profit and Loss Ratios\n2. Balance Sheet Ratios\n3. Composite Ratios","quote":"1. ‘Statement of Profit and Loss Ratios: A ratio of two variables from the statement of profit and loss is known as statement of profit and loss ratio. For example, ratio of gross profit to revenue from operations is known as gross profit ratio. It is calculated using both figures from the statement of profit and loss. 2. Balance Sheet Ratios: In case both variables are from the balance sheet, it is classified as balance sheet ratios. For example, ratio of current assets to current liabilities known as current ratio. It is calculated using both figures from balance sheet. 3. Composite Ratios:","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":199,"section":"5.5 Types of Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["types"],"order":290},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.def-a-statement-of-profit-and-loss-ratio","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"definition","front":"What is a statement of profit and loss ratio?","back":"A ratio of two variables from the statement of profit and loss is known as statement of profit and loss ratio.","quote":"A ratio of two variables from the statement of profit and loss is known as statement of profit and loss ratio.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":199,"section":"5.5 Types of Ratios"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["types"],"order":300},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fact-profitability-solvency-efficiency","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"fact","front":"What can be assessed through the technique of ratio analysis?","back":"It is possible to assess the profitability, solvency and efficiency of an enterprise through the technique of ratio analysis.","quote":"It is possible to assess the profitability, solvency and efficiency of an enterprise through the technique of ratio analysis.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy : Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac204","chapterNo":4,"page":175,"section":"4.4 Tools of Analysis of Financial Statements"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["ratio analysis"],"order":300},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.def-balance-sheet-ratios","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"definition","front":"What are balance sheet ratios?","back":"In case both variables are from the balance sheet, it is classified as balance sheet ratios.","quote":"In case both variables are from the balance sheet, it is classified as balance sheet ratios.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":199,"section":"5.5 Types of Ratios"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["types"],"order":310},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.def-a-composite-ratio","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"definition","front":"What is a composite ratio?","back":"If a ratio is computed with one variable from the statement of profit and loss and another variable from the balance sheet, it is called composite ratio.","quote":"If a ratio is computed with one variable from the statement of profit and loss and another variable from the balance sheet, it is called composite ratio.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":199,"section":"5.5 Types of Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["types"],"order":320},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.ex-which-type-of-ratio-is-the-ratio-of-current","v":2,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"example","front":"Under the traditional classification, which type of ratio is current assets to current liabilities?","back":"Balance sheet ratio.\nIn case both variables are from the balance sheet, it is classified as balance sheet ratios. For example, ratio of current assets to current liabilities known as current ratio.","quote":"2. Balance Sheet Ratios: In case both variables are from the balance sheet, it is classified as balance sheet ratios. For example, ratio of current assets to current liabilities known as current ratio.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":199,"section":"5.5 Types of Ratios"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["types"],"order":330},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.types-types-of-ratios-in-the-functional-classifica","v":2,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"types","front":"Types of ratios in the functional classification.","back":"1. Liquidity Ratios\n2. Solvency Ratios\n3. Activity (or Turnover) Ratios\n4. Profitability Ratios","quote":"1. Liquidity Ratios: To meet its commitments, business needs liquid funds. The ability of the business to pay the amount due to stakeholders as and when it is due is known as liquidity, and the ratios calculated to measure it are known as ‘Liquidity Ratios’. These are essentially short-term in nature. 2. Solvency Ratios: Solvency of business is determined by its ability to meet its contractual obligations towards stakeholders, particularly towards external stakeholders, and the ratios calculated to measure solvency position are known as ‘Solvency Ratios’. These are essentially long-term in nature. 3. Activity (or Turnover) Ratios: This refers to the ratios that are calculated for measuring the efficiency of operations of business based on effective utilisation of resources. Hence, these are also known as ‘Efficiency Ratios’. 4. Profitability Ratios:","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":199,"section":"5.5 Types of Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["types"],"order":340},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.def-liquidity-ratios","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"definition","front":"What are liquidity ratios?","back":"The ability of the business to pay the amount due to stakeholders as and when it is due is known as liquidity, and the ratios calculated to measure it are known as `Liquidity Ratios'. These are essentially short-term in nature.","quote":"The ability of the business to pay the amount due to stakeholders as and when it is due is known as liquidity, and the ratios calculated to measure it are known as `Liquidity Ratios'. These are essentially short-term in nature.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":199,"section":"5.5 Types of Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["liquidity"],"order":350},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.def-solvency-ratios","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"definition","front":"What are solvency ratios?","back":"Solvency of business is determined by its ability to meet its contractual obligations towards stakeholders, particularly towards external stakeholders, and the ratios calculated to measure solvency position are known as `Solvency Ratios'. These are essentially long-term in nature.","quote":"Solvency of business is determined by its ability to meet its contractual obligations towards stakeholders, particularly towards external stakeholders, and the ratios calculated to measure solvency position are known as `Solvency Ratios'. These are essentially long-term in nature.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":199,"section":"5.5 Types of Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["solvency"],"order":360},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.def-activity-or-turnover-ratios","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"definition","front":"What are activity (or turnover) ratios?","back":"This refers to the ratios that are calculated for measuring the efficiency of operations of business based on effective utilisation of resources. Hence, these are also known as `Efficiency Ratios'.","quote":"This refers to the ratios that are calculated for measuring the efficiency of operations of business based on effective utilisation of resources. Hence, these are also known as `Efficiency Ratios'.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":199,"section":"5.5 Types of Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["activity"],"order":370},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.def-profitability-ratios","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"definition","front":"What are profitability ratios?","back":"It refers to the analysis of profits in relation to revenue from operations or funds (or assets) employed in the business and the ratios calculated to meet this objective are known as `Profitability Ratios'.","quote":"It refers to the analysis of profits in relation to revenue from operations or funds (or assets) employed in the business and the ratios calculated to meet this objective are known as `Profitability Ratios'.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":199,"section":"5.5 Types of Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["profitability"],"order":380},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.def-liquidity-ratios-calculated-to-measure","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"definition","front":"What are liquidity ratios calculated to measure?","back":"Liquidity ratios are calculated to measure the short-term solvency of the business, i.e. the firm's ability to meet its current obligations.","quote":"Liquidity ratios are calculated to measure the short-term solvency of the business, i.e. the firm's ability to meet its current obligations.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":200,"section":"5.6 Liquidity Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["liquidity"],"order":390},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.types-which-two-ratios-are-liquidity-ratios","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"types","front":"Which two ratios are liquidity ratios?","back":"The two ratios included in this category are current ratio and liquidity ratio.","quote":"The two ratios included in this category are current ratio and liquidity ratio.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":200,"section":"5.6 Liquidity Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["liquidity"],"order":400},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.def-the-current-ratio","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"definition","front":"What is the current ratio?","back":"Current ratio is the proportion of current assets to current liabilities.","quote":"Current ratio is the proportion of current assets to current liabilities.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":200,"section":"5.6 Liquidity Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["current-ratio"],"order":410},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fml-current-ratio","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"formula","front":"Formula: Current ratio","back":"Current Ratio = Current Assets : Current Liabilities","quote":"Current Ratio = Current Assets : Current Liabilities","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":200,"section":"5.6 Liquidity Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["current-ratio"],"order":420},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.types-current-assets-include-which-items","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"types","front":"Current assets include which items?","back":"Current assets include current investments, inventories, trade receivables (debtors and bills receivables), cash and cash equivalents, short-term loans and advances and other current assets such as prepaid expenses, advance tax and accrued income, etc.","quote":"Current assets include current investments, inventories, trade receivables (debtors and bills receivables), cash and cash equivalents, short-term loans and advances and other current assets such as prepaid expenses, advance tax and accrued income, etc.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":200,"section":"5.6 Liquidity Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["current-ratio","current-assets"],"order":430},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.types-current-liabilities-include-which-items","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"types","front":"Current liabilities include which items?","back":"Current liabilities include short-term borrowings, trade payables (creditors and bills payables), other current liabilities and short-term provisions.","quote":"Current liabilities include short-term borrowings, trade payables (creditors and bills payables), other current liabilities and short-term provisions.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":200,"section":"5.6 Liquidity Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["current-ratio","current-liabilities"],"order":440},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.def-what-does-the-current-ratio-measure-signific","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"definition","front":"What does the current ratio measure (significance)?","back":"It provides a measure of degree to which current assets cover current liabilities.","quote":"It provides a measure of degree to which current assets cover current liabilities.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":201,"section":"5.6 Liquidity Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["current-ratio"],"order":450},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fact-what-does-a-very-high-current-ratio-imply","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"fact","front":"What does a very high current ratio imply?","back":"A very high current ratio implies heavy investment in current assets which is not a good sign as it reflects under utilisation or improper utilisation of resources.","quote":"A very high current ratio implies heavy investment in current assets which is not a good sign as it reflects under utilisation or improper utilisation of resources.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":201,"section":"5.6 Liquidity Ratios"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["current-ratio"],"order":460},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fact-what-risk-does-a-low-current-ratio-carry","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"fact","front":"What risk does a low current ratio carry?","back":"A low ratio endangers the business and puts it at risk of facing a situation where it will not be able to pay its short-term debt on time.","quote":"A low ratio endangers the business and puts it at risk of facing a situation where it will not be able to pay its short-term debt on time.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":201,"section":"5.6 Liquidity Ratios"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["current-ratio"],"order":470},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fact-current-ratio-safe-range","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"fact","front":"What is the normally safe range of the current ratio?","back":"2:1\nNormally, it is safe to have this ratio within the range of 2:1.","quote":"Normally, it is safe to have this ratio within the range of 2:1.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":201,"section":"5.6 Liquidity Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["current-ratio"],"order":480},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.def-the-quick-or-liquid-ratio","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"definition","front":"What is the quick (or liquid) ratio?","back":"It is the ratio of quick (or liquid) asset to current liabilities.","quote":"It is the ratio of quick (or liquid) asset to current liabilities.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":201,"section":"5.6 Liquidity Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["quick-ratio"],"order":490},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fml-quick-ratio","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"formula","front":"Formula: Quick ratio","back":"Quick ratio = Quick Assets : Current Liabilities","quote":"Quick ratio = Quick Assets : Current Liabilities","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":201,"section":"5.6 Liquidity Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["quick-ratio"],"order":500},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.def-quick-assets","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"definition","front":"What are quick assets?","back":"The quick assets are defined as those assets which are quickly convertible into cash.","quote":"The quick assets are defined as those assets which are quickly convertible into cash.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":201,"section":"5.6 Liquidity Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["quick-ratio","quick-assets"],"order":510},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fact-which-items-are-excluded-when-calculating-qu","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"fact","front":"Which items are excluded when calculating quick assets?","back":"While calculating quick assets we exclude the inventories at the end and other current assets such as prepaid expenses, advance tax, etc., from the current assets.","quote":"While calculating quick assets we exclude the inventories at the end and other current assets such as prepaid expenses, advance tax, etc., from the current assets.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":201,"section":"5.6 Liquidity Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["quick-ratio","quick-assets"],"order":520},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fact-why-is-the-quick-ratio-considered-better-tha","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"fact","front":"Why is the quick ratio considered better than the current ratio?","back":"Because of exclusion of non-liquid current assets it is considered better than current ratio as a measure of liquidity position of the business.","quote":"Because of exclusion of non-liquid current assets it is considered better than current ratio as a measure of liquidity position of the business.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":201,"section":"5.6 Liquidity Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["quick-ratio"],"order":540},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fact-quick-ratio-acid-test","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"fact","front":"The quick (liquid) ratio is a supplementary check on liquidity. What is it also known as?","back":"Acid-Test Ratio.\nIt is calculated to serve as a supplementary check on liquidity position of the business and is therefore, also known as ‘Acid-Test Ratio’.","quote":"It is calculated to serve as a supplementary check on liquidity position of the business and is therefore, also known as ‘Acid-Test Ratio’.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":201,"section":"5.6 Liquidity Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["quick-ratio"],"order":550},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fact-what-quick-ratio-is-normally-advocated-as-sa","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"fact","front":"What quick ratio is normally advocated as safe?","back":"safe to have a ratio of 1:1 as unnecessarily low ratio will be very risky and a high ratio suggests unnecessarily deployment of resources in otherwise less profitable short-term investments.","quote":"safe to have a ratio of 1:1 as unnecessarily low ratio will be very risky and a high ratio suggests unnecessarily deployment of resources in otherwise less profitable short-term investments.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":202,"section":"5.6 Liquidity Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["quick-ratio"],"order":560},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.ex-current-ratio-is-2-1-effect-of-paying-a-curr","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"example","front":"Current ratio is 2:1. Effect of paying a current liability (creditors) on the ratio?","back":"Assume that Rs. 10,000 of creditors is paid by cheque. This will reduce the current assets to Rs. 40,000 and current liabilities to Rs. 15,000. The new ratio will be 2.67 : 1 (Rs. 40,000/Rs.15,000). Hence, it has improved.","quote":"Assume that Rs. 10,000 of creditors is paid by cheque. This will reduce the current assets to Rs. 40,000 and current liabilities to Rs. 15,000. The new ratio will be 2.67 : 1 (Rs. 40,000/Rs.15,000). Hence, it has improved.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":204,"section":"5.6 Liquidity Ratios"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["current-ratio","effect-of-transactions"],"order":580},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.ex-current-ratio-is-2-1-effect-of-purchasing-go","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"example","front":"Current ratio is 2:1. Effect of purchasing goods on credit on the ratio?","back":"Assume that goods of Rs. 10,000 are purchased on credit. This will increase the current assets to Rs. 60,000 and current liabilities to Rs. 35,000. The new ratio will be 1.7:1 (Rs. 60,000/Rs. 35,000). Hence, it has reduced.","quote":"Assume that goods of Rs. 10,000 are purchased on credit. This will increase the current assets to Rs. 60,000 and current liabilities to Rs. 35,000. The new ratio will be 1.7:1 (Rs. 60,000/Rs. 35,000). Hence, it has reduced.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":204,"section":"5.6 Liquidity Ratios"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["current-ratio","effect-of-transactions"],"order":590},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.ex-current-ratio-is-2-1-effect-of-selling-a-com","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"example","front":"Current ratio is 2:1. Effect of selling a computer (a fixed asset) below book value?","back":"Due to sale of a computer (a fixed asset) the current assets will increase to Rs. 53,000 without any change in the current liabilities. The new ratio will be 2.12 : 1 (Rs. 53,000/Rs. 25,000). Hence, it has improved.","quote":"Due to sale of a computer (a fixed asset) the current assets will increase to Rs. 53,000 without any change in the current liabilities. The new ratio will be 2.12 : 1 (Rs. 53,000/Rs. 25,000). Hence, it has improved.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":204,"section":"5.6 Liquidity Ratios"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["current-ratio","effect-of-transactions"],"order":600},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.ex-current-ratio-is-2-1-effect-of-selling-goods","v":2,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"example","front":"Current ratio is 2:1. Effect of selling goods costing Rs. 10,000 for Rs. 11,000?","back":"Improves the current ratio.\nThis transaction will decrease the inventories by Rs. 10,000 and increase the cash by Rs. 11,000 thereby increasing the current assets by Rs. 1,000 without any change in the current liabilities. The new ratio will be 2.04 : 1 (Rs. 51,000/Rs. 25,000). Hence, it has improved.","quote":"This transaction will decrease the inventories by Rs. 10,000 and increase the cash by Rs. 11,000 thereby increasing the current assets by Rs. 1,000 without any change in the current liabilities. The new ratio will be 2.04 : 1 (Rs. 51,000/Rs. 25,000). Hence, it has improved.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":204,"section":"5.6 Liquidity Ratios"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["current-ratio","effect-of-transactions"],"order":610},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.def-solvency-ratios-calculated-to-determine","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"definition","front":"What are solvency ratios calculated to determine?","back":"Solvency ratios are calculated to determine the ability of the business to service its debt in the long run.","quote":"Solvency ratios are calculated to determine the ability of the business to service its debt in the long run.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":205,"section":"5.7 Solvency Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["solvency"],"order":620},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.types-name-the-solvency-ratios","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"types","front":"Name the solvency ratios.","back":"1. Debt-Equity Ratio;\n2. Debt to Capital Employed Ratio;\n3. Proprietary Ratio;\n4. Total Assets to Debt Ratio;\n5. Interest Coverage Ratio.","quote":"1. Debt-Equity Ratio;\n2. Debt to Capital Employed Ratio;\n3. Proprietary Ratio;\n4. Total Assets to Debt Ratio;\n5. Interest Coverage Ratio.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":205,"section":"5.7 Solvency Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["solvency"],"order":630},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.def-what-does-the-debt-equity-ratio-measure","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"definition","front":"What does the debt-equity ratio measure?","back":"Debt-Equity Ratio measures the relationship between long-term debt and equity.","quote":"Debt-Equity Ratio measures the relationship between long-term debt and equity.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":205,"section":"5.7 Solvency Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["debt-equity"],"order":640},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fml-debt-equity-ratio","v":2,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"formula","front":"Formula: Debt-equity ratio","back":"Debt-Equity Ratio = Long-term Debts / Shareholders’ Funds","quote":"Debt-Equity Ratio = Long-term Debts / Shareholders’ Funds","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":205,"section":"5.7 Solvency Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["debt-equity"],"order":650,"note":"Normally considered safe if the debt equity ratio is 2 : 1; it may vary from industry to industry (NCERT)."},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fact-which-capital-structure-is-considered-favour","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"fact","front":"Which capital structure is considered favourable from the security point of view?","back":"From security point of view, capital structure with less debt and more equity is considered favourable as it reduces the chances of bankruptcy.","quote":"From security point of view, capital structure with less debt and more equity is considered favourable as it reduces the chances of bankruptcy.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":205,"section":"5.7 Solvency Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["debt-equity"],"order":660},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.key-normally-it-is-considered-to-be-safe-if-debt","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"key_line","front":"Normally, it is considered to be safe if debt equity ratio is ____.","back":"Normally, it is considered to be safe if debt equity ratio is 2 : 1.","quote":"Normally, it is considered to be safe if debt equity ratio is 2 : 1.","blanks":["2 : 1"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":205,"section":"5.7 Solvency Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["debt-equity"],"order":670},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fml-shareholders-funds-equity-first-form","v":2,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"formula","front":"Formula: Shareholders' Funds (Equity), as the sum of share capital and reserve items","back":"Shareholders’ Funds (Equity) = Share capital + Reserves and Surplus + Money received against share warrants + Share application money pending allotment","quote":"Shareholders’ Funds (Equity) = Share capital + Reserves and Surplus + Money received against share warrants + Share application money pending allotment","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":205,"section":"5.7 Solvency Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["shareholders-funds"],"order":680},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fml-share-capital-as-part-of-shareholders-funds","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"formula","front":"Formula: Share capital (as part of shareholders' funds)","back":"Equity share capital + Preference share capital","quote":"Equity share capital + Preference share capital","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":205,"section":"5.7 Solvency Ratios"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["shareholders-funds"],"order":690},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fml-working-capital","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"formula","front":"Formula: Working capital","back":"Current Assets – Current Liabilities","quote":"Current Assets – Current Liabilities","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":205,"section":"5.7 Solvency Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["working-capital"],"order":700},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fact-what-does-the-debt-equity-ratio-indicate-to","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"fact","front":"What does the debt-equity ratio indicate to a long-term lender?","back":"This ratio measures the degree of indebtedness of an enterprise and gives an idea to the long-term lender regarding extent of security of the debt.","quote":"This ratio measures the degree of indebtedness of an enterprise and gives an idea to the long-term lender regarding extent of security of the debt.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":205,"section":"5.7 Solvency Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["debt-equity"],"order":710},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.key-a-low-debt-equity-ratio-reflects-more-securi","v":2,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"key_line","front":"A ____ debt equity ratio reflects more security.","back":"A low debt equity ratio reflects more security.","quote":"A low debt equity ratio reflects more security.","blanks":["low"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":205,"section":"5.7 Solvency Ratios"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["debt-equity"],"order":720},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fact-why-is-a-high-debt-equity-ratio-considered-r","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"fact","front":"Why is a high debt-equity ratio considered risky?","back":"A high ratio, on the other hand, is considered risky as it may put the firm into difficulty in meeting its obligations to outsiders.","quote":"A high ratio, on the other hand, is considered risky as it may put the firm into difficulty in meeting its obligations to outsiders.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":205,"section":"5.7 Solvency Ratios"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["debt-equity"],"order":730},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fact-how-can-greater-use-of-debt-help-the-owners","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"fact","front":"How can greater use of debt help the owners (trading on equity)?","back":"from the perspective of the owners, greater use of debt (trading on equity) may help in ensuring higher returns for them if the rate of earnings on capital employed is higher than the rate of interest payable.","quote":"from the perspective of the owners, greater use of debt (trading on equity) may help in ensuring higher returns for them if the rate of earnings on capital employed is higher than the rate of interest payable.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":205,"section":"5.7 Solvency Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["debt-equity","trading-on-equity"],"order":740},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.def-the-debt-to-capital-employed-ratio","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"definition","front":"What is the debt to capital employed ratio?","back":"The Debt to capital employed ratio refers to the ratio of long-term debt to the total of external and internal funds (capital employed or net assets).","quote":"The Debt to capital employed ratio refers to the ratio of long-term debt to the total of external and internal funds (capital employed or net assets).","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":208,"section":"5.7 Solvency Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["debt-capital-employed"],"order":750},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fml-debt-to-capital-employed-ratio","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"formula","front":"Formula: Debt to capital employed ratio","back":"Debt to Capital Employed Ratio = Long-term Debt/Capital Employed (or Net Assets)","quote":"Debt to Capital Employed Ratio = Long-term Debt/Capital Employed (or Net Assets)","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":208,"section":"5.7 Solvency Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["debt-capital-employed"],"order":760},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fml-capital-employed-and-net-assets","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"formula","front":"Formula: Capital employed (and net assets)","back":"Capital employed is equal to the long-term debt + shareholders' funds. Alternatively, it may be taken as net assets which are equal to the total assets – current liabilities","quote":"Capital employed is equal to the long-term debt + shareholders' funds. Alternatively, it may be taken as net assets which are equal to the total assets – current liabilities","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":208,"section":"5.7 Solvency Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["capital-employed"],"order":770},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fact-what-does-a-low-or-high-debt-to-capital-empl","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"fact","front":"What does a low or high debt to capital employed ratio signify?","back":"Low ratio provides security to lenders and high ratio helps management in trading on equity.","quote":"Low ratio provides security to lenders and high ratio helps management in trading on equity.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":208,"section":"5.7 Solvency Ratios"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["debt-capital-employed"],"order":780},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fml-debt-to-capital-employed-ratio-in-relation-t","v":2,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"formula","front":"Formula: Debt to capital employed ratio in relation to total assets","back":"It may be noted that Debt to Capital Employed Ratio can also be computed in relation to total assets. In that case, it usually refers to the ratio of total debts (long-term debts + current liabilities) to total assets","quote":"It may be noted that Debt to Capital Employed Ratio can also be computed in relation to total assets. In that case, it usually refers to the ratio of total debts (long-term debts + current liabilities) to total assets","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":208,"section":"5.7 Solvency Ratios"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["debt-capital-employed"],"order":790},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.def-what-does-the-proprietary-ratio-express","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"definition","front":"What does the proprietary ratio express?","back":"Proprietary ratio expresses relationship of proprietor's (shareholders) funds to net assets","quote":"Proprietary ratio expresses relationship of proprietor's (shareholders) funds to net assets","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":209,"section":"5.7 Solvency Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["proprietary"],"order":800},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fml-proprietary-ratio","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"formula","front":"Formula: Proprietary ratio","back":"Proprietary Ratio = Shareholders' Funds/Capital employed (or net assets)","quote":"Proprietary Ratio = Shareholders' Funds/Capital employed (or net assets)","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":209,"section":"5.7 Solvency Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["proprietary"],"order":810},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fact-why-is-a-higher-proprietary-ratio-a-positive","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"fact","front":"Why is a higher proprietary ratio a positive feature?","back":"Higher proportion of shareholders funds in financing the assets is a positive feature as it provides security to creditors.","quote":"Higher proportion of shareholders funds in financing the assets is a positive feature as it provides security to creditors.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":209,"section":"5.7 Solvency Ratios"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["proprietary"],"order":820},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.key-the-total-of-debt-to-capital-employed-ratio","v":2,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"key_line","front":"The total of debt to capital employed ratio and proprietory ratio is equal to ____.","back":"The total of debt to capital employed ratio and proprietory ratio is equal to 1.","quote":"The total of debt to capital employed ratio and proprietory ratio is equal to 1.","blanks":["1"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":209,"section":"5.7 Solvency Ratios"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["proprietary","debt-capital-employed"],"order":830},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.def-what-does-the-total-assets-to-debt-ratio-mea","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"definition","front":"What does the total assets to debt ratio measure?","back":"This ratio measures the extent of the coverage of long-term debts by assets.","quote":"This ratio measures the extent of the coverage of long-term debts by assets.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":209,"section":"5.7 Solvency Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["total-assets-debt"],"order":840},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fml-total-assets-to-debt-ratio","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"formula","front":"Formula: Total assets to debt ratio","back":"Total assets to Debt Ratio = Total assets/Long-term debts","quote":"Total assets to Debt Ratio = Total assets/Long-term debts","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":209,"section":"5.7 Solvency Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["total-assets-debt"],"order":850},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fact-what-does-a-higher-total-assets-to-debt-rati","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"fact","front":"What does a higher total assets to debt ratio indicate?","back":"The higher ratio indicates that assets have been mainly financed by owners funds and the long-term loans is adequately covered by assets.","quote":"The higher ratio indicates that assets have been mainly financed by owners funds and the long-term loans is adequately covered by assets.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":209,"section":"5.7 Solvency Ratios"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["total-assets-debt"],"order":860},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fact-the-total-assets-to-debt-ratio-when-net-asse","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"fact","front":"What is the total assets to debt ratio when net assets are taken instead of total assets?","back":"It is better to take the net assets (capital employed) instead of total assets for computing this ratio also. It is observed that in that case, the ratio is the reciprocal of the debt to capital employed ratio.","quote":"It is better to take the net assets (capital employed) instead of total assets for computing this ratio also. It is observed that in that case, the ratio is the reciprocal of the debt to capital employed ratio.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":209,"section":"5.7 Solvency Ratios"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["total-assets-debt"],"order":870},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.ex-debt-equity-ratio-of-x-ltd-is-0-5-1-effect-o","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"example","front":"Debt-equity ratio of X Ltd. is 0.5 : 1. Effect of a further issue of equity shares?","back":"Assume that Rs. 1,00,000 worth of equity shares are issued. This will increase the internal funds to Rs. 11,00,000. The new ratio will be 0.45 : 1 (5,00,000/11,00,000). Thus, it is clear that further issue of equity shares decreases the debt-equity ratio.","quote":"Assume that Rs. 1,00,000 worth of equity shares are issued. This will increase the internal funds to Rs. 11,00,000. The new ratio will be 0.45 : 1 (5,00,000/11,00,000). Thus, it is clear that further issue of equity shares decreases the debt-equity ratio.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":211,"section":"5.7 Solvency Ratios"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["debt-equity","effect-of-transactions"],"order":880},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.ex-debt-equity-ratio-is-0-5-1-effect-of-cash-re","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"example","front":"Debt-equity ratio is 0.5 : 1. Effect of cash received from debtors?","back":"Cash received from debtors will leave the internal and external funds unchanged as this will only affect the composition of current assets. Hence, the debt-equity ratio will remain unchanged.","quote":"Cash received from debtors will leave the internal and external funds unchanged as this will only affect the composition of current assets. Hence, the debt-equity ratio will remain unchanged.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":211,"section":"5.7 Solvency Ratios"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["debt-equity","effect-of-transactions"],"order":890},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.ex-debt-equity-ratio-is-0-5-1-effect-of-sale-of","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"example","front":"Debt-equity ratio is 0.5 : 1. Effect of sale of goods on cash basis?","back":"This will also leave the ratio unchanged as sale of goods on cash basis neither affect Debt nor equity.","quote":"This will also leave the ratio unchanged as sale of goods on cash basis neither affect Debt nor equity.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":212,"section":"5.7 Solvency Ratios"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["debt-equity","effect-of-transactions"],"order":900},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.ex-debt-equity-ratio-is-0-5-1-effect-of-redeemi","v":2,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"example","front":"Debt-equity ratio is 0.5 : 1. Effect of redeeming debentures of Rs. 1,00,000?","back":"Decreases the debt-equity ratio.\nAssume that Rs. 1,00,000 debentures are redeemed. This will decrease the long-term debt to Rs. 4,00,000. The new ratio will be 0.4 : 1 (4,00,000/10,00,000). Redemption of debentures will decrease the debit-equity ratio.","quote":"Assume that Rs. 1,00,000 debentures are redeemed. This will decrease the long-term debt to Rs. 4,00,000. The new ratio will be 0.4 : 1 (4,00,000/10,00,000). Redemption of debentures will decrease the debit-equity ratio.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":212,"section":"5.7 Solvency Ratios"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["debt-equity","effect-of-transactions"],"order":910},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.def-what-does-the-interest-coverage-ratio-deal-w","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"definition","front":"What does the interest coverage ratio deal with?","back":"It is a ratio which deals with the servicing of interest on loan.","quote":"It is a ratio which deals with the servicing of interest on loan.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":212,"section":"5.7 Solvency Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["interest-coverage"],"order":920},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.def-what-does-the-interest-coverage-ratio-expres","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"definition","front":"What does the interest coverage ratio express?","back":"It expresses the relationship between profits available for payment of interest and the amount of interest payable.","quote":"It expresses the relationship between profits available for payment of interest and the amount of interest payable.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":212,"section":"5.7 Solvency Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["interest-coverage"],"order":930},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fml-interest-coverage-ratio","v":2,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"formula","front":"Formula: Interest coverage ratio","back":"Interest Coverage Ratio = Net Profit before Interest and Tax / Interest on long-term debts","quote":"Interest Coverage Ratio = Net Profit before Interest and Tax / Interest on long-term debts","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":212,"section":"5.7 Solvency Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["interest-coverage"],"order":940,"note":"Interest Coverage Ratio = Net Profit before Interest and Tax / Interest on long-term debts (a fraction in the book)."},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fact-what-does-a-higher-interest-coverage-ratio-e","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"fact","front":"What does a higher interest coverage ratio ensure?","back":"It reveals the number of times interest on long-term debts is covered by the profits available for interest. A higher ratio ensures safety of interest on debts.","quote":"It reveals the number of times interest on long-term debts is covered by the profits available for interest. A higher ratio ensures safety of interest on debts.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":212,"section":"5.7 Solvency Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["interest-coverage"],"order":950},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fml-net-profit-before-tax-from-net-profit-after","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"formula","front":"Formula: Net profit before tax from net profit after tax (Illustration 11)","back":"Net profit after tax × 100/(100 – Tax rate)","quote":"Net profit after tax × 100/(100 – Tax rate)","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":212,"section":"5.7 Solvency Ratios"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["interest-coverage"],"order":960},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fml-net-profit-before-interest-and-tax-illustrat","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"formula","front":"Formula: Net profit before interest and tax (Illustration 11)","back":"Net profit before interest and tax = Net profit before tax + Interest","quote":"Net profit before interest and tax = Net profit before tax + Interest","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":212,"section":"5.7 Solvency Ratios"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["interest-coverage"],"order":970},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.def-what-do-activity-ratios-indicate","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"definition","front":"What do activity ratios indicate?","back":"These ratios indicate the speed at which, activities of the business are being performed.","quote":"These ratios indicate the speed at which, activities of the business are being performed.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":213,"section":"5.8 Activity (or Turnover) Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["activity"],"order":980},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fact-what-do-activity-ratios-express","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"fact","front":"What do activity ratios express?","back":"The activity ratios express the number of times assets employed, or, for that matter, any constituent of assets, is turned into sales during an accounting period.","quote":"The activity ratios express the number of times assets employed, or, for that matter, any constituent of assets, is turned into sales during an accounting period.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":213,"section":"5.8 Activity (or Turnover) Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["activity"],"order":990},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fact-what-does-a-higher-turnover-ratio-mean","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"fact","front":"What does a higher turnover ratio mean?","back":"Higher turnover ratio means better utilisation of assets and signifies improved efficiency and profitability, and as such are known as efficiency ratios.","quote":"Higher turnover ratio means better utilisation of assets and signifies improved efficiency and profitability, and as such are known as efficiency ratios.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":213,"section":"5.8 Activity (or Turnover) Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["activity"],"order":1000},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.types-name-the-important-activity-ratios","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"types","front":"Name the important activity ratios.","back":"1. Inventory Turnover;\n2. Trade receivable Turnover;\n3. Trade payable Turnover;\n4. Investment (Net assets) Turnover\n5. Fixed assets Turnover; and\n6. Working capital Turnover","quote":"1. Inventory Turnover;\n2. Trade receivable Turnover;\n3. Trade payable Turnover;\n4. Investment (Net assets) Turnover\n5. Fixed assets Turnover; and\n6. Working capital Turnover","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":213,"section":"5.8 Activity (or Turnover) Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["activity"],"order":1010},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.def-what-does-the-inventory-turnover-ratio-deter","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"definition","front":"What does the inventory turnover ratio determine?","back":"It determines the number of times inventory is converted into revenue from operations during the accounting period under consideration.","quote":"It determines the number of times inventory is converted into revenue from operations during the accounting period under consideration.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":213,"section":"5.8 Activity (or Turnover) Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["inventory-turnover"],"order":1020},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.def-inventory-turnover-ratio-expresses-the-relat","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"definition","front":"Inventory turnover ratio expresses the relationship between which two items?","back":"It expresses the relationship between the cost of revenue from operations and average inventory.","quote":"It expresses the relationship between the cost of revenue from operations and average inventory.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":213,"section":"5.8 Activity (or Turnover) Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["inventory-turnover"],"order":1030},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fml-inventory-turnover-ratio","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"formula","front":"Formula: Inventory turnover ratio","back":"Inventory Turnover Ratio = Cost of Revenue from Operations / Average Inventory","quote":"Inventory Turnover Ratio = Cost of Revenue from Operations / Average Inventory","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":213,"section":"5.8 Activity (or Turnover) Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["inventory-turnover"],"order":1040},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.def-average-inventory-and-cost-of-revenue-from-o","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"definition","front":"What are average inventory and cost of revenue from operations (NCERT)?","back":"Where average inventory refers to arithmetic average of opening and closing inventory, and the cost of revenue from operations means revenue from operations less gross profit.","quote":"Where average inventory refers to arithmetic average of opening and closing inventory, and the cost of revenue from operations means revenue from operations less gross profit.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":213,"section":"5.8 Activity (or Turnover) Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["inventory-turnover"],"order":1050},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fact-what-may-a-low-inventory-turnover-indicate","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"fact","front":"What may a low inventory turnover indicate?","back":"Low turnover of inventory may be due to bad buying, obsolete inventory, etc., and is a danger signal.","quote":"Low turnover of inventory may be due to bad buying, obsolete inventory, etc., and is a danger signal.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":213,"section":"5.8 Activity (or Turnover) Ratios"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["inventory-turnover"],"order":1060},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fact-why-must-a-high-inventory-turnover-be-carefu","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"fact","front":"Why must a high inventory turnover be carefully interpreted?","back":"High turnover is good but it must be carefully interpreted as it may be due to buying in small lots or selling quickly at low margin to realise cash.","quote":"High turnover is good but it must be carefully interpreted as it may be due to buying in small lots or selling quickly at low margin to realise cash.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":213,"section":"5.8 Activity (or Turnover) Ratios"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["inventory-turnover"],"order":1070},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fact-what-does-the-inventory-turnover-ratio-study","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"fact","front":"What does the inventory turnover ratio study, and what else does it measure?","back":"It studies the frequency of conversion of inventory of finished goods into revenue from operations. It is also a measure of liquidity.","quote":"It studies the frequency of conversion of inventory of finished goods into revenue from operations. It is also a measure of liquidity.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":213,"section":"5.8 Activity (or Turnover) Ratios"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["inventory-turnover"],"order":1080},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.def-what-does-the-trade-receivables-turnover-rat","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"definition","front":"What does the trade receivables turnover ratio express?","back":"It expresses the relationship between credit revenue from operations and trade receivable.","quote":"It expresses the relationship between credit revenue from operations and trade receivable.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":217,"section":"5.8 Activity (or Turnover) Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["trade-receivables-turnover"],"order":1100},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fml-trade-receivables-turnover-ratio","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"formula","front":"Formula: Trade receivables turnover ratio","back":"Trade Receivable Turnover ratio\n= Net Credit Revenue from Operations/Average Trade Receivable","quote":"Trade Receivable Turnover ratio\n= Net Credit Revenue from Operations/Average Trade Receivable","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":217,"section":"5.8 Activity (or Turnover) Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["trade-receivables-turnover"],"order":1110},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fml-average-trade-receivable","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"formula","front":"Formula: Average trade receivable","back":"Average Trade Receivable\n= (Opening Debtors and Bills Receivable + Closing Debtors and Bills Receivable)/2","quote":"Average Trade Receivable\n= (Opening Debtors and Bills Receivable + Closing Debtors and Bills Receivable)/2","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":217,"section":"5.8 Activity (or Turnover) Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["trade-receivables-turnover"],"order":1120},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fact-are-debtors-taken-before-or-after-the-provis","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"fact","front":"Are debtors taken before or after the provision for doubtful debts in this ratio?","back":"It needs to be noted that debtors should be taken before making any provision for doubtful debts.","quote":"It needs to be noted that debtors should be taken before making any provision for doubtful debts.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":217,"section":"5.8 Activity (or Turnover) Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["trade-receivables-turnover"],"order":1130},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fact-on-what-does-the-liquidity-position-of-the-f","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"fact","front":"On what does the liquidity position of the firm depend (trade receivables)?","back":"The liquidity position of the firm depends upon the speed with which trade receivables are realised.","quote":"The liquidity position of the firm depends upon the speed with which trade receivables are realised.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":217,"section":"5.8 Activity (or Turnover) Ratios"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["trade-receivables-turnover"],"order":1140},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fact-what-does-a-higher-trade-receivables-turnove","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"fact","front":"What does a higher trade receivables turnover mean?","back":"Higher turnover means speedy collection from trade receivable.","quote":"Higher turnover means speedy collection from trade receivable.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":217,"section":"5.8 Activity (or Turnover) Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["trade-receivables-turnover"],"order":1150},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.def-how-is-the-average-collection-period-worked","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"definition","front":"How is the average collection period worked out?","back":"The ratio is calculated by dividing the days or months in a year by trade receivables turnover ratio.","quote":"The ratio is calculated by dividing the days or months in a year by trade receivables turnover ratio.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":217,"section":"5.8 Activity (or Turnover) Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["trade-receivables-turnover","average-collection-period"],"order":1160},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.def-what-does-the-trade-payables-turnover-ratio","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"definition","front":"What does the trade payables turnover ratio indicate?","back":"Trade payables turnover ratio indicates the pattern of payment of trade payable.","quote":"Trade payables turnover ratio indicates the pattern of payment of trade payable.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":218,"section":"5.8 Activity (or Turnover) Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["trade-payables-turnover"],"order":1170},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fact-trade-payables-turnover-ratio-expresses-the","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"fact","front":"Trade payables turnover ratio expresses the relationship between which two items?","back":"As trade payable arise on account of credit purchases, it expresses relationship between credit purchases and trade payable.","quote":"As trade payable arise on account of credit purchases, it expresses relationship between credit purchases and trade payable.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":218,"section":"5.8 Activity (or Turnover) Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["trade-payables-turnover"],"order":1180},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fml-trade-payables-turnover-ratio-numerator-and","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"formula","front":"Formula: Trade payables turnover ratio (numerator and denominator)","back":"Net Credit purchases/ Average trade payable","quote":"Net Credit purchases/ Average trade payable","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":218,"section":"5.8 Activity (or Turnover) Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["trade-payables-turnover"],"order":1190},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fml-average-trade-payable","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"formula","front":"Formula: Average trade payable","back":"(Opening Creditors and Bills Payable + Closing Creditors and Bills Payable)/2","quote":"(Opening Creditors and Bills Payable + Closing Creditors and Bills Payable)/2","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":218,"section":"5.8 Activity (or Turnover) Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["trade-payables-turnover"],"order":1200},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fml-average-payment-period","v":2,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"formula","front":"Formula: Average payment period","back":"Average Payment Period = No. of days/month in a year / Trade Payables Turnover Ratio","quote":"Average Payment Period = No. of days/month in a year / Trade Payables Turnover Ratio","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":218,"section":"5.8 Activity (or Turnover) Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["trade-payables-turnover","average-payment-period"],"order":1210,"note":"Average Payment Period = No. of days/months in a year / Trade Payables Turnover Ratio."},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fact-what-does-the-trade-payables-turnover-ratio","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"fact","front":"What does the trade payables turnover ratio reveal?","back":"It reveals average payment period.","quote":"It reveals average payment period.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":218,"section":"5.8 Activity (or Turnover) Ratios"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["trade-payables-turnover"],"order":1220},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fact-what-does-a-lower-trade-payables-turnover-ra","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"fact","front":"What does a lower trade payables turnover ratio mean?","back":"Lower ratio means credit allowed by the supplier is for a long period or it may reflect delayed payment to suppliers which is not a very good policy as it may affect the reputation of the business.","quote":"Lower ratio means credit allowed by the supplier is for a long period or it may reflect delayed payment to suppliers which is not a very good policy as it may affect the reputation of the business.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":218,"section":"5.8 Activity (or Turnover) Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["trade-payables-turnover"],"order":1230},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.entry-treatment-trade-receivables-turnover-when-op","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"entry","front":"Treatment: trade receivables turnover when opening debtors and bills receivable are not available (Illustration 17)","back":"So when only year-end figures are available use the same as it is.","quote":"So when only year-end figures are available use the same as it is.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":219,"section":"5.8 Activity (or Turnover) Ratios"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["trade-receivables-turnover","treatment"],"order":1240},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.entry-treatment-trade-payables-turnover-when-credi","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"entry","front":"Treatment: trade payables turnover when credit purchases are not given (Illustration 17)","back":"Since no information regarding credit purchase is given, hence it will be related as net purchases.","quote":"Since no information regarding credit purchase is given, hence it will be related as net purchases.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":220,"section":"5.8 Activity (or Turnover) Ratios"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["trade-payables-turnover","treatment"],"order":1250},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.def-what-does-the-net-assets-capital-employed-tu","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"definition","front":"What does the net assets (capital employed) turnover ratio reflect?","back":"It reflects relationship between revenue from operations and net assets (capital employed) in the business.","quote":"It reflects relationship between revenue from operations and net assets (capital employed) in the business.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":220,"section":"5.8 Activity (or Turnover) Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["net-assets-turnover"],"order":1260},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fml-net-assets-or-capital-employed-turnover-rati","v":2,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"formula","front":"Formula: Net assets or capital employed turnover ratio","back":"Net Assets or Capital Employed Turnover ratio = Revenue from Operation / Capital Employed","quote":"Net Assets or Capital Employed Turnover ratio = Revenue from Operation / Capital Employed","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":220,"section":"5.8 Activity (or Turnover) Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["net-assets-turnover"],"order":1270,"note":"Net Assets Turnover Ratio = Revenue from Operations / Capital Employed (a fraction in the book)."},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.types-capital-employed-turnover-is-analysed-furthe","v":2,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"types","front":"Capital employed turnover is analysed further by which two turnover ratios?","back":"(a) Fixed Assets Turnover Ratio\n(b) Working Capital Turnover Ratio","quote":"Capital employed turnover ratio which studies turnover of capital employed (or Net Assets) is analysed further by following two turnover ratios : (a) Fixed Assets Turnover Ratio : It is computed as follows: Fixed asset turnover Ratio = Net Revenue from Operation Net Fixed Assets (b) Working Capital Turnover Ratio","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":220,"section":"5.8 Activity (or Turnover) Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["net-assets-turnover"],"order":1280},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fml-fixed-assets-turnover-ratio","v":2,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"formula","front":"Formula: Fixed assets turnover ratio","back":"Fixed asset turnover Ratio = Net Revenue from Operation / Net Fixed Assets","quote":"Fixed asset turnover Ratio = Net Revenue from Operation / Net Fixed Assets","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":220,"section":"5.8 Activity (or Turnover) Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["fixed-assets-turnover"],"order":1290,"note":"Fixed Assets Turnover Ratio = Net Revenue from Operations / Net Fixed Assets (a fraction in the book)."},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fml-working-capital-turnover-ratio","v":2,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"formula","front":"Formula: Working capital turnover ratio","back":"Working Capital Turnover Ratio = Net Revenue from Operation / Working Capital","quote":"Working Capital Turnover Ratio = Net Revenue from Operation / Working Capital","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":221,"section":"5.8 Activity (or Turnover) Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["working-capital-turnover"],"order":1300,"note":"Working Capital Turnover Ratio = Net Revenue from Operations / Working Capital (a fraction in the book)."},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fact-what-does-a-high-turnover-of-capital-employe","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"fact","front":"What does a high turnover of capital employed, working capital and fixed assets imply?","back":"High turnover of capital employed, working capital and fixed assets is a good sign and implies efficient utilisation of resources.","quote":"High turnover of capital employed, working capital and fixed assets is a good sign and implies efficient utilisation of resources.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":221,"section":"5.8 Activity (or Turnover) Ratios"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["net-assets-turnover"],"order":1310},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.entry-treatment-inventory-turnover-when-opening-an","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"entry","front":"Treatment: inventory turnover when opening and closing inventory are not given (Illustration 25)","back":"In absence of information regarding `Inventory in the beginning' and `Inventory at the end', the `Inventory' is treated as Average Inventory.","quote":"In absence of information regarding `Inventory in the beginning' and `Inventory at the end', the `Inventory' is treated as Average Inventory.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":231,"section":"5.8 Activity (or Turnover) Ratios"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["inventory-turnover","treatment"],"order":1330},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.def-profitability-ratios-calculated-to-analyse","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"definition","front":"What are profitability ratios calculated to analyse?","back":"Profitability ratios are calculated to analyse the earning capacity of the business which is the outcome of utilisation of resources employed in the business.","quote":"Profitability ratios are calculated to analyse the earning capacity of the business which is the outcome of utilisation of resources employed in the business.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":223,"section":"5.9 Profitability Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["profitability"],"order":1340},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.list-name-the-ten-commonly-used-profitability-rat","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"list","front":"Name the ten commonly used profitability ratios.","back":"1. Gross profit ratio\n2. Operating ratio\n3. Operating profit ratio\n4. Net profit ratio\n5. Return on Investment (ROI) or Return on Capital Employed (ROCE)\n6. Return on Net Worth (RONW)\n7. Earnings per share\n8. Book value per share\n9. Dividend payout ratio\n10. Price earning ratio.","quote":"1. Gross profit ratio\n2. Operating ratio\n3. Operating profit ratio\n4. Net profit ratio\n5. Return on Investment (ROI) or Return on Capital Employed (ROCE)\n6. Return on Net Worth (RONW)\n7. Earnings per share\n8. Book value per share\n9. Dividend payout ratio\n10. Price earning ratio.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":223,"section":"5.9 Profitability Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["profitability"],"order":1350},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.def-why-is-the-gross-profit-ratio-computed","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"definition","front":"Why is the gross profit ratio computed?","back":"Gross profit ratio as a percentage of revenue from operations is computed to have an idea about gross margin.","quote":"Gross profit ratio as a percentage of revenue from operations is computed to have an idea about gross margin.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":223,"section":"5.9 Profitability Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["gross-profit-ratio"],"order":1360},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fml-gross-profit-ratio","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"formula","front":"Formula: Gross profit ratio","back":"Gross Profit Ratio = Gross Profit/Net Revenue of Operations × 100","quote":"Gross Profit Ratio = Gross Profit/Net Revenue of Operations × 100","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":223,"section":"5.9 Profitability Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["gross-profit-ratio"],"order":1370},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fact-what-does-the-gross-profit-ratio-indicate","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"fact","front":"What does the gross profit ratio indicate?","back":"It indicates gross margin on products sold. It also indicates the margin available to cover operating expenses, non-operating expenses, etc.","quote":"It indicates gross margin on products sold. It also indicates the margin available to cover operating expenses, non-operating expenses, etc.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":223,"section":"5.9 Profitability Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["gross-profit-ratio"],"order":1380},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fact-a-change-in-gross-profit-ratio-may-be-due-to","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"fact","front":"A change in gross profit ratio may be due to what?","back":"Change in gross profit ratio may be due to change in selling price or cost of revenue from operations or a combination of both.","quote":"Change in gross profit ratio may be due to change in selling price or cost of revenue from operations or a combination of both.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":223,"section":"5.9 Profitability Ratios"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["gross-profit-ratio"],"order":1390},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fact-what-may-a-low-gross-profit-ratio-indicate","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"fact","front":"What may a low gross profit ratio indicate?","back":"A low ratio may indicate unfavourable purchase and sales policy.","quote":"A low ratio may indicate unfavourable purchase and sales policy.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":223,"section":"5.9 Profitability Ratios"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["gross-profit-ratio"],"order":1400},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fml-net-purchases-illustration-19","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"formula","front":"Formula: Net purchases (Illustration 19)","back":"Cash Purchases + Credit Purchases – Return Outwards","quote":"Cash Purchases + Credit Purchases – Return Outwards","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":224,"section":"5.9 Profitability Ratios"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["gross-profit-ratio"],"order":1410},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.def-why-is-the-operating-ratio-computed","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"definition","front":"Why is the operating ratio computed?","back":"It is computed to analyse cost of operation in relation to revenue from operations.","quote":"It is computed to analyse cost of operation in relation to revenue from operations.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":224,"section":"5.9 Profitability Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["operating-ratio"],"order":1430},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fml-operating-ratio","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"formula","front":"Formula: Operating ratio","back":"Operating Ratio =\n(Cost of Revenue from Operations + Operating Expenses)/\nNet Revenue from Operations ×100","quote":"Operating Ratio =\n(Cost of Revenue from Operations + Operating Expenses)/\nNet Revenue from Operations ×100","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":224,"section":"5.9 Profitability Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["operating-ratio"],"order":1440},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.types-operating-expenses-include-which-items","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"types","front":"Operating expenses include which items?","back":"Operating expenses include office expenses, administrative expenses, selling expenses, distribution expenses, depreciation and employee benefit expenses etc.","quote":"Operating expenses include office expenses, administrative expenses, selling expenses, distribution expenses, depreciation and employee benefit expenses etc.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":224,"section":"5.9 Profitability Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["operating-ratio"],"order":1450},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fact-how-is-cost-of-operation-determined","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"fact","front":"How is cost of operation determined?","back":"Cost of operation is determined by excluding non-operating incomes and expenses such as loss on sale of assets, interest paid, dividend received, loss by fire, speculation gain and so on.","quote":"Cost of operation is determined by excluding non-operating incomes and expenses such as loss on sale of assets, interest paid, dividend received, loss by fire, speculation gain and so on.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":224,"section":"5.9 Profitability Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["operating-ratio"],"order":1460},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.def-how-may-the-operating-profit-ratio-be-comput","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"definition","front":"How may the operating profit ratio be computed?","back":"It is calculated to reveal operating margin. It may be computed directly or as a residual of operating ratio.","quote":"It is calculated to reveal operating margin. It may be computed directly or as a residual of operating ratio.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":224,"section":"5.9 Profitability Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["operating-profit-ratio"],"order":1470},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fml-operating-profit-ratio-as-a-residual","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"formula","front":"Formula: Operating profit ratio as a residual","back":"Operating Profit Ratio\n= 100 – Operating Ratio","quote":"Operating Profit Ratio\n= 100 – Operating Ratio","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":224,"section":"5.9 Profitability Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["operating-profit-ratio"],"order":1480},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fml-operating-profit-ratio-direct-method","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"formula","front":"Formula: Operating profit ratio, direct method","back":"Operating Profit Ratio\n= Operating Profit/ Revenue from Operations × 100","quote":"Operating Profit Ratio\n= Operating Profit/ Revenue from Operations × 100","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":225,"section":"5.9 Profitability Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["operating-profit-ratio"],"order":1490},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fml-operating-profit","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"formula","front":"Formula: Operating profit","back":"Operating Profit\n= Revenue from Operations – Operating Cost","quote":"Operating Profit\n= Revenue from Operations – Operating Cost","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":225,"section":"5.9 Profitability Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["operating-profit-ratio"],"order":1500},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fact-what-does-the-operating-ratio-express","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"fact","front":"What does the operating ratio express?","back":"Operating ratio is computed to express cost of operations excluding financial charges in relation to revenue from operations.","quote":"Operating ratio is computed to express cost of operations excluding financial charges in relation to revenue from operations.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":225,"section":"5.9 Profitability Ratios"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["operating-ratio"],"order":1510},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.key-lower-operating-ratio-is-a-very-healthy-sign","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"key_line","front":"____ operating ratio is a very healthy sign.","back":"Lower operating ratio is a very healthy sign.","quote":"Lower operating ratio is a very healthy sign.","blanks":["Lower"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":225,"section":"5.9 Profitability Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["operating-ratio"],"order":1520},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.def-what-concept-of-profit-does-the-net-profit-r","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"definition","front":"What concept of profit does the net profit ratio use?","back":"Net profit ratio is based on all inclusive concept of profit.","quote":"Net profit ratio is based on all inclusive concept of profit.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":226,"section":"5.9 Profitability Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["net-profit-ratio"],"order":1540},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.def-what-does-the-net-profit-ratio-relate","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"definition","front":"What does the net profit ratio relate?","back":"It relates revenue from operations to net profit after operational as well as non-operational expenses and incomes.","quote":"It relates revenue from operations to net profit after operational as well as non-operational expenses and incomes.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":226,"section":"5.9 Profitability Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["net-profit-ratio"],"order":1550},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fml-net-profit-ratio","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"formula","front":"Formula: Net profit ratio","back":"Net Profit Ratio = Net profit/Revenue from Operations × 100","quote":"Net Profit Ratio = Net profit/Revenue from Operations × 100","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":226,"section":"5.9 Profitability Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["net-profit-ratio"],"order":1560},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fact-what-does-net-profit-generally-refer-to-in-t","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"fact","front":"What does net profit generally refer to in the net profit ratio?","back":"Generally, net profit refers to profit after tax (PAT).","quote":"Generally, net profit refers to profit after tax (PAT).","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":226,"section":"5.9 Profitability Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["net-profit-ratio"],"order":1570},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fact-which-ratio-uses-net-profit-ratio-as-its-mai","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"fact","front":"Which ratio uses net profit ratio as its main variable?","back":"Besides revealing profitability, it is the main variable in computation of Return on Investment.","quote":"Besides revealing profitability, it is the main variable in computation of Return on Investment.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":226,"section":"5.9 Profitability Ratios"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["net-profit-ratio"],"order":1580},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fact-what-does-the-net-profit-ratio-reflect-for-i","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"fact","front":"What does the net profit ratio reflect for investors?","back":"It reflects the overall efficiency of the business, assumes great significance from the point of view of investors.","quote":"It reflects the overall efficiency of the business, assumes great significance from the point of view of investors.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":226,"section":"5.9 Profitability Ratios"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["net-profit-ratio"],"order":1590},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.def-what-does-return-on-capital-employed-investm","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"definition","front":"What does Return on Capital Employed (Investment) explain?","back":"It explains the overall utilisation of funds by a business enterprise.","quote":"It explains the overall utilisation of funds by a business enterprise.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":226,"section":"5.9 Profitability Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["roi"],"order":1600},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.def-meant-by-capital-employed","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"definition","front":"What is meant by capital employed?","back":"Capital employed means the long-term funds employed in the business and includes shareholders' funds, debentures and long-term loans.","quote":"Capital employed means the long-term funds employed in the business and includes shareholders' funds, debentures and long-term loans.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":226,"section":"5.9 Profitability Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["roi","capital-employed"],"order":1610},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fact-how-else-may-capital-employed-be-taken","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"fact","front":"How else may capital employed be taken?","back":"Alternatively, capital employed may be taken as the total of non-current assets and working capital.","quote":"Alternatively, capital employed may be taken as the total of non-current assets and working capital.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":226,"section":"5.9 Profitability Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["roi","capital-employed"],"order":1620},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.key-profit-refers-to-the-profit-before-interest","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"key_line","front":"Profit refers to the ____ (PBIT) for computation of this ratio.","back":"Profit refers to the Profit Before Interest and Tax (PBIT) for computation of this ratio.","quote":"Profit refers to the Profit Before Interest and Tax (PBIT) for computation of this ratio.","blanks":["Profit Before Interest and Tax"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":226,"section":"5.9 Profitability Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["roi"],"order":1630},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fml-return-on-investment-capital-employed","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"formula","front":"Formula: Return on investment (capital employed)","back":"Return on Investment (or Capital Employed) =\nProfit before Interest and Tax/ Capital Employed × 100","quote":"Return on Investment (or Capital Employed) =\nProfit before Interest and Tax/ Capital Employed × 100","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":226,"section":"5.9 Profitability Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["roi"],"order":1640},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fact-which-ratio-is-a-good-measure-of-profitabili","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"fact","front":"Which ratio is a good measure of profitability for inter-firm comparison?","back":"For inter-firm comparison, return on capital employed funds is considered a good measure of profitability.","quote":"For inter-firm comparison, return on capital employed funds is considered a good measure of profitability.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":226,"section":"5.9 Profitability Ratios"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["roi"],"order":1650},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.def-return-on-net-worth-ronw","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"definition","front":"What is Return on Net Worth (RONW)?","back":"A better measure of profitability from shareholders point of view is obtained by determining return on total shareholders' funds, it is also termed as Return on Net Worth (RONW)","quote":"A better measure of profitability from shareholders point of view is obtained by determining return on total shareholders' funds, it is also termed as Return on Net Worth (RONW)","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":227,"section":"5.9 Profitability Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["ronw"],"order":1660},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fml-return-on-shareholders-funds","v":2,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"formula","front":"Formula: Return on shareholders' funds","back":"Return on Shareholders’ Fund = Profit after Tax / Shareholders’ Funds × 100","quote":"Return on Shareholders’ Fund = Profit after Tax / Shareholders’ Funds × 100","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":227,"section":"5.9 Profitability Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["ronw"],"order":1670,"note":"Return on Shareholders' Funds = Profit after Tax / Shareholders' Funds x 100 (a fraction in the book)."},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fact-how-should-return-on-shareholders-funds-comp","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"fact","front":"How should return on shareholders' funds compare with return on investment?","back":"It should be higher than the return on investment otherwise it would imply that company's funds have not been employed profitably.","quote":"It should be higher than the return on investment otherwise it would imply that company's funds have not been employed profitably.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":227,"section":"5.9 Profitability Ratios"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["ronw"],"order":1680},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fml-earnings-per-share-eps","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"formula","front":"Formula: Earnings per share (EPS)","back":"EPS = Profit available for equity shareholders/Number of Equity Shares","quote":"EPS = Profit available for equity shareholders/Number of Equity Shares","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":227,"section":"5.9 Profitability Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["eps"],"order":1690},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fml-earnings-profit-available-for-equity-shareho","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"formula","front":"Formula: Earnings (profit available for equity shareholders)","back":"Profit after Tax – Dividend on Preference Shares","quote":"Profit after Tax – Dividend on Preference Shares","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":227,"section":"5.9 Profitability Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["eps"],"order":1700},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fact-why-is-eps-important","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"fact","front":"Why is EPS important?","back":"This ratio is very important from equity shareholders point of view and also for the share price in the stock market.","quote":"This ratio is very important from equity shareholders point of view and also for the share price in the stock market.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":227,"section":"5.9 Profitability Ratios"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["eps"],"order":1710},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fml-book-value-per-share","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"formula","front":"Formula: Book value per share","back":"Book Value per share = Equity shareholders' funds/Number of Equity Shares","quote":"Book Value per share = Equity shareholders' funds/Number of Equity Shares","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":227,"section":"5.9 Profitability Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["bvps"],"order":1720},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fml-equity-shareholders-fund","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"formula","front":"Formula: Equity shareholders' fund","back":"Equity shareholder fund refers to Shareholders' Funds – Preference Share Capital.","quote":"Equity shareholder fund refers to Shareholders' Funds – Preference Share Capital.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":227,"section":"5.9 Profitability Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["bvps"],"order":1730},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fact-why-is-book-value-per-share-important-to-equ","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"fact","front":"Why is book value per share important to equity shareholders?","back":"This ratio is again very important from equity shareholders point of view as it gives an idea about the value of their holding and affects market price of the shares.","quote":"This ratio is again very important from equity shareholders point of view as it gives an idea about the value of their holding and affects market price of the shares.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":227,"section":"5.9 Profitability Ratios"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["bvps"],"order":1740},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.def-what-does-the-dividend-payout-ratio-refer-to","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"definition","front":"What does the dividend payout ratio refer to?","back":"This refers to the proportion of earning that are distributed to the shareholders.","quote":"This refers to the proportion of earning that are distributed to the shareholders.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":227,"section":"5.9 Profitability Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["dividend-payout"],"order":1750},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fml-dividend-payout-ratio","v":2,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"formula","front":"Formula: Dividend payout ratio","back":"Dividend Payout Ratio = Dividend per share / Earnings per share","quote":"Dividend Payout Ratio = Dividend per share / Earnings per share","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":227,"section":"5.9 Profitability Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["dividend-payout"],"order":1760,"note":"Dividend Payout Ratio = Dividend per share / Earnings per share (a fraction in the book)."},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fact-what-does-the-dividend-payout-ratio-reflect","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"fact","front":"What does the dividend payout ratio reflect?","back":"This reflects company's dividend policy and growth in owner's equity.","quote":"This reflects company's dividend policy and growth in owner's equity.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":227,"section":"5.9 Profitability Ratios"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["dividend-payout"],"order":1770},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fml-price-earning-p-e-ratio","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"formula","front":"Formula: Price earning (P/E) ratio","back":"P/E Ratio = Market Price of a share/earnings per share","quote":"P/E Ratio = Market Price of a share/earnings per share","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":228,"section":"5.9 Profitability Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["pe-ratio"],"order":1780},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.ex-eps-of-x-ltd-is-rs-10-and-market-price-is-rs","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"example","front":"EPS of X Ltd. is Rs. 10 and market price is Rs. 100. What is the price earning ratio?","back":"if the EPS of X Ltd. is Rs. 10 and market price is Rs. 100, the price earning ratio will be 10 (100/10).","quote":"if the EPS of X Ltd. is Rs. 10 and market price is Rs. 100, the price earning ratio will be 10 (100/10).","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":228,"section":"5.9 Profitability Ratios"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["pe-ratio"],"order":1790},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fact-what-does-the-p-e-ratio-reflect","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"fact","front":"What does the P/E ratio reflect?","back":"It reflects investors expectation about the growth in the firm's earnings and reasonableness of the market price of its shares.","quote":"It reflects investors expectation about the growth in the firm's earnings and reasonableness of the market price of its shares.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":228,"section":"5.9 Profitability Ratios"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["pe-ratio"],"order":1800},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.accounting-ratios.fact-the-objective-of-ratio-analysis-ncert-summar","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.accounting-ratios","chapter":"Accounting Ratios","unit":"Analysis of Financial Statements","kind":"fact","front":"What is the objective of ratio analysis (NCERT summary)?","back":"The objective of ratio analysis is to provide a deeper analysis of the profitability, liquidity, solvency and activity levels in the business. It is also to identify the problem areas as well as the strong areas of the business.","quote":"The objective of ratio analysis is to provide a deeper analysis of the profitability, liquidity, solvency and activity levels in the business. It is also to identify the problem areas as well as the strong areas of the business.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy: Company Accounts and Analysis of Financial Statements","code":"leac205","chapterNo":5,"page":233,"section":"Summary"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["objectives"],"order":1840},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner.kl-deed-ends","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner","chapter":"Retirement & Death of Partner","unit":"Reconstitution of a Partnership firm","kind":"key_line","front":"On the retirement or death of a partner, the existing partnership deed ____, and in its place, a ____ needs to be framed","back":"On the retirement or death of a partner, the existing partnership deed comes to an end, and in its place, a new partnership deed needs to be framed","quote":"On the retirement or death of a partner, the existing partnership deed comes to an end, and in its place, a new partnership deed needs to be framed","blanks":["comes to an end","new partnership deed"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy – Partnership Accounts","code":"leac103","chapterNo":3,"page":107,"section":"Introduction"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["retirement","death","partnership deed"],"order":10},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner.list-dues-credits-1","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner","chapter":"Retirement & Death of Partner","unit":"Reconstitution of a Partnership firm","kind":"list","front":"Sum due to a retiring/deceased partner includes: name items (i) to (v).","back":"(i) credit balance of his capital account;\n(ii) credit balance of his current account (if any);\n(iii) his share of goodwill;\n(iv) his share of accumulated profits (reserves);\n(v) his share in the gain of revaluation of assets and liabilities;","quote":"The sum due to the retiring partner (in case of retirement) and to the legal representatives/ executors (in case of death) includes: (i) credit balance of his capital account; (ii) credit balance of his current account (if any); (iii) his share of goodwill; (iv) his share of accumulated profits (reserves); (v) his share in the gain of revaluation of assets and liabilities;","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy – Partnership Accounts","code":"leac103","chapterNo":3,"page":107,"section":"3.1 Ascertaining the Amount Due to Retiring/ Deceased Partner"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["amount due","retirement","death"],"order":30},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner.list-dues-credits-2","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner","chapter":"Retirement & Death of Partner","unit":"Reconstitution of a Partnership firm","kind":"list","front":"Sum due to a retiring/deceased partner: name items (vi) to (viii), after the capital, goodwill and reserve items.","back":"(vi) his share of profits up to the date of retirement/death;\n(vii) interest on his capital, if involved, up to the date of retirement/death; and\n(viii) salary/commission, if any, due to him up to the date of retirement/death.","quote":"(vi) his share of profits up to the date of retirement/death; (vii) interest on his capital, if involved, up to the date of retirement/death; and (viii) salary/commission, if any, due to him up to the date of retirement/death.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy – Partnership Accounts","code":"leac103","chapterNo":3,"page":108,"section":"3.1 Ascertaining the Amount Due to Retiring/ Deceased Partner"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["amount due","retirement","death"],"order":40},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner.def-retirement-meaning","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner","chapter":"Retirement & Death of Partner","unit":"Reconstitution of a Partnership firm","kind":"definition","front":"What is retirement of an existing partner?","back":"It means withdrawal by a partner from the business of the firm which may be due to his bad health, old age or change in business interests.","quote":"It means withdrawal by a partner from the business of the firm which may be due to his bad health, old age or change in business interests.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy : Partnership Accounts","code":"leac102","chapterNo":2,"page":49,"section":"2.1 Modes of Reconstitution of a Partnership Firm"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["retirement"],"order":50},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner.list-dues-deductions","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner","chapter":"Retirement & Death of Partner","unit":"Reconstitution of a Partnership firm","kind":"list","front":"Name the deductions that may have to be made from the retiring/deceased partner's share.","back":"(i) debit balance of his current account (if any);\n(ii) his share of goodwill to be written off, if necessary;\n(iii) his share of accumulated losses;\n(iv) his share of loss on revaluation of assets and liabilities;\n(v) his share of loss up to the date of retirement/death;\n(vi) his drawings up to the date of retirement/death;\n(vii) interest on drawings, if involved, up to the date of retirement/death.","quote":"The following deductions, if any, may have to be made from his share: (i) debit balance of his current account (if any); (ii) his share of goodwill to be written off, if necessary; (iii) his share of accumulated losses; (iv) his share of loss on revaluation of assets and liabilities; (v) his share of loss up to the date of retirement/death; (vi) his drawings up to the date of retirement/death; (vii) interest on drawings, if involved, up to the date of retirement/death.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy – Partnership Accounts","code":"leac103","chapterNo":3,"page":108,"section":"3.1 Ascertaining the Amount Due to Retiring/ Deceased Partner"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["amount due","deductions"],"order":50},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner.fact-retire-at-will","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner","chapter":"Retirement & Death of Partner","unit":"Reconstitution of a Partnership firm","kind":"fact","front":"Under what kind of partnership can a partner retire at any time?","back":"In fact a partner can retire any time if the partnership is at will.","quote":"In fact a partner can retire any time if the partnership is at will.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy : Partnership Accounts","code":"leac102","chapterNo":2,"page":49,"section":"2.1 Modes of Reconstitution of a Partnership Firm"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["retirement"],"order":60},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner.list-accounting-aspects","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner","chapter":"Retirement & Death of Partner","unit":"Reconstitution of a Partnership firm","kind":"list","front":"Name the 8 accounting aspects involved on retirement or death of a partner.","back":"1. Ascertainment of new profit sharing ratio and gaining ratio;\n2. Treatment of goodwill;\n3. Revaluation of assets and liabilities;\n4. Adjustment in respect of unrecorded assets and liabilities;\n5. Distribution of accumulated profits and losses;\n6. Ascertainment of share of profit or loss up to the date of retirement/death;\n7. Adjustment of capital, if required;\n8. Settlement of the amounts due to retired/deceased partner;","quote":"Thus, similar to admission, the various accounting aspects involved on retirement or death of a partner are as follows: 1. Ascertainment of new profit sharing ratio and gaining ratio; 2. Treatment of goodwill; 3. Revaluation of assets and liabilities; 4. Adjustment in respect of unrecorded assets and liabilities; 5. Distribution of accumulated profits and losses; 6. Ascertainment of share of profit or loss up to the date of retirement/death; 7. Adjustment of capital, if required; 8. Settlement of the amounts due to retired/deceased partner;","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy – Partnership Accounts","code":"leac103","chapterNo":3,"page":108,"section":"3.1 Ascertaining the Amount Due to Retiring/ Deceased Partner"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["accounting aspects","retirement","death"],"order":60},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner.def-new-psr","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner","chapter":"Retirement & Death of Partner","unit":"Reconstitution of a Partnership firm","kind":"definition","front":"What is the new profit sharing ratio (on retirement or death of a partner)?","back":"New profit sharing ratio is the ratio in which the remaining partners will share future profits after the retirement or death of any partner.","quote":"New profit sharing ratio is the ratio in which the remaining partners will share future profits after the retirement or death of any partner.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy – Partnership Accounts","code":"leac103","chapterNo":3,"page":108,"section":"3.2 New Profit Sharing Ratio"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["new profit sharing ratio"],"order":70},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner.fact-death-reconstitution","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner","chapter":"Retirement & Death of Partner","unit":"Reconstitution of a Partnership firm","kind":"fact","front":"On the death of a partner, when may the partnership stand reconstituted?","back":"Partnership may also stand reconstituted on death of a partner, if the remaining partners decide to continue the business of the firm as usual.","quote":"Partnership may also stand reconstituted on death of a partner, if the remaining partners decide to continue the business of the firm as usual.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy : Partnership Accounts","code":"leac102","chapterNo":2,"page":49,"section":"2.1 Modes of Reconstitution of a Partnership Firm"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["death"],"order":70},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner.kl-new-share-consists","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner","chapter":"Retirement & Death of Partner","unit":"Reconstitution of a Partnership firm","kind":"key_line","front":"The new share of each of the remaining partner will consist of his ____ in the firm plus the share ____ from the retiring /deceased partner.","back":"The new share of each of the remaining partner will consist of his own share in the firm plus the share acquired from the retiring /deceased partner.","quote":"The new share of each of the remaining partner will consist of his own share in the firm plus the share acquired from the retiring /deceased partner.","blanks":["own share","acquired"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy – Partnership Accounts","code":"leac103","chapterNo":3,"page":108,"section":"3.2 New Profit Sharing Ratio"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["new profit sharing ratio"],"order":80},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner.fact-assumed-old-ratio","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner","chapter":"Retirement & Death of Partner","unit":"Reconstitution of a Partnership firm","kind":"fact","front":"No information on how continuing partners acquire the outgoing partner's share: in what ratio is it assumed?","back":"it is assumed that they will acquire it in the old profit sharing ratio and so share the future profits in their old ratio.","quote":"In fact, in the absence of any information regarding profit sharing ratio in which the remaining partners acquire the share of retiring/deceased partner, it is assumed that they will acquire it in the old profit sharing ratio and so share the future profits in their old ratio.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy – Partnership Accounts","code":"leac103","chapterNo":3,"page":108,"section":"3.2 New Profit Sharing Ratio"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["new profit sharing ratio"],"order":90},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner.fact-situation-a","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner","chapter":"Retirement & Death of Partner","unit":"Reconstitution of a Partnership firm","kind":"fact","front":"Situation (a) for new profit sharing ratio: continuing partners acquire the share in the old ratio. Is a new ratio computed?","back":"normally, the continuing partners acquire the share of retiring or deceased partners in the old profit sharing ratio, and there is no need to compute the new profit sharing ratio among them, as it will be same as the old profit sharing ratio among them.","quote":"normally, the continuing partners acquire the share of retiring or deceased partners in the old profit sharing ratio, and there is no need to compute the new profit sharing ratio among them, as it will be same as the old profit sharing ratio among them.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy – Partnership Accounts","code":"leac103","chapterNo":3,"page":108,"section":"3.2 New Profit Sharing Ratio"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["new profit sharing ratio"],"order":100},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner.fact-situation-b","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner","chapter":"Retirement & Death of Partner","unit":"Reconstitution of a Partnership firm","kind":"fact","front":"Situation (b): continuing partners acquire the share in a proportion other than their old ratio. What follows?","back":"In that case, there is need to compute the new profit sharing ratio among them.","quote":"The continuing partners may acquire the share in the profits of the retiring/deceased partner in a proportion other than their old ratio, In that case, there is need to compute the new profit sharing ratio among them.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy – Partnership Accounts","code":"leac103","chapterNo":3,"page":108,"section":"3.2 New Profit Sharing Ratio"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["new profit sharing ratio"],"order":110},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner.fm-new-share","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner","chapter":"Retirement & Death of Partner","unit":"Reconstitution of a Partnership firm","kind":"formula","front":"Formula: new share of a continuing partner.","back":"New share of Continuing Partner = Old Share + Acquired share from the Outgoing Partner","quote":"New share of Continuing Partner = Old Share + Acquired share from the Outgoing Partner","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy – Partnership Accounts","code":"leac103","chapterNo":3,"page":109,"section":"3.2 New Profit Sharing Ratio"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["new profit sharing ratio","formula"],"order":120,"note":"New share = old share plus the part of the outgoing partner's share the continuing partner takes over."},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner.fact-situation-c","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner","chapter":"Retirement & Death of Partner","unit":"Reconstitution of a Partnership firm","kind":"fact","front":"Situation (c): the continuing partners agree on a specified new profit sharing ratio. What is the new ratio?","back":"In that case the ratio so specified will be the new profit sharing ratio.","quote":"The contributing partners may agree on a specified new profit sharing ratio: In that case the ratio so specified will be the new profit sharing ratio.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy – Partnership Accounts","code":"leac103","chapterNo":3,"page":109,"section":"3.2 New Profit Sharing Ratio"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["new profit sharing ratio"],"order":130},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner.fact-goodwill-who-compensates","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner","chapter":"Retirement & Death of Partner","unit":"Reconstitution of a Partnership firm","kind":"fact","front":"On retirement/death, who compensates the outgoing partner for his share of goodwill, and in what ratio?","back":"the retiring/ deceased partner compensated for his share of goodwill by the continuing partners (who have gained due to acquisition of share of profit from the retiring/ deceased partner) in their gaining ratio.","quote":"Hence, at the time of retirement/death of a partner, goodwill is valued as per agreement among the partners the retiring/ deceased partner compensated for his share of goodwill by the continuing partners (who have gained due to acquisition of share of profit from the retiring/ deceased partner) in their gaining ratio.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy – Partnership Accounts","code":"leac103","chapterNo":3,"page":113,"section":"3.4 Treatment of Goodwill"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["goodwill","gaining ratio"],"order":280},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner.fact-goodwill-not-in-books-rule","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner","chapter":"Retirement & Death of Partner","unit":"Reconstitution of a Partnership firm","kind":"fact","front":"Goodwill does not appear in the firm's books on retirement. How is credit given to the retiring partner?","back":"credit is given to the retiring partner for the share in goodwill by debiting the goodwill account to gaining partners capital accounts (individually) in their gaining ratio","quote":"When goodwill does not appear in the books of the firm, credit is given to the retiring partner for the share in goodwill by debiting the goodwill account to gaining partners capital accounts (individually) in their gaining ratio.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy – Partnership Accounts","code":"leac103","chapterNo":3,"page":113,"section":"3.4.1 When goodwill does not appear in the books"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["goodwill"],"order":290},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner.entry-goodwill-not-in-books","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner","chapter":"Retirement & Death of Partner","unit":"Reconstitution of a Partnership firm","kind":"entry","front":"Journal entry: goodwill not in the books; retiring partner's share of goodwill adjusted to gaining partners.","back":"Gaining Partners Capital A/c\nDr. (Individually)\nTo Retiring Partners Capital A/c\n(Share in goodwill of retiring partner adjusted)","quote":"Gaining Partners Capital A/c\nDr. (Individually)\nTo Retiring Partners Capital A/c\n(Share in goodwill of retiring partner adjusted)","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy – Partnership Accounts","code":"leac103","chapterNo":3,"page":114,"section":"3.4.1 When goodwill does not appear in the books"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["goodwill","journal entry"],"order":300},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner.fact-goodwill-continuing-sacrifices","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner","chapter":"Retirement & Death of Partner","unit":"Reconstitution of a Partnership firm","kind":"fact","front":"A continuing partner also sacrifices part of his share. How is his capital account treated for goodwill?","back":"his capital account will also be credited along with the retiring/deceased partner's capital account in proportion to his sacrifice and the other continuing partners' capital accounts will be debited based on their gain in the future profit ratio.","quote":"In such a situation his capital account will also be credited along with the retiring/deceased partner's capital account in proportion to his sacrifice and the other continuing partners' capital accounts will be debited based on their gain in the future profit ratio.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy – Partnership Accounts","code":"leac103","chapterNo":3,"page":114,"section":"3.4.1 When goodwill does not appear in the books"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["goodwill","sacrifice"],"order":310},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner.entry-shilpa-neeru-deepa","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner","chapter":"Retirement & Death of Partner","unit":"Reconstitution of a Partnership firm","kind":"entry","front":"Journal entry: Shilpa compensates retiring Neeru and sacrificing Deepa for goodwill.","back":"Shilpa's Capital A/c\nDr.\nTo Neeru's Capital A/c\nTo Deepa's Capital A/c\n(Shilpa compensated Neeru for her share of\ngoodwill and to Deepa for the sacrifice made\nby her on Neeru's retirement)","quote":"Shilpa's Capital A/c\nDr.\nTo Neeru's Capital A/c\nTo Deepa's Capital A/c\n(Shilpa compensated Neeru for her share of\ngoodwill and to Deepa for the sacrifice made\nby her on Neeru's retirement)","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy – Partnership Accounts","code":"leac103","chapterNo":3,"page":116,"section":"3.4.1 When goodwill does not appear in the books"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["goodwill","journal entry","example"],"order":340},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner.entry-pammy-goodwill-adjusted","v":2,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner","chapter":"Retirement & Death of Partner","unit":"Reconstitution of a Partnership firm","kind":"entry","front":"Journal entry: after writing off old goodwill, Pammy's share of new goodwill is adjusted to Hanny and Sunny.","back":"Hanny's Capital\nDr.\nSunny's Capital\nDr.\nTo Pammy's Capital A/c\n(Pammy's share of goodwill adjusted to\nHanny's and Sunny's capital account\nto the extent of their gain)","quote":"Hanny's Capital\nDr.\nSunny's Capital\nDr.\nTo Pammy's Capital A/c\n(Pammy's share of goodwill adjusted to\nHanny's and Sunny's capital account\nto the extent of their gain)","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy – Partnership Accounts","code":"leac103","chapterNo":3,"page":118,"section":"3.4.1 When goodwill does not appear in the books"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["goodwill","journal entry","example"],"order":360},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner.def-hidden-goodwill","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner","chapter":"Retirement & Death of Partner","unit":"Reconstitution of a Partnership firm","kind":"definition","front":"What is hidden goodwill (retiring/deceased partner settled by a lump sum)?","back":"If the firm has agreed to settle the retiring or deceased partner's account by paying him a lump sum amount, then the amount paid to him in excess of what is due to him, based on the balance in his capital account after making necessary adjustments in respect of accumulated profits and losses and revaluation of assets and liabilities, etc., shall be treated as his share of goodwill (known as hidden goodwill).","quote":"If the firm has agreed to settle the retiring or deceased partner's account by paying him a lump sum amount, then the amount paid to him in excess of what is due to him, based on the balance in his capital account after making necessary adjustments in respect of accumulated profits and losses and revaluation of assets and liabilities, etc., shall be treated as his share of goodwill (known as hidden goodwill).","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy – Partnership Accounts","code":"leac103","chapterNo":3,"page":118,"section":"3.4.2 Hidden Goodwill"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["hidden goodwill","goodwill"],"order":370},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner.ex-hidden-goodwill-r","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner","chapter":"Retirement & Death of Partner","unit":"Reconstitution of a Partnership firm","kind":"example","front":"R's capital balance after adjustments is Rs. 60,000, but P and Q agree to pay him Rs. 75,000. What is the Rs. 15,000?","back":"It implies that Rs. 15,000 is R's share of goodwill of the firm.","quote":"It implies that Rs. 15,000 is R's share of goodwill of the firm.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy – Partnership Accounts","code":"leac103","chapterNo":3,"page":119,"section":"3.4.2 Hidden Goodwill"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["hidden goodwill","example"],"order":380},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner.entry-hidden-goodwill-entry","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner","chapter":"Retirement & Death of Partner","unit":"Reconstitution of a Partnership firm","kind":"entry","front":"Journal entry: hidden goodwill; R's share of goodwill adjusted in P's and Q's capital accounts in gaining ratio 3:2.","back":"P's Capital A/c\nDr.\nQ's Capital A/c\nDr.\nTo R's Capital A/c\n(R's share of goodwill adjusted in P's\nand Q's capital accounts in their\ngaining ratio of 3:2)","quote":"P's Capital A/c\nDr.\nQ's Capital A/c\nDr.\nTo R's Capital A/c\n(R's share of goodwill adjusted in P's\nand Q's capital accounts in their\ngaining ratio of 3:2)","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy – Partnership Accounts","code":"leac103","chapterNo":3,"page":119,"section":"3.4.2 Hidden Goodwill"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["hidden goodwill","journal entry"],"order":390},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner.fact-revaluation-purpose","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner","chapter":"Retirement & Death of Partner","unit":"Reconstitution of a Partnership firm","kind":"fact","front":"Why is a Revaluation Account prepared on retirement/death, and in what ratio is its result shared?","back":"a Revaluation Account is prepared in order to ascertain net gain (loss) on revaluation of assets and/or liabilities and bringing unrecorded items into firm's books and the same is transferred to the capital account of all partners including retiring/deceased partners in their old profit sharing ratio.","quote":"a Revaluation Account is prepared in order to ascertain net gain (loss) on revaluation of assets and/or liabilities and bringing unrecorded items into firm's books and the same is transferred to the capital account of all partners including retiring/deceased partners in their old profit sharing ratio.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy – Partnership Accounts","code":"leac103","chapterNo":3,"page":120,"section":"3.5 Adjustment for Revaluation of Assets and Liabilities"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["revaluation"],"order":400},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner.entry-reval-asset-up","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner","chapter":"Retirement & Death of Partner","unit":"Reconstitution of a Partnership firm","kind":"entry","front":"Journal entry: increase in the value of assets (retirement/death revaluation).","back":"Assets A/c's (Individually)\nDr.\nTo Revaluation A/c\n(Increase in the value of assets)","quote":"Assets A/c's (Individually)\nDr.\nTo Revaluation A/c\n(Increase in the value of assets)","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy – Partnership Accounts","code":"leac103","chapterNo":3,"page":120,"section":"3.5 Adjustment for Revaluation of Assets and Liabilities"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["revaluation","journal entry"],"order":410},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner.entry-reval-asset-down","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner","chapter":"Retirement & Death of Partner","unit":"Reconstitution of a Partnership firm","kind":"entry","front":"Journal entry: decrease in the value of assets (retirement/death revaluation).","back":"Revaluation A/c\nDr.\nTo Assets A/c's (Individually)\n(Decrease in the value of assets)","quote":"Revaluation A/c\nDr.\nTo Assets A/c's (Individually)\n(Decrease in the value of assets)","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy – Partnership Accounts","code":"leac103","chapterNo":3,"page":120,"section":"3.5 Adjustment for Revaluation of Assets and Liabilities"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["revaluation","journal entry"],"order":420},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner.entry-reval-liab-up","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner","chapter":"Retirement & Death of Partner","unit":"Reconstitution of a Partnership firm","kind":"entry","front":"Journal entry: increase in the amount of liabilities (retirement/death revaluation).","back":"Revaluation A/c\nDr.\nTo Liabilities A/c (Individually)\n(Increase in the amount of liabilities)","quote":"Revaluation A/c\nDr.\nTo Liabilities A/c (Individually)\n(Increase in the amount of liabilities)","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy – Partnership Accounts","code":"leac103","chapterNo":3,"page":120,"section":"3.5 Adjustment for Revaluation of Assets and Liabilities"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["revaluation","journal entry"],"order":430},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner.entry-reval-liab-down","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner","chapter":"Retirement & Death of Partner","unit":"Reconstitution of a Partnership firm","kind":"entry","front":"Journal entry: decrease in the amount of liabilities (retirement/death revaluation).","back":"Liabilities A/c's (Individually)\nDr.\nTo Revaluation A/c\n(Decrease in the amount of liabilities)","quote":"Liabilities A/c's (Individually)\nDr.\nTo Revaluation A/c\n(Decrease in the amount of liabilities)","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy – Partnership Accounts","code":"leac103","chapterNo":3,"page":120,"section":"3.5 Adjustment for Revaluation of Assets and Liabilities"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["revaluation","journal entry"],"order":440},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner.entry-reval-unrecorded-asset","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner","chapter":"Retirement & Death of Partner","unit":"Reconstitution of a Partnership firm","kind":"entry","front":"Journal entry: an unrecorded asset brought into the books.","back":"Assets A/c\nDr.\nTo Revaluation A/c\n(Unrecorded asset brought into book)","quote":"Assets A/c\nDr.\nTo Revaluation A/c\n(Unrecorded asset brought into book)","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy – Partnership Accounts","code":"leac103","chapterNo":3,"page":120,"section":"3.5 Adjustment for Revaluation of Assets and Liabilities"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["revaluation","journal entry"],"order":450},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner.entry-reval-unrecorded-liab","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner","chapter":"Retirement & Death of Partner","unit":"Reconstitution of a Partnership firm","kind":"entry","front":"Journal entry: an unrecorded liability brought into the books.","back":"Revaluation A/c\nDr.\nTo Liability A/c\n(Unrecorded liability brought into books)","quote":"Revaluation A/c\nDr.\nTo Liability A/c\n(Unrecorded liability brought into books)","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy – Partnership Accounts","code":"leac103","chapterNo":3,"page":120,"section":"3.5 Adjustment for Revaluation of Assets and Liabilities"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["revaluation","journal entry"],"order":460},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner.entry-reval-profit","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner","chapter":"Retirement & Death of Partner","unit":"Reconstitution of a Partnership firm","kind":"entry","front":"Journal entry: distribution of profit on revaluation.","back":"Revaluation A/c\nDr.\nTo All Partners' Capital A/c's (Individually)\n(Profit on revaluation transferred\nto partner's capital)","quote":"Revaluation A/c\nDr.\nTo All Partners' Capital A/c's (Individually)\n(Profit on revaluation transferred\nto partner's capital)","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy – Partnership Accounts","code":"leac103","chapterNo":3,"page":120,"section":"3.5 Adjustment for Revaluation of Assets and Liabilities"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["revaluation","journal entry"],"order":470},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner.entry-reval-loss","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner","chapter":"Retirement & Death of Partner","unit":"Reconstitution of a Partnership firm","kind":"entry","front":"Journal entry: distribution of loss on revaluation.","back":"All Partners' Capital A/c's (Individually)\nDr.\nTo Revaluation A/c\n(Loss on revaluation transferred to\npartner's capital accounts)","quote":"All Partners' Capital A/c's (Individually)\nDr.\nTo Revaluation A/c\n(Loss on revaluation transferred to\npartner's capital accounts)","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy – Partnership Accounts","code":"leac103","chapterNo":3,"page":121,"section":"3.5 Adjustment for Revaluation of Assets and Liabilities"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["revaluation","journal entry"],"order":480},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner.fact-accumulated-entitled","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner","chapter":"Retirement & Death of Partner","unit":"Reconstitution of a Partnership firm","kind":"fact","front":"What is the retiring/deceased partner's position on accumulated profits and losses?","back":"The retiring/deceased partner is entitled to his/her share in the accumulated profits and is also liable to share the accumulated losses, if any.","quote":"The retiring/deceased partner is entitled to his/her share in the accumulated profits and is also liable to share the accumulated losses, if any.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy – Partnership Accounts","code":"leac103","chapterNo":3,"page":122,"section":"3.6 Adjustment of Accumulated Profits and Losses"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["accumulated profits"],"order":490},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner.fact-accumulated-belong-all","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner","chapter":"Retirement & Death of Partner","unit":"Reconstitution of a Partnership firm","kind":"fact","front":"Accumulated profits or losses on a partner's retirement or death: whose are they and how are they transferred?","back":"These accumulated profits or losses belong to all the partners and should be transferred to the capital accounts of all partners in their old profit sharing ratio.","quote":"These accumulated profits or losses belong to all the partners and should be transferred to the capital accounts of all partners in their old profit sharing ratio.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy – Partnership Accounts","code":"leac103","chapterNo":3,"page":122,"section":"3.6 Adjustment of Accumulated Profits and Losses"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["accumulated profits"],"order":500},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner.entry-reserves-transfer","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner","chapter":"Retirement & Death of Partner","unit":"Reconstitution of a Partnership firm","kind":"entry","front":"Journal entry: transfer of accumulated profits (reserves) to partners.","back":"Reserves A/c\nDr.\nTo All Partners' Capital A/c's (Individually)\n(Reserves transferred to all partners'\ncapital account's in old profit sharing ratio).","quote":"Reserves A/c\nDr.\nTo All Partners' Capital A/c's (Individually)\n(Reserves transferred to all partners'\ncapital account's in old profit sharing ratio).","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy – Partnership Accounts","code":"leac103","chapterNo":3,"page":122,"section":"3.6 Adjustment of Accumulated Profits and Losses"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["accumulated profits","journal entry"],"order":510},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner.entry-accumulated-loss-transfer","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner","chapter":"Retirement & Death of Partner","unit":"Reconstitution of a Partnership firm","kind":"entry","front":"Journal entry: transfer of accumulated losses to partners.","back":"All Partners' Capital A/c's (Individually)\nDr.\nTo Profit and Loss A/c\n(Accumulated loss transferred to all partners'\ncapital accounts in their old profit-sharing ratio)","quote":"All Partners' Capital A/c's (Individually)\nDr.\nTo Profit and Loss A/c\n(Accumulated loss transferred to all partners'\ncapital accounts in their old profit-sharing ratio)","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy – Partnership Accounts","code":"leac103","chapterNo":3,"page":122,"section":"3.6 Adjustment of Accumulated Profits and Losses"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["accumulated profits","journal entry"],"order":520},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner.fact-middle-claim","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner","chapter":"Retirement & Death of Partner","unit":"Reconstitution of a Partnership firm","kind":"fact","front":"If a partner retires in the middle of the year, what does his claim include beyond the year-end balances?","back":"the claim shall include share of profit or loss, interest on capital, interest on drawings if any, from the date of last balance sheet to the date of retirement.","quote":"In such a case the claim shall include share of profit or loss, interest on capital, interest on drawings if any, from the date of last balance sheet to the date of retirement.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy – Partnership Accounts","code":"leac103","chapterNo":3,"page":123,"section":"When Partner Retires in the Middle of the Year"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["intervening period"],"order":530},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner.entry-retiring-pl-suspense","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner","chapter":"Retirement & Death of Partner","unit":"Reconstitution of a Partnership firm","kind":"entry","front":"Journal entry: retiring partner's share of profit for the intervening period.","back":"Profit & Loss Suspense A/c\nDr.\nTo Retiring Partners Capital A/c\n(Share of profit for intervening/period)","quote":"Profit & Loss Suspense A/c\nDr.\nTo Retiring Partners Capital A/c\n(Share of profit for intervening/period)","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy – Partnership Accounts","code":"leac103","chapterNo":3,"page":124,"section":"When Partner Retires in the Middle of the Year"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["intervening period","journal entry"],"order":540},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner.entry-close-pl-suspense","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner","chapter":"Retirement & Death of Partner","unit":"Reconstitution of a Partnership firm","kind":"entry","front":"Journal entry: closing the Profit & Loss Suspense Account to the gaining partners.","back":"Gaining Partners Capital A/c\nDr. (in gaining ratio)\nTo Profit & Loss Suspense A/c","quote":"Gaining Partners Capital A/c\nDr. (in gaining ratio)\nTo Profit & Loss Suspense A/c","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy – Partnership Accounts","code":"leac103","chapterNo":3,"page":125,"section":"When Partner Retires in the Middle of the Year"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["intervening period","journal entry"],"order":550},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner.entry-retiring-alt-entry","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner","chapter":"Retirement & Death of Partner","unit":"Reconstitution of a Partnership firm","kind":"entry","front":"Alternative journal entry in place of the suspense entries: retiring partner's share of profit credited.","back":"Gaining Partners Capital A/c\nDr.\nTo Retiring partners Capital A/c\n(Share of profit of retiring partner credited)","quote":"Gaining Partners Capital A/c\nDr.\nTo Retiring partners Capital A/c\n(Share of profit of retiring partner credited)","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy – Partnership Accounts","code":"leac103","chapterNo":3,"page":125,"section":"When Partner Retires in the Middle of the Year"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["intervening period","journal entry"],"order":560},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner.types-settlement-modes","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner","chapter":"Retirement & Death of Partner","unit":"Reconstitution of a Partnership firm","kind":"types","front":"Modes of settling the account of the outgoing partner (as per the partnership deed).","back":"in lumpsum immediately\nin various instalments with or without interest as agreed\npartly in cash immediately and partly in instalment at the agreed intervals","quote":"The outgoing partner's account is settled as per the terms of partnership deed i.e., in lumpsum immediately or in various instalments with or without interest as agreed or partly in cash immediately and partly in instalment at the agreed intervals.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy – Partnership Accounts","code":"leac103","chapterNo":3,"page":125,"section":"3.7 Disposal of Amount Due to Retiring Partner"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["settlement","outgoing partner"],"order":570},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner.fact-section-37","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner","chapter":"Retirement & Death of Partner","unit":"Reconstitution of a Partnership firm","kind":"fact","front":"No agreement on paying the outgoing partner: what option does Section 37 of the Indian Partnership Act, 1932 give?","back":"the outgoing partner has an option to receive either interest @ 6% p.a. till the date of payment or such share of profits which has been earned with his/her money (i.e., based on capital ratio).","quote":"In the absence of any agreement, Section 37 of the Indian Partnership Act, 1932 is applicable, which states that the outgoing partner has an option to receive either interest @ 6% p.a. till the date of payment or such share of profits which has been earned with his/her money (i.e., based on capital ratio).","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy – Partnership Accounts","code":"leac103","chapterNo":3,"page":125,"section":"3.7 Disposal of Amount Due to Retiring Partner"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["settlement","section 37"],"order":580},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner.fact-due-to-loan-account","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner","chapter":"Retirement & Death of Partner","unit":"Reconstitution of a Partnership firm","kind":"fact","front":"If the firm cannot pay the retiring partner immediately, where is the amount due transferred?","back":"In case the firm is not in a position to make the payment immediately, the amount due is transferred to the retiring Partner's Loan Account, and as and when the amount is paid it is debited to his account.","quote":"In case the firm is not in a position to make the payment immediately, the amount due is transferred to the retiring Partner's Loan Account, and as and when the amount is paid it is debited to his account.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy – Partnership Accounts","code":"leac103","chapterNo":3,"page":125,"section":"3.7 Disposal of Amount Due to Retiring Partner"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["settlement","loan account"],"order":590},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner.entry-paid-cash-full","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner","chapter":"Retirement & Death of Partner","unit":"Reconstitution of a Partnership firm","kind":"entry","front":"Journal entry: retiring partner is paid cash in full.","back":"Retiring Partners' Capital A/c\nDr.\nTo Cash/Bank A/c","quote":"1. When retiring partner is paid cash in full.\nRetiring Partners' Capital A/c\nDr.\nTo Cash/Bank A/c","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy – Partnership Accounts","code":"leac103","chapterNo":3,"page":125,"section":"3.7 Disposal of Amount Due to Retiring Partner"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["settlement","journal entry"],"order":600},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner.entry-whole-as-loan","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner","chapter":"Retirement & Death of Partner","unit":"Reconstitution of a Partnership firm","kind":"entry","front":"Journal entry: retiring partner's whole amount is treated as a loan.","back":"Retiring Partners' Capital A/c\nDr.\nTo Retiring Partners' Loan A/c","quote":"2. When retiring partners' whole amount is treated as loan.\nRetiring Partners' Capital A/c\nDr.\nTo Retiring Partners' Loan A/c","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy – Partnership Accounts","code":"leac103","chapterNo":3,"page":125,"section":"3.7 Disposal of Amount Due to Retiring Partner"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["settlement","journal entry","loan account"],"order":610},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner.entry-part-cash-part-loan","v":2,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner","chapter":"Retirement & Death of Partner","unit":"Reconstitution of a Partnership firm","kind":"entry","front":"Journal entry: retiring partner is partly paid in cash and the rest treated as a loan.","back":"Retiring Partners' Capital A/c Dr. (Total Amount due)\nTo Cash/Bank A/c (Amount Paid)\nTo Retiring Partners' Loan A/c (Amount of Loan)","quote":"3. When retiring partner is partly paid in cash and the remaining amount treated as loan.\nRetiring Partners' Capital A/c Dr. (Total Amount due)\nTo Cash/Bank A/c (Amount Paid)\nTo Retiring Partners' Loan A/c (Amount of Loan)","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy – Partnership Accounts","code":"leac103","chapterNo":3,"page":125,"section":"3.7 Disposal of Amount Due to Retiring Partner"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["settlement","journal entry","loan account"],"order":620},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner.entry-loan-interest","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner","chapter":"Retirement & Death of Partner","unit":"Reconstitution of a Partnership firm","kind":"entry","front":"Journal entry: interest on the retiring partner's loan (loan settled by instalments).","back":"Interest A/c\nDr.\nTo Retiring Partner's Loan A/c","quote":"a) For interest on loan\nInterest A/c\nDr.\nTo Retiring Partner's Loan A/c","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy – Partnership Accounts","code":"leac103","chapterNo":3,"page":126,"section":"3.7 Disposal of Amount Due to Retiring Partner"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["settlement","journal entry","loan account"],"order":630},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner.entry-loan-instalment","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner","chapter":"Retirement & Death of Partner","unit":"Reconstitution of a Partnership firm","kind":"entry","front":"Journal entry: payment of an instalment of the retiring partner's loan.","back":"Retiring Partner's Loan A/c\nDr.\nTo Cash/Bank A/c","quote":"b) For payment of instalment\nRetiring Partner's Loan A/c\nDr.\nTo Cash/Bank A/c","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy – Partnership Accounts","code":"leac103","chapterNo":3,"page":126,"section":"3.7 Disposal of Amount Due to Retiring Partner"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["settlement","journal entry","loan account"],"order":640},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner.fact-loan-balance-sheet","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner","chapter":"Retirement & Death of Partner","unit":"Reconstitution of a Partnership firm","kind":"fact","front":"Where is the balance of the retiring partner's loan account shown in the Balance Sheet, and until when?","back":"The balance of the retiring partner's loan account is shown on the liabilities side of the Balance Sheet till the last instalment is paid to him/her.","quote":"Note: 1. The balance of the retiring partner's loan account is shown on the liabilities side of the Balance Sheet till the last instalment is paid to him/her.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy – Partnership Accounts","code":"leac103","chapterNo":3,"page":126,"section":"3.7 Disposal of Amount Due to Retiring Partner"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["loan account"],"order":650},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner.fact-executor-account","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner","chapter":"Retirement & Death of Partner","unit":"Reconstitution of a Partnership firm","kind":"fact","front":"On death of a partner, where is the amount credited to him/her transferred?","back":"in case of death of a partner, the amount credited to him/her is transferred to his Executors' Account","quote":"It may noted that the accounting treatment for disposal of amount due to retiring partner and deceased partner is similar with a difference that in case of death of a partner, the amount credited to him/her is transferred to his Executors' Account and the payment has to be made to him/her.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy – Partnership Accounts","code":"leac103","chapterNo":3,"page":128,"section":"3.7 Disposal of Amount Due to Retiring Partner"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["death","executor"],"order":660},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner.fact-capital-adjust-decide","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner","chapter":"Retirement & Death of Partner","unit":"Reconstitution of a Partnership firm","kind":"fact","front":"What may the remaining partners decide on retirement or death regarding their capitals?","back":"the remaining partners may decide to adjust their capital contributions in their profit sharing ratio.","quote":"At the time of retirement or death of a partner, the remaining partners may decide to adjust their capital contributions in their profit sharing ratio.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy – Partnership Accounts","code":"leac103","chapterNo":3,"page":134,"section":"3.8 Adjustment of Partners' Capitals"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["capital adjustment"],"order":670},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner.fact-capital-total-new-firm","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner","chapter":"Retirement & Death of Partner","unit":"Reconstitution of a Partnership firm","kind":"fact","front":"In adjusting capitals, what is treated as the total capital of the new firm, unless specified otherwise?","back":"the sum of balances in the capitals of continuing partners may be treated as the total capital of the new firm, unless specified otherwise.","quote":"In such a situation, the sum of balances in the capitals of continuing partners may be treated as the total capital of the new firm, unless specified otherwise.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy – Partnership Accounts","code":"leac103","chapterNo":3,"page":134,"section":"3.8 Adjustment of Partners' Capitals"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["capital adjustment"],"order":680},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner.fact-capital-new-capital","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner","chapter":"Retirement & Death of Partner","unit":"Reconstitution of a Partnership firm","kind":"fact","front":"How is the new capital of each continuing partner ascertained, and how are excess or deficiency found?","back":"to ascertain the new capital of the continuing partners, the total capital of the firm is divided amongst the remaining partners as per the new profit sharing ratio, and the excess or deficiency of capital in the individual capital account's may be worked out.","quote":"Then, to ascertain the new capital of the continuing partners, the total capital of the firm is divided amongst the remaining partners as per the new profit sharing ratio, and the excess or deficiency of capital in the individual capital account's may be worked out.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy – Partnership Accounts","code":"leac103","chapterNo":3,"page":134,"section":"3.8 Adjustment of Partners' Capitals"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["capital adjustment"],"order":690},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner.entry-capital-excess-withdrawn","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner","chapter":"Retirement & Death of Partner","unit":"Reconstitution of a Partnership firm","kind":"entry","front":"Journal entry: excess capital withdrawn by the partner.","back":"Partners' Capital A/c\nDr.\nTo Cash / Bank A/c","quote":"(i) For excess capital withdrawn by the partner :\nPartners' Capital A/c\nDr.\nTo Cash / Bank A/c","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy – Partnership Accounts","code":"leac103","chapterNo":3,"page":134,"section":"3.8 Adjustment of Partners' Capitals"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["capital adjustment","journal entry"],"order":700},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner.entry-capital-brought-in","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner","chapter":"Retirement & Death of Partner","unit":"Reconstitution of a Partnership firm","kind":"entry","front":"Journal entry: capital to be brought in by the partner.","back":"Cash / Bank A/c\nDr.\nTo Partners' Capital A/c","quote":"(ii) For amount of capital to be brought in by the partner:\nCash / Bank A/c\nDr.\nTo Partners' Capital A/c","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy – Partnership Accounts","code":"leac103","chapterNo":3,"page":134,"section":"3.8 Adjustment of Partners' Capitals"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["capital adjustment","journal entry"],"order":710},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner.fact-death-similar","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner","chapter":"Retirement & Death of Partner","unit":"Reconstitution of a Partnership firm","kind":"fact","front":"How does the accounting on death of a partner compare with retirement, and to whom is the claim transferred?","back":"the accounting treatment in the event of death of a partner is similar to that in case of retirement of a partner, and that in case of death of a partner his claim is transferred to his executors and settled in the same manner as that of the retired partner.","quote":"As stated earlier, the accounting treatment in the event of death of a partner is similar to that in case of retirement of a partner, and that in case of death of a partner his claim is transferred to his executors and settled in the same manner as that of the retired partner.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy – Partnership Accounts","code":"leac103","chapterNo":3,"page":140,"section":"3.9 Death of a Partner"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["death","executor"],"order":760},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner.fact-death-timing","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner","chapter":"Retirement & Death of Partner","unit":"Reconstitution of a Partnership firm","kind":"fact","front":"Retirement versus death of a partner: what is the major difference in timing?","back":"while the retirement normally takes place at the end of an accounting period, the death of a partner may occur any time.","quote":"while the retirement normally takes place at the end of an accounting period, the death of a partner may occur any time.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy – Partnership Accounts","code":"leac103","chapterNo":3,"page":140,"section":"3.9 Death of a Partner"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["death","intervening period"],"order":770},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner.fact-death-claim-includes","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner","chapter":"Retirement & Death of Partner","unit":"Reconstitution of a Partnership firm","kind":"fact","front":"On death, what does the deceased partner's claim also include from the last Balance Sheet date?","back":"his claim shall also include his share of profit or loss, interest on capital, interest on drawings (if any) from the date of the last Balance Sheet to the date of his death","quote":"Hence, in case of a partner, his claim shall also include his share of profit or loss, interest on capital, interest on drawings (if any) from the date of the last Balance Sheet to the date of his death","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy – Partnership Accounts","code":"leac103","chapterNo":3,"page":140,"section":"3.9 Death of a Partner"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["death","intervening period"],"order":780},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner.fm-intervening-profit","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner","chapter":"Retirement & Death of Partner","unit":"Reconstitution of a Partnership firm","kind":"formula","front":"Formula: retiring/deceased partner's share of profit for the intervening period (on preceding year's profit).","back":"Proceeding Year's Profit × Proportionate Period × Share of Deceased Partner","quote":"Proceeding Year's Profit × Proportionate Period × Share of Deceased Partner","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy – Partnership Accounts","code":"leac103","chapterNo":3,"page":141,"section":"3.9 Death of a Partner"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["intervening period","formula"],"order":790,"note":"NCERT prints 'Proceeding'; it means the preceding year's profit, times the months elapsed over 12, times his profit share."},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner.types-death-profit-bases","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner","chapter":"Retirement & Death of Partner","unit":"Reconstitution of a Partnership firm","kind":"types","front":"Bases for calculating the deceased partner's share of profit for the intervening period.","back":"last year's profit\naverage of past few years\nsales","quote":"considered cumbersome to close the books and prepare final account, for the period, the deceased partner's share of profit may be calculated on the basis of last year's profit (or average of past few years) or on the basis of sales.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy – Partnership Accounts","code":"leac103","chapterNo":3,"page":141,"section":"3.9 Death of a Partner"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["death","intervening period"],"order":810},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner.entry-death-close-suspense","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner","chapter":"Retirement & Death of Partner","unit":"Reconstitution of a Partnership firm","kind":"entry","front":"Journal entry: closing the Profit & Loss Suspense Account to the gaining partners on death.","back":"Gaining Partners Capital A/c [In gaining ratio]\nTo Profit and Loss Suspense A/c\n(P&L Suspense account transferred).","quote":"Gaining Partners Capital A/c [In gaining ratio]\nTo Profit and Loss Suspense A/c\n(P&L Suspense account transferred).","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy – Partnership Accounts","code":"leac103","chapterNo":3,"page":142,"section":"3.9 Death of a Partner"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["death","journal entry"],"order":830},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner.entry-death-alt-entry","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner","chapter":"Retirement & Death of Partner","unit":"Reconstitution of a Partnership firm","kind":"entry","front":"Alternative journal entry on death: deceased partner's share of profit credited by the gaining partners.","back":"Gaining Partners' Capital A/c\nDr.\nTo Deceased Partner Capital A/c\n(share of profit of Deceased Partner credited)","quote":"Gaining Partners' Capital A/c\nDr.\nTo Deceased Partner Capital A/c\n(share of profit of Deceased Partner credited)","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy – Partnership Accounts","code":"leac103","chapterNo":3,"page":142,"section":"3.9 Death of a Partner"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["death","journal entry"],"order":840},
{"id":"kc.accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner.fact-summary-goodwill-written-off","v":1,"subject":"accountancy","chapterKey":"accountancy.retirement-and-death-of-partner","chapter":"Retirement & Death of Partner","unit":"Reconstitution of a Partnership firm","kind":"fact","front":"If goodwill already appears in the books on retirement/death, how is it written off?","back":"it will be written off by debiting all partner's capital account in their old profit sharing ratio.","quote":"If goodwill already appears in the books, it will be written off by debiting all partner's capital account in their old profit sharing ratio.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Accountancy – Partnership Accounts","code":"leac103","chapterNo":3,"page":147,"section":"Summary"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["goodwill"],"order":850},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.def-managerial-principle","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"definition","front":"Define a managerial principle.","back":"A managerial principle is a broad and general guideline for decision-making and behaviour.","quote":"A managerial principle is a broad and general guideline for decision-making and behaviour.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":32,"section":"Principles of Management: The Concept"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["principles"],"order":10},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.def-techniques-of-management","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"definition","front":"What are techniques of management?","back":"Techniques are procedures or methods, which involve a series of steps to be taken to accomplish desired goals.","quote":"Techniques are procedures or methods, which involve a series of steps to be taken to accomplish desired goals.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":33,"section":"Principles of Management: The Concept"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["principles","techniques"],"order":20},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.key-principles-vs-techniques","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"key_line","front":"Principles are ____ to take decisions or actions while practicing techniques.","back":"Principles are guidelines to take decisions or actions while practicing techniques.","quote":"Principles are guidelines to take decisions or actions while practicing techniques.","blanks":["guidelines"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":33,"section":"Principles of Management: The Concept"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["principles","techniques"],"order":30},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.def-values","v":2,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"definition","front":"What are values, in contrast to principles?","back":"Values are something, which are acceptable or desirable. They have moral connotations.","quote":"Values are something, which are acceptable or desirable. They have moral connotations.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":33,"section":"Principles of Management: The Concept"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["values"],"order":40},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.diff-values-vs-principles","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"difference","front":"Values vs principles of management: how each is formed.","back":"Values are general rules for behaviour of individuals in society formed through common practice whereas principles of management are formed after research in work situations, which are technical in nature.","quote":"Values are general rules for behaviour of individuals in society formed through common practice whereas principles of management are formed after research in work situations, which are technical in nature.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":33,"section":"Principles of Management: The Concept"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["values","principles"],"order":50},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.def-principles-general-propositions","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"definition","front":"What are principles of management, by nature?","back":"Principles are general propositions, which are applicable when certain conditions are present.","quote":"Principles are general propositions, which are applicable when certain conditions are present.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":33,"section":"Nature of Principles of Management"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["nature"],"order":60},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.fact-principles-developed-on-basis","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"fact","front":"On what basis have the principles of management been developed?","back":"Observation and experimentation, and managers' personal experiences\nThese have been developed on the basis of observation and experimentation as well as personal experiences of the managers.","quote":"These have been developed on the basis of observation and experimentation as well as personal experiences of the managers.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":33,"section":"Nature of Principles of Management"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["nature"],"order":70},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.key-nature-science-art","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"key_line","front":"Derivation of these principles may be said to be a matter of ____ and their creative application may be regarded as an ____.","back":"Derivation of these principles may be said to be a matter of science and their creative application may be regarded as an art.","quote":"Derivation of these principles may be said to be a matter of science and their creative application may be regarded as an art.","blanks":["science","art"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":33,"section":"Nature of Principles of Management"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["nature","science","art"],"order":80},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.fact-principles-cause-effect-relationship","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"fact","front":"What kind of relationship do the principles of management denote?","back":"Cause and effect\nThese principles are guidelines to action. They denote a cause and effect relationship.","quote":"These principles are guidelines to action. They denote a cause and effect relationship.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":33,"section":"Nature of Principles of Management"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["nature"],"order":90},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.list-nature-of-principles","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"list","front":"Name the 7 points on the nature of principles of management.","back":"formed by practice\ngeneral guidelines\nuniversal\nflexible\nbehavioural\ncontingent\ncause and effect relationship","quote":"The nature of management principles can be discussed under the heads- formed by practice; general guidelines; universal; flexible; behavioural; contingent; and cause and effect relationship","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":64,"section":"Nature of Principles of Management"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["nature"],"order":100},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.key-nature-universal","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"key_line","front":"The principles of management are intended to apply to ____, business as well as non-business","back":"The principles of management are intended to apply to all types of organisations, business as well as non-business","quote":"The principles of management are intended to apply to all types of organisations, business as well as non-business","blanks":["all types of organisations"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":34,"section":"Nature of Principles of Management"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["nature","universal"],"order":110},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.fact-principles-not-straitjacket","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"fact","front":"As general guidelines, what do principles of management not provide?","back":"Readymade, straitjacket solutions to all managerial problems\nThe principles are guidelines to action but do not provide readymade, straitjacket solutions to all managerial problems.","quote":"The principles are guidelines to action but do not provide readymade, straitjacket solutions to all managerial problems.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":34,"section":"Nature of Principles of Management"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["nature"],"order":120},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.key-nature-formed-by-practice","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"key_line","front":"The principles of management are formed by ____ and collective wisdom of managers as well as ____.","back":"The principles of management are formed by experience and collective wisdom of managers as well as experimentation.","quote":"The principles of management are formed by experience and collective wisdom of managers as well as experimentation.","blanks":["experience","experimentation"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":34,"section":"Nature of Principles of Management"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["nature"],"order":130},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.fact-principles-flexible-not-rigid","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"fact","front":"Principles of management are not rigid prescriptions. Which nature point is this?","back":"Flexible\nThe principles of management are not rigid prescriptions, which have to be followed absolutely. They are flexible and can be modified by the manager when the situation so demands.","quote":"The principles of management are not rigid prescriptions, which have to be followed absolutely. They are flexible and can be modified by the manager when the situation so demands.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":34,"section":"Nature of Principles of Management"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["nature","flexible"],"order":140},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.key-nature-behavioural","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"key_line","front":"Management principles aim at influencing behaviour of human beings. Therefore, principles of management are mainly ____ in nature.","back":"Management principles aim at influencing behaviour of human beings. Therefore, principles of management are mainly behavioural in nature.","quote":"Management principles aim at influencing behaviour of human beings. Therefore, principles of management are mainly behavioural in nature.","blanks":["behavioural"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":35,"section":"Nature of Principles of Management"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["nature","behavioural"],"order":150},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.key-nature-cause-effect","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"key_line","front":"The principles of management are intended to establish relationship between ____","back":"The principles of management are intended to establish relationship between cause and effect","quote":"The principles of management are intended to establish relationship between cause and effect","blanks":["cause and effect"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":35,"section":"Nature of Principles of Management"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["nature"],"order":160},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.key-nature-contingent","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"key_line","front":"The application of principles of management is ____ or dependent upon the prevailing situation at a particular point of time.","back":"The application of principles of management is contingent or dependent upon the prevailing situation at a particular point of time.","quote":"The application of principles of management is contingent or dependent upon the prevailing situation at a particular point of time.","blanks":["contingent"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":35,"section":"Nature of Principles of Management"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["nature","contingent"],"order":170},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.list-significance-of-principles","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"list","front":"Name the 8 points on the significance of principles of management.","back":"Increase in efficiency\nOptimum utilisation of resources\nScientific decision making\nAdaptation to changing environment\nFulfilling social responsibilities\nProper research and development\nTraining managers\nEffective administration","quote":"The significance can be discussed under the following heads- Increase in efficiency; Optimum utilisation of resources; Scientific decision making; Adaptation to changing environment; Fulfilling social responsibilities; Proper research and development; Training managers; and Effective administration.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":64,"section":"Significance of Principles of Management"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["significance"],"order":190},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.def-optimum-use","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"definition","front":"What is meant by optimum use of resources?","back":"By optimum use we mean that the resources should be put to use in such a manner that they should give maximum benefit with minimum cost.","quote":"By optimum use we mean that the resources should be put to use in such a manner that they should give maximum benefit with minimum cost.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":37,"section":"Significance of Principles of Management"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["significance","resources"],"order":210},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.key-significance-social-responsibility","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"key_line","front":"The increased awareness of the public, forces businesses especially limited companies to fulfill their ____.","back":"The increased awareness of the public, forces businesses especially limited companies to fulfill their social responsibilities.","quote":"The increased awareness of the public, forces businesses especially limited companies to fulfill their social responsibilities.","blanks":["social responsibilities"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":38,"section":"Significance of Principles of Management"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["significance"],"order":240},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.fact-classical-school-streams","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"fact","front":"Name the other two streams of the classical school besides scientific management.","back":"The other two streams belonging to the classical school are Fayol's Administrative Theory and Max Weber's Bureaucracy.","quote":"The other two streams belonging to the classical school are Fayol's Administrative Theory and Max Weber's Bureaucracy.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":41,"section":"Taylor's Scientific Management"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["classical school"],"order":260},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.fact-taylor-engineer","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"fact","front":"What were F.W. Taylor and Henri Fayol by profession?","back":"Whereas F.W. Taylor was an American mechanical engineer, Henri Fayol was a French mining engineer.","quote":"Whereas F.W. Taylor was an American mechanical engineer, Henri Fayol was a French mining engineer.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":31,"section":"Taylor's Scientific Management"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["taylor","fayol"],"order":270},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.key-taylor-one-best-way","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"key_line","front":"Taylor thought that by scientifically analysing work, it would be possible to find `____' to do it.","back":"Taylor thought that by scientifically analysing work, it would be possible to find `one best way' to do it.","quote":"Taylor thought that by scientifically analysing work, it would be possible to find `one best way' to do it.","blanks":["one best way"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":42,"section":"Taylor's Scientific Management"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["taylor"],"order":280},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.fact-taylor-most-remembered","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"fact","front":"What is Taylor most remembered for?","back":"He is most remembered for his time and motion studies.","quote":"He is most remembered for his time and motion studies.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":42,"section":"Taylor's Scientific Management"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["taylor"],"order":290},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.fact-taylor-coined-term","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"fact","front":"Which term did Taylor coin, in which article and year?","back":"He is known for coinage of the term `Scientific Management' in his article `The Principles of Scientific Management' published in 1911.","quote":"He is known for coinage of the term `Scientific Management' in his article `The Principles of Scientific Management' published in 1911.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":42,"section":"Taylor's Scientific Management"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["taylor"],"order":300},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.def-rule-of-thumb","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"definition","front":"What is meant by the `rule of thumb'?","back":"In the earlier days of the Industrial Revolution, in the absence of an established theory of factory organisation, factory owners or managers relied on personal judgment in attending to the problems they confronted in the course of managing their work. This is what is referred to as `rule of thumb'.","quote":"In the earlier days of the Industrial Revolution, in the absence of an established theory of factory organisation, factory owners or managers relied on personal judgment in attending to the problems they confronted in the course of managing their work. This is what is referred to as `rule of thumb'.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":42,"section":"Taylor's Scientific Management"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["taylor","rule of thumb"],"order":310},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.fact-father-of-scientific-management","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"fact","front":"Who is the `Father of Scientific Management'?","back":"In this scenario, Taylor emerged as the `Father of Scientific Management'.","quote":"In this scenario, Taylor emerged as the `Father of Scientific Management'.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":44,"section":"Taylor's Scientific Management"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["taylor"],"order":320},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.def-scientific-management-implies","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"definition","front":"What does scientific management imply?","back":"It implies conducting business activities according to standardised tools, methods and trained personnel in order to increase the output, improve its quality and reduce costs and wastes.","quote":"It implies conducting business activities according to standardised tools, methods and trained personnel in order to increase the output, improve its quality and reduce costs and wastes.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":44,"section":"Taylor's Scientific Management"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["taylor","scientific management"],"order":330},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.def-scientific-management-taylor","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"definition","front":"Define scientific management (F.W. Taylor).","back":"Scientific management means knowing exactly what you want men to do and seeing that they do it in the best and cheapest way.","quote":"Scientific management means knowing exactly what you want men to do and seeing that they do it in the best and cheapest way.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":44,"section":"Taylor's Scientific Management"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["taylor","scientific management"],"order":340},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.fact-bethlehem-three-fold","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"fact","front":"By how much did productivity rise at Bethlehem Steel under scientific management?","back":"The Bethlehem Steel company where Taylor himself worked achieved three-fold increase in productivity by application of scientific management principles.","quote":"The Bethlehem Steel company where Taylor himself worked achieved three-fold increase in productivity by application of scientific management principles.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":44,"section":"Taylor's Scientific Management"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["taylor"],"order":350},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.list-taylor-principles","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"list","front":"Name Taylor's 5 principles of scientific management.","back":"Science, not the rule of thumb\nHarmony not discord\nCooperation not individualism\nMaximum not restricted output\nDevelopment of each person to her/ his greatest efficiency and prosperity","quote":"Taylor's principles of scientific management are -- Science, not the rule of thumb; Harmony not discord; Cooperation not individualism; Maximum not restricted output; Development of each person to her/ his greatest efficiency and prosperity.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":64,"section":"Principles of Scientific Management"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["taylor","principles"],"order":360,"note":"The chapter text headings give four principles; the summary list adds 'Maximum not restricted output'."},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.fact-scientific-method-work-study","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"fact","front":"In 'Science not Rule of Thumb', traditional methods were investigated through what?","back":"Work-study\nScientific method involved investigation of traditional methods through work-study, unifying the best practices and developing a standard method","quote":"Scientific method involved investigation of traditional methods through work-study, unifying the best practices and developing a standard method","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":44,"section":"Principles of Scientific Management"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["taylor","science not rule of thumb"],"order":380},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.key-principle-mental-revolution","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"key_line","front":"To achieve this state, Taylor called for complete ____ on the part of both management and workers.","back":"To achieve this state, Taylor called for complete mental revolution on the part of both management and workers.","quote":"To achieve this state, Taylor called for complete mental revolution on the part of both management and workers.","blanks":["mental revolution"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":45,"section":"Principles of Scientific Management"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["taylor","mental revolution"],"order":400},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.fact-cooperation-extension-of","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"fact","front":"Which Taylor principle is `Cooperation, Not Individualism' an extension of?","back":"There should be complete cooperation between the labour and the management instead of individualism. This principle is an extension of principle of `Harmony not discord'.","quote":"There should be complete cooperation between the labour and the management instead of individualism. This principle is an extension of principle of `Harmony not discord'.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":46,"section":"Principles of Scientific Management"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["taylor"],"order":420},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.list-taylor-techniques","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"list","front":"Name the 7 techniques of scientific management (Taylor).","back":"Functional foremanship\nStandardisation and simplification of work\nFatigue Study\nMethod Study\nTime Study\nMotion Study\nDifferential Wage System","quote":"The techniques of scientific management as per Taylor were -- Functional foremanship; Standardisation and simplification of work; Fatigue Study; Method Study; Time Study; Motion Study; and Differential Wage System.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":64,"section":"Techniques of Scientific Management"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["taylor","techniques"],"order":450},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.def-functional-foremanship","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"definition","front":"What is functional foremanship?","back":"Taylor advocated separation of planning and execution functions. This concept was extended to the lowest level of the shop floor. It was known as functional foremanship.","quote":"Taylor advocated separation of planning and execution functions. This concept was extended to the lowest level of the shop floor. It was known as functional foremanship.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":47,"section":"Techniques of Scientific Management"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["taylor","functional foremanship"],"order":460},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.list-planning-incharge","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"list","front":"Name the four specialists under the planning in-charge and their roles.","back":"Under planning incharge four personnel namely instruction card clerk, route clerk, time and cost clerk and a disciplinarian worked. These four personnel would draft instructions for the workers, specify the route of production, prepare time and cost sheet and ensure discipline respectively.","quote":"Under planning incharge four personnel namely instruction card clerk, route clerk, time and cost clerk and a disciplinarian worked. These four personnel would draft instructions for the workers, specify the route of production, prepare time and cost sheet and ensure discipline respectively.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":47,"section":"Techniques of Scientific Management"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["taylor","functional foremanship"],"order":470},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.list-production-incharge","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"list","front":"Name the four specialists under the production in-charge and their roles.","back":"Under Production incharge, personnel who would work were speed boss, gang boss, repair boss, and inspector. These respectively were responsible for timely and accurate completion of job, keeping machines and tools etc., ready for operation by workers, ensure proper working condition of machines and tools and check the quality of work.","quote":"Under Production incharge, personnel who would work were speed boss, gang boss, repair boss, and inspector. These respectively were responsible for timely and accurate completion of job, keeping machines and tools etc., ready for operation by workers, ensure proper working condition of machines and tools and check the quality of work.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":47,"section":"Techniques of Scientific Management"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["taylor","functional foremanship"],"order":480},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.key-foremanship-extension","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"key_line","front":"Functional foremanship is an extension of the principle of ____ to the shop floor.","back":"Functional foremanship is an extension of the principle of division of work and specialisation to the shop floor.","quote":"Functional foremanship is an extension of the principle of division of work and specialisation to the shop floor.","blanks":["division of work and specialisation"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":47,"section":"Techniques of Scientific Management"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["taylor","functional foremanship"],"order":490},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.def-standardisation","v":2,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"definition","front":"Define standardisation as a technique of scientific management.","back":"Standardisation refers to the process of setting standards for every business activity; it can be standardisation of process, raw material, time, product, machinery, methods or working conditions.","quote":"Standardisation refers to the process of setting standards for every business activity; it can be standardisation of process, raw material, time, product, machinery, methods or working conditions.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":48,"section":"Techniques of Scientific Management"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["taylor","standardisation"],"order":520},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.list-objectives-of-standardisation","v":2,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"list","front":"Name the four objectives of standardisation.","back":"(i) To reduce a given line or product to fixed types, sizes and characteristics.\n(ii) To establish interchangeability of manufactured parts and products.\n(iii) To establish standards of excellence and quality in materials.\n(iv) To establish standards of performance of men and machines.","quote":"(i) To reduce a given line or product to fixed types, sizes and characteristics. (ii) To establish interchangeability of manufactured parts and products. (iii) To establish standards of excellence and quality in materials. (iv) To establish standards of performance of men and machines.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":49,"section":"Techniques of Scientific Management"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["taylor","standardisation"],"order":530},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.diff-simplification-vs-standardisation","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"difference","front":"Simplification vs standardisation: the difference in aim.","back":"Simplification aims at eliminating superfluous varieties, sizes and dimensions while standardisation implies devising new varieties instead of the existing ones.","quote":"Simplification aims at eliminating superfluous varieties, sizes and dimensions while standardisation implies devising new varieties instead of the existing ones.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":49,"section":"Techniques of Scientific Management"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["taylor","simplification","standardisation"],"order":540},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.key-method-study-objective","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"key_line","front":"The objective of method study is to find out ____ of doing the job.","back":"The objective of method study is to find out one best way of doing the job.","quote":"The objective of method study is to find out one best way of doing the job.","blanks":["one best way"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":49,"section":"Techniques of Scientific Management"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["taylor","method study"],"order":550},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.key-method-study-assembly-line","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"key_line","front":"Taylor devised the concept of ____ by using method study.","back":"Taylor devised the concept of assembly line by using method study.","quote":"Taylor devised the concept of assembly line by using method study.","blanks":["assembly line"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":49,"section":"Techniques of Scientific Management"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["taylor","method study"],"order":560},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.def-motion-study","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"definition","front":"Define motion study.","back":"Motion study refers to the study of movements like lifting, putting objects, sitting and changing positions, etc., which are undertaken while doing a typical job.","quote":"Motion study refers to the study of movements like lifting, putting objects, sitting and changing positions, etc., which are undertaken while doing a typical job.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":49,"section":"Techniques of Scientific Management"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["taylor","motion study"],"order":570},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.fact-motion-study-brick-laying","v":2,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"fact","front":"Taylor and Gailberth reduced motions in brick layering from 18 to how many?","back":"5\nTaylor and his associate Frank Gailberth were able to reduce motions in brick layering from 18 to just 5.","quote":"Taylor and his associate Frank Gailberth were able to reduce motions in brick layering from 18 to just 5.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":49,"section":"Techniques of Scientific Management"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["taylor","motion study"],"order":580},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.types-motions-found","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"types","front":"Types of body motions found in a motion study.","back":"(i) Motions which are productive\n(ii) Motions which are incidental (e.g., going to stores)\n(iii) Motions which are unproductive","quote":"(i) Motions which are productive (ii) Motions which are incidental (e.g., going to stores) (iii) Motions which are unproductive.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":49,"section":"Techniques of Scientific Management"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["taylor","motion study"],"order":590},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.def-time-study","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"definition","front":"Define time study.","back":"It determines the standard time taken to perform a well-defined job.","quote":"It determines the standard time taken to perform a well-defined job.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":50,"section":"Techniques of Scientific Management"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["taylor","time study"],"order":600},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.list-time-study-objectives","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"list","front":"Name the objectives of time study.","back":"to determine the number of workers to be employed\nframe suitable incentive schemes\ndetermine labour costs","quote":"The objective of time study is to determine the number of workers to be employed; frame suitable incentive schemes and determine labour costs.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":50,"section":"Techniques of Scientific Management"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["taylor","time study"],"order":610},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.def-fatigue-study","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"definition","front":"Define fatigue study.","back":"Fatigue study seeks to determine the amount and frequency of rest intervals in completing a task.","quote":"Fatigue study seeks to determine the amount and frequency of rest intervals in completing a task.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":50,"section":"Techniques of Scientific Management"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["taylor","fatigue study"],"order":620},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.fact-differential-piece-wage","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"fact","front":"Which wage system did Taylor advocate to differentiate efficient from inefficient workers?","back":"Differential piece wage system\nTaylor was a strong advocate of piece wage system. He wanted to differentiate between efficient and inefficient workers.","quote":"Taylor was a strong advocate of piece wage system. He wanted to differentiate between efficient and inefficient workers.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":50,"section":"Techniques of Scientific Management"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["taylor","differential piece wage"],"order":630},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.def-mental-revolution","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"definition","front":"Define mental revolution (Taylor).","back":"Mental revolution involves a change in the attitude of workers and management towards one another from competition to cooperation.","quote":"Mental revolution involves a change in the attitude of workers and management towards one another from competition to cooperation.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":51,"section":"Techniques of Scientific Management"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["taylor","mental revolution"],"order":660},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.fact-fayol-life","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"fact","front":"Who was Henri Fayol?","back":"Henri Fayol (1841-1925) was a French management theorist whose theories concerning scientific organisation of labour were widely influential in the beginning of twentieth century.","quote":"Henri Fayol (1841-1925) was a French management theorist whose theories concerning scientific organisation of labour were widely influential in the beginning of twentieth century.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":53,"section":"Fayol's Principles of Management"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["fayol"],"order":670},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.list-fayol-functions","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"list","front":"Name the functions of management according to Fayol.","back":"to plan\nto organise\nto command\nto coordinate\nto control","quote":"According to Fayol, the functions of management are to plan, to organise, to command, to coordinate and to control.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":64,"section":"Fayol's Principles of Management"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["fayol","functions"],"order":690},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.list-fayol-activities","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"list","front":"Into which activities could Fayol divide all activities of an industrial undertaking?","back":"Technical\nCommercial\nFinancial\nSecurity\nAccounting\nManagerial","quote":"According to Fayol, all activities of an industrial undertaking could be divided into: Technical; Commercial; Financial; Security; Accounting and Managerial.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":54,"section":"Fayol's Principles of Management"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["fayol"],"order":700},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.list-fayol-manager-qualities","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"list","front":"Name the qualities a manager must possess, as suggested by Fayol.","back":"Physical\nMoral\nEducation\nKnowledge and experience","quote":"He also suggested that qualities a manager must possess should be -- Physical, Moral, Education, Knowledge and experience.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":54,"section":"Fayol's Principles of Management"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["fayol"],"order":710},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.fact-fayol-limitless","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"fact","front":"How many management principles did Fayol believe could help improve an organisation's operation?","back":"He believed that the number of management principles that might help to improve an organisation's operation is potentially limitless.","quote":"He believed that the number of management principles that might help to improve an organisation's operation is potentially limitless.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":54,"section":"Fayol's Principles of Management"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["fayol"],"order":720},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.fact-father-of-general-management","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"fact","front":"Who is known as the `Father of General Management'?","back":"For his contribution he is also known as the `Father of General Management'","quote":"For his contribution he is also known as the `Father of General Management'","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":54,"section":"Fayol's Principles of Management"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["fayol"],"order":740},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.list-fayol-14-principles","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"list","front":"Name Fayol's 14 principles of management.","back":"Division of work\nAuthority and responsibility\nDiscipline\nUnity of command\nUnity of direction\nSubordination of individual interest to general interest\nRemuneration of Personnel\nCentralisation and decentralisation\nScalar Chain\nOrder\nEquity\nStability in the tenure of Personnel\nInitiative\nEspirit De corps","quote":"Fayol listed 14 principles of management -- Division of work; Authority and responsibility; Discipline; Unity of command; Unity of direction; Subordination of individual interest to general interest; Remuneration of Personnel; Centralisation and decentralisation; Scalar Chain; Order; Equity; Stability in the tenure of Personnel; Initiative; and Espirit De corps.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":64,"section":"Fayol's Principles of Management"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["fayol","14 principles"],"order":750},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.key-fayol-division-of-work","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"key_line","front":"Work is divided into small tasks/ jobs. A trained specialist who is competent is required to perform each job. Thus, division of work leads to ____.","back":"Work is divided into small tasks/ jobs. A trained specialist who is competent is required to perform each job. Thus, division of work leads to specialisation.","quote":"Work is divided into small tasks/ jobs. A trained specialist who is competent is required to perform each job. Thus, division of work leads to specialisation.","blanks":["specialisation"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":54,"section":"Fayol's Principles of Management"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["fayol","division of work"],"order":760},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.key-fayol-division-quote","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"key_line","front":"The intent of division of work is to produce more and better work for the same effort. ____ is the most efficient way to use human effort.","back":"The intent of division of work is to produce more and better work for the same effort. Specialisation is the most efficient way to use human effort.","quote":"The intent of division of work is to produce more and better work for the same effort. Specialisation is the most efficient way to use human effort.","blanks":["Specialisation"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":54,"section":"Fayol's Principles of Management"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["fayol","division of work"],"order":770},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.def-fayol-authority","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"definition","front":"Define authority and responsibility (Fayol).","back":"Authority is the right to give orders and obtain obedience, and responsibility is the corollary of authority.","quote":"Authority is the right to give orders and obtain obedience, and responsibility is the corollary of authority.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":55,"section":"Fayol's Principles of Management"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["fayol","authority and responsibility"],"order":780},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.types-fayol-types-of-authority","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"types","front":"Types of authority (Fayol).","back":"official authority, which is the authority to command\npersonal authority which is the authority of the individual manager","quote":"The two types of authority are official authority, which is the authority to command, and personal authority which is the authority of the individual manager.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":55,"section":"Fayol's Principles of Management"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["fayol","authority and responsibility"],"order":790},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.key-fayol-authority-balance","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"key_line","front":"Managers require authority commensurate with their responsibility. There should be a ____ between authority and responsibility.","back":"Managers require authority commensurate with their responsibility. There should be a balance between authority and responsibility.","quote":"Managers require authority commensurate with their responsibility. There should be a balance between authority and responsibility.","blanks":["balance"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":55,"section":"Fayol's Principles of Management"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["fayol","authority and responsibility"],"order":800},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.def-fayol-discipline","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"definition","front":"Define discipline (Fayol).","back":"Discipline is the obedience to organisational rules and employment agreement which are necessary for the working of the organisation.","quote":"Discipline is the obedience to organisational rules and employment agreement which are necessary for the working of the organisation.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":56,"section":"Fayol's Principles of Management"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["fayol","discipline"],"order":810},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.list-fayol-discipline-requires","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"list","front":"What does discipline require, according to Fayol?","back":"good superiors at all levels\nclear and fair agreements\njudicious application of penalties","quote":"According to Fayol, discipline requires good superiors at all levels, clear and fair agreements and judicious application of penalties.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":56,"section":"Fayol's Principles of Management"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["fayol","discipline"],"order":820},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.key-fayol-unity-of-command-boss","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"key_line","front":"According to Fayol there should be ____ for every individual employee.","back":"According to Fayol there should be one and only one boss for every individual employee.","quote":"According to Fayol there should be one and only one boss for every individual employee.","blanks":["one and only one boss"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":56,"section":"Fayol's Principles of Management"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["fayol","unity of command"],"order":830},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.def-fayol-unity-of-command","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"definition","front":"Define the principle of unity of command.","back":"The principle of unity of command states that each participant in a formal organisation should receive orders from and be responsible to only one superior.","quote":"The principle of unity of command states that each participant in a formal organisation should receive orders from and be responsible to only one superior.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":56,"section":"Fayol's Principles of Management"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["fayol","unity of command"],"order":840},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.fact-fayol-dual-subordination","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"fact","front":"Under unity of command, what should be avoided to prevent confusion about tasks?","back":"Dual subordination\nDual subordination should be avoided. This is to prevent confusion regarding tasks to be done.","quote":"Dual subordination should be avoided. This is to prevent confusion regarding tasks to be done.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":56,"section":"Fayol's Principles of Management"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["fayol","unity of command"],"order":850},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.def-fayol-unity-of-direction","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"definition","front":"Define the principle of unity of direction.","back":"All the units of an organisation should be moving towards the same objectives through coordinated and focussed efforts. Each group of activities having the same objective must have one head and one plan.","quote":"All the units of an organisation should be moving towards the same objectives through coordinated and focussed efforts. Each group of activities having the same objective must have one head and one plan.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":56,"section":"Fayol's Principles of Management"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["fayol","unity of direction"],"order":860},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.diff-command-vs-direction-meaning","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"difference","front":"Unity of command vs unity of direction: meaning (command first, then direction).","back":"One subordinate should receive orders from and should be responsible to only one superior.\nEach group of activities having same objective must have one head and one plan.","quote":"One subordinate should receive orders from and should be responsible to only one superior. Each group of activities having same objective must have one head and one plan.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":57,"section":"Fayol's Principles of Management"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["fayol","unity of command","unity of direction"],"order":870},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.diff-command-vs-direction-aim","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"difference","front":"Unity of command vs unity of direction: aim (command first, then direction).","back":"It prevents dual subordination.\nIt prevents overlapping of activities.","quote":"It prevents dual subordination. It prevents overlapping of activities.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":57,"section":"Fayol's Principles of Management"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["fayol","unity of command","unity of direction"],"order":880},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.diff-command-vs-direction-implications","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"difference","front":"Unity of command vs unity of direction: implications (command first, then direction).","back":"It affects an individual employee.\nIt affects the entire organisation.","quote":"It affects an individual employee. It affects the entire organisation.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":57,"section":"Fayol's Principles of Management"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["fayol","unity of command","unity of direction"],"order":890},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.fact-fayol-general-interest-prevails","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"fact","front":"Fayol's subordination of individual interest to general interest: whose interests prevail?","back":"The interests of the organisation\nThe interests of an organisation should take priority over the interests of any one individual employee according to Fayol.","quote":"The interests of an organisation should take priority over the interests of any one individual employee according to Fayol.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":57,"section":"Fayol's Principles of Management"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["fayol","general interest"],"order":900},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.key-fayol-remuneration","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"key_line","front":"The employees should be paid fair wages, which should give them at least a ____. At the same time it should be within the ____ of the company.","back":"The employees should be paid fair wages, which should give them at least a reasonable standard of living. At the same time it should be within the paying capacity of the company.","quote":"The employees should be paid fair wages, which should give them at least a reasonable standard of living. At the same time it should be within the paying capacity of the company.","blanks":["reasonable standard of living","paying capacity"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":58,"section":"Fayol's Principles of Management"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["fayol","remuneration"],"order":910},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.def-fayol-centralisation","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"definition","front":"Define centralisation and decentralisation.","back":"The concentration of decision-making authority is called centralisation whereas its dispersal among more than one person is known as decentralisation.","quote":"The concentration of decision-making authority is called centralisation whereas its dispersal among more than one person is known as decentralisation.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":58,"section":"Fayol's Principles of Management"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["fayol","centralisation"],"order":920},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.key-fayol-decentralisation-large","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"key_line","front":"In general large organisations have more ____ than small organisations.","back":"In general large organisations have more decentralisation than small organisations.","quote":"In general large organisations have more decentralisation than small organisations.","blanks":["decentralisation"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":58,"section":"Fayol's Principles of Management"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["fayol","centralisation"],"order":930},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.def-fayol-scalar-chain","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"definition","front":"Define scalar chain.","back":"The formal lines of authority from highest to lowest ranks are known as scalar chain.","quote":"The formal lines of authority from highest to lowest ranks are known as scalar chain.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":58,"section":"Fayol's Principles of Management"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["fayol","scalar chain"],"order":940},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.fact-fayol-gang-plank","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"fact","front":"In Fayol's scalar chain, what is the 'Gang Plank'?","back":"A shorter route used in an emergency\nThis is a shorter route and has been provided so that communication is not delayed.","quote":"However, if there is an emergency then ‘E’ can directly contact ‘O’ through ‘Gang Plank’ as shown in the diagram. This is a shorter route and has been provided so that communication is not delayed.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":59,"section":"Fayol's Principles of Management"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["fayol","scalar chain","gang plank"],"order":950},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.def-fayol-order-states","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"definition","front":"What does the principle of order state?","back":"The principle of order states that `A place for everything (everyone) and everything (everyone) in its (her/his) place'.","quote":"The principle of order states that `A place for everything (everyone) and everything (everyone) in its (her/his) place'.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":59,"section":"Fayol's Principles of Management"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["fayol","order"],"order":970},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.key-fayol-stability","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"key_line","front":"Employee ____ should be minimised to maintain organisational efficiency","back":"Employee turnover should be minimised to maintain organisational efficiency","quote":"Employee turnover should be minimised to maintain organisational efficiency","blanks":["turnover"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":60,"section":"Fayol's Principles of Management"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["fayol","stability of personnel"],"order":990},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.def-fayol-initiative","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"definition","front":"Define initiative (Fayol).","back":"Initiative means taking the first step with self-motivation. It is thinking out and executing the plan.","quote":"Initiative means taking the first step with self-motivation. It is thinking out and executing the plan.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":62,"section":"Fayol's Principles of Management"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["fayol","initiative"],"order":1000},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.key-fayol-initiative-suggestion","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"key_line","front":"A good company should have an ____ whereby initiative/suggestions which result in substantial cost/time reduction should be rewarded.","back":"A good company should have an employee suggestion system whereby initiative/suggestions which result in substantial cost/time reduction should be rewarded.","quote":"A good company should have an employee suggestion system whereby initiative/suggestions which result in substantial cost/time reduction should be rewarded.","blanks":["employee suggestion system"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":62,"section":"Fayol's Principles of Management"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["fayol","initiative"],"order":1010},
{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.key-fayol-esprit-de-corps","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"key_line","front":"Management should promote a team spirit of ____ among employees, according to Fayol.","back":"Management should promote a team spirit of unity and harmony among employees, according to Fayol.","quote":"Management should promote a team spirit of unity and harmony among employees, according to Fayol.","blanks":["unity and harmony"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":62,"section":"Fayol's Principles of Management"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["fayol","esprit de corps"],"order":1020},
