{"id":"kc.business_studies.principles-of-management.def-principles-meaning-summary","v":1,"subject":"business_studies","chapterKey":"business_studies.principles-of-management","chapter":"Principles of Management","unit":"Principles of Management","kind":"definition","front":"Meaning of principles of management (summary).","back":"Principles of management are general guidelines, which can be used for conduct in work places under certain situations. They help managers to take and implement decisions.","quote":"Principles of management are general guidelines, which can be used for conduct in work places under certain situations. They help managers to take and implement decisions.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Business Studies Part 1","code":"lebs102","chapterNo":2,"page":64,"section":"Summary"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["principles"],"order":1120},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.def-money","v":1,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"definition","front":"What is money?","back":"Money is the commonly accepted medium of exchange.","quote":"Money is the commonly accepted medium of exchange.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":36,"section":"Money and Banking"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["money"],"order":10},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.def-barter-exchange","v":1,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"definition","front":"Define barter exchange.","back":"Economic exchanges without the mediation of money are referred to as barter exchanges.","quote":"Economic exchanges without the mediation of money are referred to as barter exchanges.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":36,"section":"Money and Banking"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["barter"],"order":20},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.example-barter-rice-clothing","v":2,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"example","front":"A person has surplus rice and wants clothing but finds no one to swap with. Which barter difficulty is this?","back":"double coincidence of wants\nIf she is not lucky enough she may not be able to find another person who has the diametrically opposite demand for rice with a surplus of clothing to offer in exchange.","quote":"However, they presume the rather improbable double coincidence of wants. Consider, for example, an individual who has a surplus of rice which she wishes to exchange for clothing. If she is not lucky enough she may not be able to find another person who has the diametrically opposite demand for rice with a surplus of clothing to offer in exchange.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":36,"section":"Money and Banking"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["barter"],"order":30},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.key-medium-of-exchange","v":1,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"key_line","front":"As explained above, the first and foremost role of money is that it acts as a ____.","back":"As explained above, the first and foremost role of money is that it acts as a medium of exchange.","quote":"As explained above, the first and foremost role of money is that it acts as a medium of exchange.","blanks":["medium of exchange"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":37,"section":"3.1 Functions of Money"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["functions"],"order":40},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.key-unit-of-account","v":1,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"key_line","front":"Money also acts as a convenient ____. The value of all goods and services can be expressed in monetary units.","back":"Money also acts as a convenient unit of account. The value of all goods and services can be expressed in monetary units.","quote":"Money also acts as a convenient unit of account. The value of all goods and services can be expressed in monetary units.","blanks":["unit of account"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":37,"section":"3.1 Functions of Money"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["functions"],"order":50},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.fact-purchasing-power-deterioration","v":1,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"fact","front":"If the general price level rises, what happens to the purchasing power of money?","back":"Thus if prices of all commodities increase in terms of money i.e., there is a general increase in the price level, the value of money in terms of any commodity must have decreased – in the sense that a unit of money can now purchase less of any commodity. We call it a deterioration in the purchasing power of money.","quote":"Thus if prices of all commodities increase in terms of money i.e., there is a general increase in the price level, the value of money in terms of any commodity must have decreased – in the sense that a unit of money can now purchase less of any commodity. We call it a deterioration in the purchasing power of money.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":37,"section":"3.1 Functions of Money"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["purchasing power"],"order":60},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.fact-money-vs-rice-storage","v":1,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"fact","front":"Why is money easier than perishable goods for carrying wealth forward?","back":"Money is not perishable and its storage costs are also considerably lower. It is also acceptable to anyone at any point of time.","quote":"Money is not perishable and its storage costs are also considerably lower. It is also acceptable to anyone at any point of time.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":37,"section":"3.1 Functions of Money"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["functions"],"order":70},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.fact-store-of-value-function","v":1,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"fact","front":"Which function of money lets individuals keep wealth for future use?","back":"Thus money can act as a store of value for individuals. Wealth can be stored in the form of money for future use.","quote":"Thus money can act as a store of value for individuals. Wealth can be stored in the form of money for future use.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":37,"section":"3.1 Functions of Money"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["functions"],"order":80},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.fact-store-of-value-stable","v":1,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"fact","front":"For money to act as a store of value, what must be true of its value?","back":"However, to perform this function well, the value of money must be sufficiently stable.","quote":"Wealth can be stored in the form of money for future use. However, to perform this function well, the value of money must be sufficiently stable.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":37,"section":"3.1 Functions of Money"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["functions"],"order":90},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.fact-other-stores-of-value","v":2,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"fact","front":"Why are assets like gold, landed property, houses or bonds less suitable than money as a store of value?","back":"It may be noted that any asset other than money can also act as a store of value, e.g. gold, landed property, houses or even bonds (to be introduced shortly). However, they may not be easily convertible to other commodities and do not have universal acceptability.","quote":"It may be noted that any asset other than money can also act as a store of value, e.g. gold, landed property, houses or even bonds (to be introduced shortly). However, they may not be easily convertible to other commodities and do not have universal acceptability.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":37,"section":"3.1 Functions of Money"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["functions"],"order":100},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.fact-money-comprises","v":1,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"fact","front":"What does money comprise in a modern economy?","back":"In a modern economy, money comprises cash and bank deposits.","quote":"In a modern economy, money comprises cash and bank deposits.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":38,"section":"3.2.2 Supply of Money"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["money supply"],"order":110},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.types-money-creating-institutions","v":1,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"types","front":"Types of institutions that create the money supply.","back":"central bank of the economy\ncommercial banking system","quote":"These are created by a system comprising two types of institutions: central bank of the economy and the commercial banking system.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":38,"section":"3.2.2 Supply of Money"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["money supply"],"order":120},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.fact-rbi-1935","v":1,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"fact","front":"When did India get its central bank, and what is it called?","back":"India got its central bank in 1935. Its name is the ‘Reserve Bank of India’.","quote":"India got its central bank in 1935. Its name is the ‘Reserve Bank of India’.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":38,"section":"3.2.2 Supply of Money"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["rbi"],"order":130},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.list-central-bank-functions","v":1,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"list","front":"Name the functions of the central bank.","back":"It issues the currency of the country.\nIt controls money supply of the country through various methods, like bank rate, open market operations and variations in reserve ratios.\nIt acts as a banker to the government.\nIt is the custodian of the foreign exchange reserves of the economy.\nIt also acts as a bank to the banking system","quote":"Central bank has several important functions. It issues the currency of the country. It controls money supply of the country through various methods, like bank rate, open market operations and variations in reserve ratios. It acts as a banker to the government. It is the custodian of the foreign exchange reserves of the economy. It also acts as a bank to the banking system, which is discussed in detail later.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":38,"section":"3.2.2 Supply of Money"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["rbi","central bank"],"order":140},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.def-high-powered-money","v":1,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"definition","front":"What is high-powered money?","back":"This currency issued by the central bank can be held by the public or by the commercial banks, and is called the ‘high-powered money’ or ‘reserve money’ or ‘monetary base’ as it acts as a basis for credit creation.","quote":"This currency issued by the central bank can be held by the public or by the commercial banks, and is called the ‘high-powered money’ or ‘reserve money’ or ‘monetary base’ as it acts as a basis for credit creation.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":38,"section":"3.2.2 Supply of Money"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["high-powered money"],"order":150},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.fact-commercial-banks-role","v":1,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"fact","front":"What do commercial banks do with the deposits they accept?","back":"They accept deposits from the public and lend out part of these funds to those who want to borrow.","quote":"They accept deposits from the public and lend out part of these funds to those who want to borrow.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":38,"section":"3.2.2 Supply of Money"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["commercial banks"],"order":160},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.def-spread","v":1,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"definition","front":"What is the ‘spread’ of a commercial bank?","back":"The interest rate paid by the banks to depositors is lower than the rate charged from the borrowers. This difference between these two types of interest rates, called the ‘spread’ is the profit appropriated by the bank.","quote":"The interest rate paid by the banks to depositors is lower than the rate charged from the borrowers. This difference between these two types of interest rates, called the ‘spread’ is the profit appropriated by the bank.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":38,"section":"3.2.2 Supply of Money"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["commercial banks"],"order":170},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.fact-banks-mediate","v":1,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"fact","front":"Between whom do commercial banks mediate?","back":"Commercial banks mediate between individuals or firms with excess funds and lend to those who need funds.","quote":"Commercial banks mediate between individuals or firms with excess funds and lend to those who need funds.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":39,"section":"3.2.2 Supply of Money"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["commercial banks"],"order":190},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.fact-bank-lend-maximum","v":1,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"fact","front":"Why must a bank not lend out all its funds?","back":"Since banks earn interest from loans they make, any bank would like to lend the maximum possible. However, being able to repay depositors on demand is crucial to the bank’s survival.","quote":"Since banks earn interest from loans they make, any bank would like to lend the maximum possible. However, being able to repay depositors on demand is crucial to the bank’s survival.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":39,"section":"3.2.2 Supply of Money"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["commercial banks"],"order":200},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.fact-why-banks-can-lend","v":1,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"fact","front":"Why can banks lend?","back":"Banks can lend simply because they do not expect all the depositors to withdraw what they have deposited at the same time.","quote":"Banks can lend simply because they do not expect all the depositors to withdraw what they have deposited at the same time.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":39,"section":"3.3 Money Creation by Banking System"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["money creation"],"order":220},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.key-new-deposit","v":1,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"key_line","front":"When the banks lend to any person, a ____ is opened in that person’s name.","back":"When the banks lend to any person, a new deposit is opened in that person’s name.","quote":"When the banks lend to any person, a new deposit is opened in that person’s name.","blanks":["new deposit"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":39,"section":"3.3 Money Creation by Banking System"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["money creation"],"order":230},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.def-balance-sheet","v":1,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"definition","front":"What is a balance sheet?","back":"Balance sheet is a record of assets and liabilities of any firm.","quote":"Balance sheet is a record of assets and liabilities of any firm.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":40,"section":"3.3 Money Creation by Banking System"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["balance sheet"],"order":240},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.fact-balance-sheet-sides","v":1,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"fact","front":"On which sides of a balance sheet are assets and liabilities conventionally recorded?","back":"Conventionally, the assets of the firm are recorded on the left hand side and liabilities on the right hand side.","quote":"Conventionally, the assets of the firm are recorded on the left hand side and liabilities on the right hand side.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":40,"section":"3.3 Money Creation by Banking System"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["balance sheet"],"order":250},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.key-balance-equal","v":1,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"key_line","front":"Accounting rules say that both sides of the balance sheet must be equal or total assets must be equal to the ____.","back":"Accounting rules say that both sides of the balance sheet must be equal or total assets must be equal to the total liabilities.","quote":"Accounting rules say that both sides of the balance sheet must be equal or total assets must be equal to the total liabilities.","blanks":["total liabilities"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":40,"section":"3.3 Money Creation by Banking System"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["balance sheet"],"order":260},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.def-assets","v":1,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"definition","front":"What are assets?","back":"Assets are things a firm owns or what a firm can claim from others.","quote":"Assets are things a firm owns or what a firm can claim from others.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":40,"section":"3.3 Money Creation by Banking System"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["balance sheet"],"order":270},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.def-reserves","v":1,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"definition","front":"What are reserves of a commercial bank?","back":"Reserves are deposits which commercial banks keep with the Central bank, Reserve Bank of India (RBI) and its cash.","quote":"Reserves are deposits which commercial banks keep with the Central bank, Reserve Bank of India (RBI) and its cash.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":40,"section":"3.3 Money Creation by Banking System"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["reserves"],"order":280},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.fact-reserves-form","v":1,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"fact","front":"In what forms are a bank’s reserves kept?","back":"These reserves are kept partly as cash and partly in the form of financial instruments (bonds and treasury bills) issued by the RBI.","quote":"These reserves are kept partly as cash and partly in the form of financial instruments (bonds and treasury bills) issued by the RBI.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":40,"section":"3.3 Money Creation by Banking System"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["reserves"],"order":290},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.formula-assets-formula","v":1,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"formula","front":"Formula: assets of a bank","back":"Assets = Reserves + Loans","quote":"Similarly, commercial banks like State Bank of India (SBI) keep their deposits with RBI and these are called Reserves. Assets = Reserves + Loans","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":40,"section":"3.3 Money Creation by Banking System"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["balance sheet"],"order":300},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.def-liabilities","v":1,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"definition","front":"What are liabilities of a firm, and the main liability of a bank?","back":"Liabilities for any firm are its debts or what it owes to others. For a bank, the main liability is the deposits which people keep with it.","quote":"Liabilities for any firm are its debts or what it owes to others. For a bank, the main liability is the deposits which people keep with it.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":40,"section":"3.3 Money Creation by Banking System"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["balance sheet"],"order":310},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.formula-liabilities-formula","v":1,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"formula","front":"Formula: liabilities of a bank","back":"Liabilities = Deposits","quote":"For a bank, the main liability is the deposits which people keep with it. Liabilities = Deposits","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":40,"section":"3.3 Money Creation by Banking System"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["balance sheet"],"order":320},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.formula-net-worth-formula","v":1,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"formula","front":"Formula: net worth","back":"Net Worth = Assets – Liabilities","quote":"Hence if assets are greater than liabilities, they are recorded on the right hand side as Net Worth. Net Worth = Assets – Liabilities","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":40,"section":"3.3.1 Balance Sheet of a Fictional Bank"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["balance sheet"],"order":330},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.fact-crr-names","v":1,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"fact","front":"By what names is the legal minimum reserve ratio known?","back":"This is called the ‘Required Reserve Ratio’ or the ‘Reserve Ratio’ or ‘Cash Reserve Ratio’ (CRR).","quote":"This is called the ‘Required Reserve Ratio’ or the ‘Reserve Ratio’ or ‘Cash Reserve Ratio’ (CRR).","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":41,"section":"3.3.2 Limits to Credit Creation and Money Multiplier"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["crr"],"order":360},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.def-crr","v":1,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"definition","front":"What is the Cash Reserve Ratio (CRR)?","back":"Cash Reserve Ratio (CRR) = Percentage of deposits which a bank must keep as cash reserves with the bank.","quote":"Cash Reserve Ratio (CRR) = Percentage of deposits which a bank must keep as cash reserves with the bank.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":41,"section":"3.3.2 Limits to Credit Creation and Money Multiplier"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["crr"],"order":370},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.def-slr","v":1,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"definition","front":"What is the Statutory Liquidity Ratio (SLR)?","back":"Apart from the CRR, banks are also required to keep some reserves in liquid form in the short term. This ratio is called Statutory Liquidity Ratio or SLR.","quote":"Apart from the CRR, banks are also required to keep some reserves in liquid form in the short term. This ratio is called Statutory Liquidity Ratio or SLR.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":41,"section":"3.3.2 Limits to Credit Creation and Money Multiplier"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["slr"],"order":380},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.fact-reserve-ratio-limits-credit","v":1,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"fact","front":"What does the statutory requirement of the reserve ratio do to the amount of credit banks can create?","back":"The statutory requirement of the reserve ratio acts as a limit to the amount of credit that banks can create.","quote":"The statutory requirement of the reserve ratio acts as a limit to the amount of credit that banks can create.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":41,"section":"3.3.2 Limits to Credit Creation and Money Multiplier"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["crr"],"order":400},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.fact-deposits-500","v":1,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"fact","front":"With a 20 per cent reserve ratio, deposits of what amount can reserves of Rs 100 support?","back":"The required reserves will be Rs 100 only when the total deposits become Rs 500. This is because for deposits of Rs 500, cash reserves would have to be Rs 100 (20 per cent of 500 = 100).","quote":"The required reserves will be Rs 100 only when the total deposits become Rs 500. This is because for deposits of Rs 500, cash reserves would have to be Rs 100 (20 per cent of 500 = 100).","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":41,"section":"3.3.2 Limits to Credit Creation and Money Multiplier"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["money multiplier"],"order":410},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.key-only-rbi-issues-currency","v":1,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"key_line","front":"Reserve Bank is the only institution which can issue ____.","back":"Reserve Bank is the only institution which can issue currency.","quote":"Reserve Bank is the only institution which can issue currency.","blanks":["currency"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":42,"section":"3.4 Policy Tools to Control Money Supply"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["rbi"],"order":440},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.def-lender-of-last-resort","v":1,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"definition","front":"What is the RBI’s role as the ‘lender of last resort’?","back":"This role of RBI, that of being ready to lend to banks at all times is another important function of the central bank, and due to this central bank is said to be the lender of last resort.","quote":"This role of RBI, that of being ready to lend to banks at all times is another important function of the central bank, and due to this central bank is said to be the lender of last resort.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":42,"section":"3.4 Policy Tools to Control Money Supply"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["rbi"],"order":450},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.types-tools-types","v":1,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"types","front":"Types of tools the central bank uses to control money supply.","back":"Quantitative tools, control the extent of money supply by changing the CRR, or bank rate or open market operations.\nQualitative tools include persuasion by the Central bank in order to make commercial banks discourage or encourage lending which is done through moral suasion, margin requirement, etc.","quote":"The tools used by the Central bank to control money supply can be quantitative or qualitative. Quantitative tools, control the extent of money supply by changing the CRR, or bank rate or open market operations. Qualitative tools include persuasion by the Central bank in order to make commercial banks discourage or encourage lending which is done through moral suasion, margin requirement, etc.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":42,"section":"3.4 Policy Tools to Control Money Supply"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["monetary policy"],"order":460},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.key-quantitative-tools","v":1,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"key_line","front":"Quantitative tools, control the extent of money supply by changing the ____, or ____ or ____.","back":"Quantitative tools, control the extent of money supply by changing the CRR, or bank rate or open market operations.","quote":"Quantitative tools, control the extent of money supply by changing the CRR, or bank rate or open market operations.","blanks":["CRR","bank rate","open market operations"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":42,"section":"3.4 Policy Tools to Control Money Supply"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["monetary policy"],"order":470},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.list-qualitative-tools","v":2,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"list","front":"Which two qualitative tools of money control does NCERT name (persuasion of banks on lending)?","back":"moral suasion\nmargin requirement","quote":"Qualitative tools include persuasion by the Central bank in order to make commercial banks discourage or encourage lending which is done through moral suasion, margin requirement, etc.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":42,"section":"3.4 Policy Tools to Control Money Supply"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["monetary policy"],"order":480},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.fact-reserve-ratio-change-effect","v":1,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"fact","front":"What happens to bank lending, deposits and money supply when the central bank changes the reserve ratio?","back":"It should be evident by now that if the Central bank changes the reserve ratio, this would lead to changes in lending by the banks which, in turn, would impact the deposits and hence, the money supply.","quote":"It should be evident by now that if the Central bank changes the reserve ratio, this would lead to changes in lending by the banks which, in turn, would impact the deposits and hence, the money supply.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":43,"section":"3.4 Policy Tools to Control Money Supply"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["crr"],"order":490},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.def-open-market-operations","v":1,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"definition","front":"What are Open Market Operations?","back":"Open Market Operations refers to buying and selling of bonds issued by the Government in the open market. This purchase and sale is entrusted to the Central bank on behalf of the Government.","quote":"Open Market Operations refers to buying and selling of bonds issued by the Government in the open market. This purchase and sale is entrusted to the Central bank on behalf of the Government.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":43,"section":"3.4 Policy Tools to Control Money Supply"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["omo"],"order":500},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.diff-omo-buy-vs-sell","v":1,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"difference","front":"RBI buying vs selling a government bond: effect on money supply.","back":"When RBI buys a Government bond in the open market, it pays for it by giving a cheque. This cheque increases the total amount of reserves in the economy and thus increases the money supply.\nSelling of a bond by RBI (to private individuals or institutions) leads to reduction in quantity of reserves and hence the money supply.","quote":"When RBI buys a Government bond in the open market, it pays for it by giving a cheque. This cheque increases the total amount of reserves in the economy and thus increases the money supply. Selling of a bond by RBI (to private individuals or institutions) leads to reduction in quantity of reserves and hence the money supply.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":43,"section":"3.4 Policy Tools to Control Money Supply"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["omo"],"order":510},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.types-omo-types","v":1,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"types","front":"Types of open market operations.","back":"outright\nrepo","quote":"There are two types of open market operations: outright and repo.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":43,"section":"3.4 Policy Tools to Control Money Supply"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["omo"],"order":520},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.def-outright-omo","v":1,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"definition","front":"What are outright open market operations?","back":"Outright open market operations are permanent in nature: when the central bank buys these securities (thus injecting money into the system), it is without any promise to sell them later.","quote":"Outright open market operations are permanent in nature: when the central bank buys these securities (thus injecting money into the system), it is without any promise to sell them later.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":43,"section":"3.4 Policy Tools to Control Money Supply"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["omo"],"order":530},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.def-repo","v":2,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"definition","front":"What is a repurchase agreement (repo)?","back":"When the central bank buys the security, this agreement of purchase also has specification about date and price of resale of this security. This type of agreement is called a repurchase agreement or repo.","quote":"when the central bank buys the security, this agreement of purchase also has specification about date and price of resale of this security. This type of agreement is called a repurchase agreement or repo.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":43,"section":"3.4 Policy Tools to Control Money Supply"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["omo","repo"],"order":540},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.def-repo-rate","v":1,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"definition","front":"What is the repo rate?","back":"This type of agreement is called a repurchase agreement or repo. The interest rate at which the money is lent in this way is called the repo rate.","quote":"This type of agreement is called a repurchase agreement or repo. The interest rate at which the money is lent in this way is called the repo rate.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":43,"section":"3.4 Policy Tools to Control Money Supply"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["repo"],"order":550},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.def-reverse-repo","v":2,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"definition","front":"What is a reverse repurchase agreement (reverse repo)?","back":"Instead of outright sale of securities the central bank may sell the securities through an agreement which has a specification about the date and price at which it will be repurchased. This type of agreement is called a reverse repurchase agreement or reverse repo.","quote":"instead of outright sale of securities the central bank may sell the securities through an agreement which has a specification about the date and price at which it will be repurchased. This type of agreement is called a reverse repurchase agreement or reverse repo.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":43,"section":"3.4 Policy Tools to Control Money Supply"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["omo","repo"],"order":570},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.def-reverse-repo-rate","v":1,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"definition","front":"What is the reverse repo rate?","back":"This type of agreement is called a reverse repurchase agreement or reverse repo. The rate at which the money is withdrawn in this manner is called the reverse repo rate.","quote":"This type of agreement is called a reverse repurchase agreement or reverse repo. The rate at which the money is withdrawn in this manner is called the reverse repo rate.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":43,"section":"3.4 Policy Tools to Control Money Supply"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["repo"],"order":580},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.fact-repo-main-tool","v":1,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"fact","front":"Which RBI operations are now the main tool of its monetary policy?","back":"The Reserve Bank of India conducts repo and reverse repo operations at various maturities: overnight, 7-day, 14-day, etc. This type of operations have now become the main tool of monetary policy of the Reserve Bank of India.","quote":"The Reserve Bank of India conducts repo and reverse repo operations at various maturities: overnight, 7-day, 14-day, etc. This type of operations have now become the main tool of monetary policy of the Reserve Bank of India.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":43,"section":"3.4 Policy Tools to Control Money Supply"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["repo"],"order":590},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.def-bank-rate","v":1,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"definition","front":"What is the Bank Rate in India?","back":"The RBI can influence money supply by changing the rate at which it gives loans to the commercial banks. This rate is called the Bank Rate in India.","quote":"The RBI can influence money supply by changing the rate at which it gives loans to the commercial banks. This rate is called the Bank Rate in India.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":43,"section":"3.4 Policy Tools to Control Money Supply"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["bank rate"],"order":600},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.fact-bank-rate-effect","v":1,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"fact","front":"How does a rise in the bank rate affect money supply?","back":"By increasing the bank rate, loans taken by commercial banks become more expensive; this reduces the reserves held by the commercial bank and hence decreases money supply. A fall in the bank rate can increase the money supply.","quote":"By increasing the bank rate, loans taken by commercial banks become more expensive; this reduces the reserves held by the commercial bank and hence decreases money supply. A fall in the bank rate can increase the money supply.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":43,"section":"3.4 Policy Tools to Control Money Supply"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["bank rate"],"order":610},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.fact-currency-issuers","v":1,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"fact","front":"Who issues currency notes and who issues coins in India?","back":"In India currency notes are issued by the Reserve Bank of India (RBI), which is the monetary authority in India. However, coins are issued by the Government of India.","quote":"In India currency notes are issued by the Reserve Bank of India (RBI), which is the monetary authority in India. However, coins are issued by the Government of India.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":48,"section":"The Supply of Money: Various Measures"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["money supply"],"order":620},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.def-demand-deposits","v":1,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"definition","front":"What are demand deposits and time deposits?","back":"Such deposits are called demand deposits as they are payable by the bank on demand from the account-holder. Other deposits, e.g. fixed deposits, have a fixed period to maturity and are referred to as time deposits.","quote":"Such deposits are called demand deposits as they are payable by the bank on demand from the account-holder. Other deposits, e.g. fixed deposits, have a fixed period to maturity and are referred to as time deposits.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":48,"section":"The Supply of Money: Various Measures"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["money supply"],"order":630},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.fact-currency-value-guarantee","v":1,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"fact","front":"From what do currency notes and coins derive their value?","back":"The value of the currency notes and coins is derived from the guarantee provided by the issuing authority of these items.","quote":"The value of the currency notes and coins is derived from the guarantee provided by the issuing authority of these items.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":48,"section":"The Supply of Money: Various Measures"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["fiat money"],"order":640},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.def-fiat-money","v":1,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"definition","front":"What is fiat money?","back":"Currency notes and coins are therefore called fiat money. They do not have intrinsic value like a gold or silver coin.","quote":"Currency notes and coins are therefore called fiat money. They do not have intrinsic value like a gold or silver coin.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":48,"section":"The Supply of Money: Various Measures"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["fiat money"],"order":650},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.def-legal-tender","v":1,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"definition","front":"What is a legal tender?","back":"They are also called legal tenders as they cannot be refused by any citizen of the country for settlement of any kind of transaction. Cheques drawn on savings or current accounts, however, can be refused by anyone as a mode of payment. Hence, demand deposits are not legal tenders.","quote":"They are also called legal tenders as they cannot be refused by any citizen of the country for settlement of any kind of transaction. Cheques drawn on savings or current accounts, however, can be refused by anyone as a mode of payment. Hence, demand deposits are not legal tenders.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":48,"section":"The Supply of Money: Various Measures"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["legal tender"],"order":660},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.def-money-supply","v":1,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"definition","front":"What is money supply?","back":"Money supply, like money demand, is a stock variable. The total stock of money in circulation among the public at a particular point of time is called money supply.","quote":"Money supply, like money demand, is a stock variable. The total stock of money in circulation among the public at a particular point of time is called money supply.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":48,"section":"The Supply of Money: Various Measures"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["money supply"],"order":670},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.fact-four-measures","v":1,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"fact","front":"Which four alternative measures of money supply does RBI publish?","back":"RBI publishes figures for four alternative measures of money supply, viz. M1, M2, M3 and M4.","quote":"RBI publishes figures for four alternative measures of money supply, viz. M1, M2, M3 and M4.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":48,"section":"The Supply of Money: Various Measures"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["money supply"],"order":680},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.formula-m1","v":1,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"formula","front":"Formula: M1","back":"M1 = CU + DD","quote":"M1 = CU + DD","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":48,"section":"The Supply of Money: Various Measures"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["money supply"],"order":690},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.formula-m2","v":1,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"formula","front":"Formula: M2","back":"M2 = M1 + Savings deposits with Post Office savings banks","quote":"M2 = M1 + Savings deposits with Post Office savings banks","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":48,"section":"The Supply of Money: Various Measures"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["money supply"],"order":700},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.formula-m3","v":1,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"formula","front":"Formula: M3","back":"M3 = M1 + Net time deposits of commercial banks","quote":"M3 = M1 + Net time deposits of commercial banks","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":48,"section":"The Supply of Money: Various Measures"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["money supply"],"order":710},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.formula-m4","v":1,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"formula","front":"Formula: M4","back":"M4 = M3 + Total deposits with Post Office savings organisations (excluding National Savings Certificates)","quote":"M4 = M3 + Total deposits with Post Office savings organisations (excluding National Savings Certificates)","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":48,"section":"The Supply of Money: Various Measures"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["money supply"],"order":720},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.def-cu-dd","v":1,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"definition","front":"In M1 = CU + DD, what are CU and DD?","back":"where, CU is currency (notes plus coins) held by the public and DD is net demand deposits held by commercial banks.","quote":"where, CU is currency (notes plus coins) held by the public and DD is net demand deposits held by commercial banks.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":48,"section":"The Supply of Money: Various Measures"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":["money supply"],"order":730},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.fact-net-deposits","v":1,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"fact","front":"What does the word ‘net’ mean in net demand deposits?","back":"The word ‘net’ implies that only deposits of the public held by the banks are to be included in money supply. The interbank deposits, which a commercial bank holds in other commercial banks, are not to be regarded as part of money supply.","quote":"The word ‘net’ implies that only deposits of the public held by the banks are to be included in money supply. The interbank deposits, which a commercial bank holds in other commercial banks, are not to be regarded as part of money supply.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":48,"section":"The Supply of Money: Various Measures"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["money supply"],"order":740},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.types-narrow-broad-money","v":2,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"types","front":"Which measures of money supply are narrow money and which are broad money?","back":"M1 and M2 are known as narrow money.\nM3 and M4 are known as broad money.","quote":"M1 and M2 are known as narrow money. M3 and M4 are known as broad money.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":48,"section":"The Supply of Money: Various Measures"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["money supply"],"order":750},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.fact-liquidity-order","v":1,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"fact","front":"Rank M1 to M4 by liquidity. Which is most and which is least liquid?","back":"These measures are in decreasing order of liquidity. M1 is most liquid and easiest for transactions whereas M4 is least liquid of all.","quote":"These measures are in decreasing order of liquidity. M1 is most liquid and easiest for transactions whereas M4 is least liquid of all.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":48,"section":"The Supply of Money: Various Measures"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["money supply"],"order":760},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.fact-m3-common","v":1,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"fact","front":"Which is the most commonly used measure of money supply?","back":"M3 is the most commonly used measure of money supply.","quote":"M3 is the most commonly used measure of money supply.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":48,"section":"The Supply of Money: Various Measures"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["money supply"],"order":770},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.key-summary-barter","v":2,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"key_line","front":"Exchange of commodities without the mediation of money is called Barter Exchange. It suffers from lack of ____.","back":"Exchange of commodities without the mediation of money is called Barter Exchange. It suffers from lack of double coincidence of wants.","quote":"Exchange of commodities without the mediation of money is called Barter Exchange. It suffers from lack of double coincidence of wants.","blanks":["double coincidence of wants"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":49,"section":"Summary"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["barter"],"order":780},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.fact-summary-supply-consists","v":2,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"fact","front":"What does the supply of money consist of?","back":"Supply of money, on the other hand, consists of currency notes and coins, demand and time deposits held by commercial banks, etc.","quote":"Supply of money, on the other hand, consists of currency notes and coins, demand and time deposits held by commercial banks, etc.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":49,"section":"Summary"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["money supply"],"order":790},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.fact-summary-monetary-authority","v":2,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"fact","front":"In India, which institution regulates the supply of money and acts as the monetary authority?","back":"In India, the supply of money is regulated by the Reserve Bank of India (RBI) which acts as the monetary authority of the country.","quote":"In India, the supply of money is regulated by the Reserve Bank of India (RBI) which acts as the monetary authority of the country.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":49,"section":"Summary"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["rbi"],"order":800},
{"id":"kc.economics.money-and-banking.key-summary-rbi-regulates","v":1,"subject":"economics","chapterKey":"economics.money-and-banking","chapter":"Money & Banking","unit":"Money and Banking","kind":"key_line","front":"RBI regulates money supply by controlling the stock of ____, the ____ and ____ of the commercial banks.","back":"RBI regulates money supply by controlling the stock of high powered money, the bank rate and reserve requirements of the commercial banks.","quote":"RBI regulates money supply by controlling the stock of high powered money, the bank rate and reserve requirements of the commercial banks.","blanks":["high powered money","bank rate","reserve requirements"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Introductory Macroeconomics","code":"leec103","chapterNo":3,"page":50,"section":"Summary"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":["rbi"],"order":810},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.def-dna-is-a-long-polymer-1","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"definition","front":"Define DNA.","back":"DNA is a long polymer of deoxyribonucleotides.","quote":"DNA is a long polymer of deoxyribonucleotides.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":80,"section":"5.1 The DNA"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":10},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.list-a-nucleotide-has-three-components-1","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"list","front":"Name the three components of a nucleotide.","back":"a nitrogenous base\na pentose sugar (ribose in case of RNA, and deoxyribose for DNA)\na phosphate group","quote":"A nucleotide has three components – a nitrogenous base, a pentose sugar (ribose in case of RNA, and deoxyribose for DNA), and a phosphate group.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":80,"section":"5.1.1 Structure of Polynucleotide Chain"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":20},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.types-there-are-two-types-of-1","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"types","front":"Types of nitrogenous bases, with their members.","back":"Purines (Adenine and Guanine)\nPyrimidines (Cytosine, Uracil and Thymine)","quote":"There are two types of nitrogenous bases – Purines (Adenine and Guanine), and Pyrimidines (Cytosine, Uracil and Thymine).","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":80,"section":"5.1.1 Structure of Polynucleotide Chain"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":30},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.kl-uracil-is-present-in-rna-1","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"key_line","front":"____ is present in RNA at the place of Thymine.","back":"Uracil is present in RNA at the place of Thymine.","quote":"Uracil is present in RNA at the place of Thymine.","blanks":["Uracil"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":80,"section":"5.1.1 Structure of Polynucleotide Chain"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":40},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.term-a-nitrogenous-base-is-linked-1","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"term","front":"A nitrogenous base is linked to the OH of 1' C pentose sugar through a ____ linkage to form a nucleoside","back":"A nitrogenous base is linked to the OH of 1' C pentose sugar through a N-glycosidic linkage to form a nucleoside","quote":"A nitrogenous base is linked to the OH of 1' C pentose sugar through a N-glycosidic linkage to form a nucleoside","blanks":["N-glycosidic"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":80,"section":"5.1.1 Structure of Polynucleotide Chain"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":50},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-phosphoester-linkage-nucleotide-2","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"Which linkage joins a phosphate group to the OH of 5' C of a nucleoside to form a nucleotide?","back":"When a phosphate group is linked to OH of 5' C of a nucleoside through phosphoester linkage, a corresponding nucleotide (or deoxynucleotide depending upon the type of sugar present) is formed.","quote":"When a phosphate group is linked to OH of 5' C of a nucleoside through phosphoester linkage, a corresponding nucleotide (or deoxynucleotide depending upon the type of sugar present) is formed.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":80,"section":"5.1.1 Structure of Polynucleotide Chain"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":60},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.term-two-nucleotides-are-linked-through-3","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"term","front":"Two nucleotides are linked through 3'-5' ____ linkage to form a dinucleotide.","back":"Two nucleotides are linked through 3'-5' phosphodiester linkage to form a dinucleotide.","quote":"Two nucleotides are linked through 3'-5' phosphodiester linkage to form a dinucleotide.","blanks":["phosphodiester"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":80,"section":"5.1.1 Structure of Polynucleotide Chain"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":70},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.kl-in-rna-every-nucleotide-residue-2","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"key_line","front":"In RNA, every nucleotide residue has an additional –OH group present at 2'-position in the ____.","back":"In RNA, every nucleotide residue has an additional –OH group present at 2'-position in the ribose.","quote":"In RNA, every nucleotide residue has an additional –OH group present at 2'-position in the ribose.","blanks":["ribose"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":81,"section":"5.1.1 Structure of Polynucleotide Chain"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":80},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-dna-as-an-acidic-substance-1","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"Who first identified DNA in the nucleus, when, and what did he name it?","back":"DNA as an acidic substance present in nucleus was first identified by Friedrich Meischer in 1869. He named it as 'Nuclein'.","quote":"DNA as an acidic substance present in nucleus was first identified by Friedrich Meischer in 1869. He named it as 'Nuclein'.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":81,"section":"5.1.1 Structure of Polynucleotide Chain"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":90},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-it-was-only-in-1953-2","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"Who proposed the Double Helix model of DNA, and on whose X-ray data?","back":"It was only in 1953 that James Watson and Francis Crick, based on the X-ray diffraction data produced by Maurice Wilkins and Rosalind Franklin, proposed a very simple but famous Double Helix model for the structure of DNA.","quote":"It was only in 1953 that James Watson and Francis Crick, based on the X-ray diffraction data produced by Maurice Wilkins and Rosalind Franklin, proposed a very simple but famous Double Helix model for the structure of DNA.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":81,"section":"5.1.1 Structure of Polynucleotide Chain"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":100},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-the-observation-of-erwin-chargaff-3","v":2,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"Which observation of Erwin Chargaff also underpinned Watson and Crick's double helix model?","back":"One of the hallmarks of their proposition was base pairing between the two strands of polynucleotide chains. However, this proposition was also based on the observation of Erwin Chargaff that for a double stranded DNA, the ratios between Adenine and Thymine and Guanine and Cytosine are constant and equals one.","quote":"One of the hallmarks of their proposition was base pairing between the two strands of polynucleotide chains. However, this proposition was also based on the observation of Erwin Chargaff that for a double stranded DNA, the ratios between Adenine and Thymine and Guanine and Cytosine are constant and equals one.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":81,"section":"5.1.1 Structure of Polynucleotide Chain"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":110},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-they-are-said-to-be-4","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"Why can the base sequence of one DNA strand predict the other?","back":"They are said to be complementary to each other, and therefore if the sequence of bases in one strand is known then the sequence in other strand can be predicted.","quote":"They are said to be complementary to each other, and therefore if the sequence of bases in one strand is known then the sequence in other strand can be predicted.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":81,"section":"5.1.1 Structure of Polynucleotide Chain"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":120},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-it-is-made-of-two-5","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"Double helix of DNA: what forms the backbone and where do the bases lie?","back":"It is made of two polynucleotide chains, where the backbone is constituted by sugar-phosphate, and the bases project inside.","quote":"It is made of two polynucleotide chains, where the backbone is constituted by sugar-phosphate, and the bases project inside.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":81,"section":"5.1.1 Structure of Polynucleotide Chain"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":130},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.kl-the-two-chains-have-anti-3","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"key_line","front":"The two chains have ____ polarity. It means, if one chain has the polarity 5'→3', the other has 3'→5'.","back":"The two chains have anti-parallel polarity. It means, if one chain has the polarity 5'→3', the other has 3'→5'.","quote":"The two chains have anti-parallel polarity. It means, if one chain has the polarity 5'→3', the other has 3'→5'.","blanks":["anti-parallel"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":81,"section":"5.1.1 Structure of Polynucleotide Chain"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":140},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-adenine-forms-two-hydrogen-bonds-6","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"How many H-bonds join A-T and G-C base pairs in DNA?","back":"Adenine forms two hydrogen bonds with Thymine from opposite strand and vice-versa. Similarly, Guanine is bonded with Cytosine with three H-bonds.","quote":"Adenine forms two hydrogen bonds with Thymine from opposite strand and vice-versa. Similarly, Guanine is bonded with Cytosine with three H-bonds.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":81,"section":"5.1.1 Structure of Polynucleotide Chain"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":150},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.kl-as-a-result-always-a-4","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"key_line","front":"As a result, always a ____ comes opposite to a pyrimidine.","back":"As a result, always a purine comes opposite to a pyrimidine.","quote":"As a result, always a purine comes opposite to a pyrimidine.","blanks":["purine"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":81,"section":"5.1.1 Structure of Polynucleotide Chain"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":160},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.kl-the-two-chains-are-coiled-5","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"key_line","front":"The two chains are coiled in a ____ fashion.","back":"The two chains are coiled in a right-handed fashion.","quote":"The two chains are coiled in a right-handed fashion.","blanks":["right-handed"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":81,"section":"5.1.1 Structure of Polynucleotide Chain"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":170},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-pitch-of-the-dna-helix-6","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"What is the pitch of the DNA double helix?","back":"The pitch of the helix is 3.4 nm (a nanometre is one billionth of a metre, that is 10-9 m)","quote":"The pitch of the helix is 3.4 nm (a nanometre is one billionth of a metre, that is 10-9 m)","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":81,"section":"5.1.1 Structure of Polynucleotide Chain"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":180},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.kl-the-distance-between-a-bp-7","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"key_line","front":"The distance between a bp in a helix is approximately ____","back":"The distance between a bp in a helix is approximately 0.34 nm","quote":"The distance between a bp in a helix is approximately 0.34 nm","blanks":["0.34 nm"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":82,"section":"5.1.1 Structure of Polynucleotide Chain"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":190},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-the-plane-of-one-base-7","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"What, in addition to H-bonds, stabilises the DNA double helix?","back":"The plane of one base pair stacks over the other in double helix. This, in addition to H-bonds, confers stability of the helical structure","quote":"The plane of one base pair stacks over the other in double helix. This, in addition to H-bonds, confers stability of the helical structure","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":82,"section":"5.1.1 Structure of Polynucleotide Chain"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":200},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.def-francis-crick-proposed-the-central-2","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"definition","front":"State the central dogma (Francis Crick).","back":"Francis Crick proposed the Central dogma in molecular biology, which states that the genetic information flows from DNA→RNA→Protein.","quote":"Francis Crick proposed the Central dogma in molecular biology, which states that the genetic information flows from DNA→RNA→Protein.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":82,"section":"5.1.1 Structure of Polynucleotide Chain"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":210},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-in-some-viruses-the-flow-8","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"In some viruses, in which direction does genetic information flow?","back":"In some viruses the flow of information is in reverse direction, that is, from RNA to DNA.","quote":"In some viruses the flow of information is in reverse direction, that is, from RNA to DNA.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":83,"section":"5.1.1 Structure of Polynucleotide Chain"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":220},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.kl-dna-being-negatively-charged-is-8","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"key_line","front":"DNA (being negatively charged) is held with some proteins (that have positive charges) in a region termed as '____'.","back":"DNA (being negatively charged) is held with some proteins (that have positive charges) in a region termed as 'nucleoid'.","quote":"DNA (being negatively charged) is held with some proteins (that have positive charges) in a region termed as 'nucleoid'.","blanks":["nucleoid"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":83,"section":"5.1.2 Packaging of DNA Helix"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":230},
