{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.term-the-negatively-charged-dna-is-4","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"term","front":"The negatively charged DNA is wrapped around the positively charged histone octamer to form a structure called ____","back":"The negatively charged DNA is wrapped around the positively charged histone octamer to form a structure called nucleosome","quote":"The negatively charged DNA is wrapped around the positively charged histone octamer to form a structure called nucleosome","blanks":["nucleosome"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":83,"section":"5.1.2 Packaging of DNA Helix"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":270},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.kl-a-typical-nucleosome-contains-200-12","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"key_line","front":"A typical nucleosome contains ____ of DNA helix.","back":"A typical nucleosome contains 200 bp of DNA helix.","quote":"A typical nucleosome contains 200 bp of DNA helix.","blanks":["200 bp"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":83,"section":"5.1.2 Packaging of DNA Helix"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":280},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.kl-nucleosomes-constitute-the-repeating-unit-13","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"key_line","front":"Nucleosomes constitute the repeating unit of a structure in nucleus called ____","back":"Nucleosomes constitute the repeating unit of a structure in nucleus called chromatin","quote":"Nucleosomes constitute the repeating unit of a structure in nucleus called chromatin","blanks":["chromatin"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":83,"section":"5.1.2 Packaging of DNA Helix"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":290},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.kl-the-nucleosomes-in-chromatin-are-14","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"key_line","front":"The nucleosomes in chromatin are seen as '____' structure when viewed under electron microscope (EM).","back":"The nucleosomes in chromatin are seen as 'beads-on-string' structure when viewed under electron microscope (EM).","quote":"The nucleosomes in chromatin are seen as 'beads-on-string' structure when viewed under electron microscope (EM).","blanks":["beads-on-string"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":83,"section":"5.1.2 Packaging of DNA Helix"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":300},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.diff-in-a-typical-nucleus-some-1","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"difference","front":"Euchromatin vs heterochromatin: packing, staining and transcriptional activity.","back":"In a typical nucleus, some region of chromatin are loosely packed (and stains light) and are referred to as euchromatin. The chromatin that is more densely packed and stains dark are called as Heterochromatin. Euchromatin is said to be transcriptionally active chromatin, whereas heterochromatin is inactive.","quote":"In a typical nucleus, some region of chromatin are loosely packed (and stains light) and are referred to as euchromatin. The chromatin that is more densely packed and stains dark are called as Heterochromatin. Euchromatin is said to be transcriptionally active chromatin, whereas heterochromatin is inactive.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":84,"section":"5.1.2 Packaging of DNA Helix"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":310},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-in-1928-frederick-griffith-in-9","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"Who performed the transformation experiments in 1928, and on which bacterium?","back":"In 1928, Frederick Griffith, in a series of experiments with Streptococcus pneumoniae (bacterium responsible for pneumonia), witnessed a miraculous transformation in the bacteria.","quote":"In 1928, Frederick Griffith, in a series of experiments with Streptococcus pneumoniae (bacterium responsible for pneumonia), witnessed a miraculous transformation in the bacteria.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":84,"section":"5.2 The Search for Genetic Material"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":320},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-this-is-because-the-s-10","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"Why do S strain colonies look smooth and shiny while R strain colonies are rough?","back":"This is because the S strain bacteria have a mucous (polysaccharide) coat, while R strain does not.","quote":"This is because the S strain bacteria have a mucous (polysaccharide) coat, while R strain does not.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":84,"section":"5.2 The Search for Genetic Material"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":330},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-mice-infected-with-the-s-11","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"Griffith: what happens to mice infected with the S strain and the R strain?","back":"Mice infected with the S strain (virulent) die from pneumonia infection but mice infected with the R strain do not develop pneumonia.","quote":"Mice infected with the S strain (virulent) die from pneumonia infection but mice infected with the R strain do not develop pneumonia.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":84,"section":"5.2 The Search for Genetic Material"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":340},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-he-observed-that-heat-killed-12","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"Griffith: what happened when heat-killed S strain bacteria were injected into mice?","back":"He observed that heat-killed S strain bacteria injected into mice did not kill them.","quote":"He observed that heat-killed S strain bacteria injected into mice did not kill them.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":84,"section":"5.2 The Search for Genetic Material"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":350},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-injected-a-mixture-of-heat-13","v":2,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"Griffith: what happened when a mixture of heat-killed S and live R bacteria was injected into mice?","back":"When he injected a mixture of heat-killed S and live R bacteria, the mice died. Moreover, he recovered living S bacteria from the dead mice.","quote":"When he injected a mixture of heat-killed S and live R bacteria, the mice died. Moreover, he recovered living S bacteria from the dead mice.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":85,"section":"5.2 The Search for Genetic Material"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":360},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-he-concluded-that-the-r-14","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"What did Griffith conclude about the R strain bacteria?","back":"He concluded that the R strain bacteria had somehow been transformed by the heat-killed S strain bacteria.","quote":"He concluded that the R strain bacteria had somehow been transformed by the heat-killed S strain bacteria.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":85,"section":"5.2 The Search for Genetic Material"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":370},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-prior-to-the-work-of-15","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"Before the work of Avery, MacLeod and McCarty, what was the genetic material thought to be?","back":"Prior to the work of Oswald Avery, Colin MacLeod and Maclyn McCarty (1933-44), the genetic material was thought to be a protein.","quote":"Prior to the work of Oswald Avery, Colin MacLeod and Maclyn McCarty (1933-44), the genetic material was thought to be a protein.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":85,"section":"5.2 The Search for Genetic Material"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":380},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-they-discovered-that-dna-alone-16","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"Avery, MacLeod and McCarty: what alone from S bacteria transformed R bacteria?","back":"They discovered that DNA alone from S bacteria caused R bacteria to become transformed.","quote":"They discovered that DNA alone from S bacteria caused R bacteria to become transformed.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":85,"section":"5.2 The Search for Genetic Material"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":390},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-they-also-discovered-that-protein-17","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"Avery, MacLeod and McCarty: effect of proteases, RNases and DNase on transformation?","back":"They also discovered that protein-digesting enzymes (proteases) and RNA-digesting enzymes (RNases) did not affect transformation, so the transforming substance was not a protein or RNA. Digestion with DNase did inhibit transformation, suggesting that the DNA caused the transformation.","quote":"They also discovered that protein-digesting enzymes (proteases) and RNA-digesting enzymes (RNases) did not affect transformation, so the transforming substance was not a protein or RNA. Digestion with DNase did inhibit transformation, suggesting that the DNA caused the transformation.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":85,"section":"5.2 The Search for Genetic Material"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":400},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-the-unequivocal-proof-that-dna-18","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"Who gave the unequivocal proof that DNA is the genetic material, and when?","back":"The unequivocal proof that DNA is the genetic material came from the experiments of Alfred Hershey and Martha Chase (1952). They worked with viruses that infect bacteria called bacteriophages.","quote":"The unequivocal proof that DNA is the genetic material came from the experiments of Alfred Hershey and Martha Chase (1952). They worked with viruses that infect bacteria called bacteriophages.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":85,"section":"5.2.1 The Genetic Material is DNA"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":410},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-they-grew-some-viruses-on-19","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"Hershey and Chase: how were the viruses labelled?","back":"They grew some viruses on a medium that contained radioactive phosphorus and some others on medium that contained radioactive sulfur.","quote":"They grew some viruses on a medium that contained radioactive phosphorus and some others on medium that contained radioactive sulfur.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":85,"section":"5.2.1 The Genetic Material is DNA"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":420},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-viruses-grown-in-the-presence-20","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"Hershey and Chase: why did radioactive phosphorus label only DNA and radioactive sulfur only protein?","back":"Viruses grown in the presence of radioactive phosphorus contained radioactive DNA but not radioactive protein because DNA contains phosphorus but protein does not. Similarly, viruses grown on radioactive sulfur contained radioactive protein but not radioactive DNA because DNA does not contain sulfur.","quote":"Viruses grown in the presence of radioactive phosphorus contained radioactive DNA but not radioactive protein because DNA contains phosphorus but protein does not. Similarly, viruses grown on radioactive sulfur contained radioactive protein but not radioactive DNA because DNA does not contain sulfur.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":85,"section":"5.2.1 The Genetic Material is DNA"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":430},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.steps-radioactive-phages-were-allowed-to-1","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"steps","front":"Hershey-Chase experiment: steps after labelling, in order.","back":"Radioactive phages were allowed to attach to E. coli bacteria.\nthe viral coats were removed from the bacteria by agitating them in a blender.\nThe virus particles were separated from the bacteria by spinning them in a centrifuge.","quote":"Radioactive phages were allowed to attach to E. coli bacteria. Then, as the infection proceeded, the viral coats were removed from the bacteria by agitating them in a blender. The virus particles were separated from the bacteria by spinning them in a centrifuge.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":86,"section":"5.2.1 The Genetic Material is DNA"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":440},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-bacteria-which-was-infected-with-21","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"Hershey and Chase: which infected bacteria became radioactive, and what did it show?","back":"Bacteria which was infected with viruses that had radioactive DNA were radioactive, indicating that DNA was the material that passed from the virus to the bacteria. Bacteria that were infected with viruses that had radioactive proteins were not radioactive.","quote":"Bacteria which was infected with viruses that had radioactive DNA were radioactive, indicating that DNA was the material that passed from the virus to the bacteria. Bacteria that were infected with viruses that had radioactive proteins were not radioactive.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":86,"section":"5.2.1 The Genetic Material is DNA"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":450},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-in-some-viruses-rna-is-22","v":2,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"Which nucleic acid is the genetic material in some viruses, for example Tobacco Mosaic viruses?","back":"However, it subsequently became clear that in some viruses, RNA is the genetic material (for example, Tobacco Mosaic viruses, QB bacteriophage, etc.).","quote":"However, it subsequently became clear that in some viruses, RNA is the genetic material (for example, Tobacco Mosaic viruses, QB bacteriophage, etc.).","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":87,"section":"5.2.2 Properties of Genetic Material (DNA versus RNA)"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":460},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.list-a-molecule-that-can-act-2","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"list","front":"Name the four criteria a molecule must fulfil to act as genetic material.","back":"It should be able to generate its replica (Replication).\nIt should be stable chemically and structurally.\nIt should provide the scope for slow changes (mutation) that are required for evolution.\nIt should be able to express itself in the form of 'Mendelian Characters'.","quote":"A molecule that can act as a genetic material must fulfill the following criteria: (i) It should be able to generate its replica (Replication). (ii) It should be stable chemically and structurally. (iii) It should provide the scope for slow changes (mutation) that are required for evolution. (iv) It should be able to express itself in the form of 'Mendelian Characters'.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":87,"section":"5.2.2 Properties of Genetic Material (DNA versus RNA)"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":470},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-the-other-molecules-in-the-23","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"Which criterion for genetic material do proteins fail to fulfil?","back":"The other molecules in the living system, such as proteins fail to fulfill first criteria itself.","quote":"The other molecules in the living system, such as proteins fail to fulfill first criteria itself.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":87,"section":"5.2.2 Properties of Genetic Material (DNA versus RNA)"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":480},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.kl-2-oh-group-present-at-15","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"key_line","front":"____ present at every nucleotide in RNA is a reactive group and makes RNA labile and easily degradable.","back":"2'-OH group present at every nucleotide in RNA is a reactive group and makes RNA labile and easily degradable.","quote":"2'-OH group present at every nucleotide in RNA is a reactive group and makes RNA labile and easily degradable.","blanks":["2'-OH group"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":87,"section":"5.2.2 Properties of Genetic Material (DNA versus RNA)"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":490},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-dna-chemically-is-less-reactive-24","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"Why is DNA a better genetic material than RNA?","back":"DNA chemically is less reactive and structurally more stable when compared to RNA. Therefore, among the two nucleic acids, the DNA is a better genetic material.","quote":"DNA chemically is less reactive and structurally more stable when compared to RNA. Therefore, among the two nucleic acids, the DNA is a better genetic material.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":87,"section":"5.2.2 Properties of Genetic Material (DNA versus RNA)"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":500},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.kl-in-fact-the-presence-of-16","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"key_line","front":"In fact, the presence of ____ at the place of uracil also confers additional stability to DNA.","back":"In fact, the presence of thymine at the place of uracil also confers additional stability to DNA.","quote":"In fact, the presence of thymine at the place of uracil also confers additional stability to DNA.","blanks":["thymine"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":87,"section":"5.2.2 Properties of Genetic Material (DNA versus RNA)"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":510},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-both-dna-and-rna-are-25","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"Which of DNA and RNA mutates at a faster rate, and why?","back":"Both DNA and RNA are able to mutate. In fact, RNA being unstable, mutate at a faster rate.","quote":"Both DNA and RNA are able to mutate. In fact, RNA being unstable, mutate at a faster rate.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":87,"section":"5.2.2 Properties of Genetic Material (DNA versus RNA)"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":520},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-rna-can-directly-code-for-26","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"Which nucleic acid can directly code for protein synthesis?","back":"RNA can directly code for the synthesis of proteins, hence can easily express the characters. DNA, however, is dependent on RNA for synthesis of proteins.","quote":"RNA can directly code for the synthesis of proteins, hence can easily express the characters. DNA, however, is dependent on RNA for synthesis of proteins.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":87,"section":"5.2.2 Properties of Genetic Material (DNA versus RNA)"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":530},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-but-dna-being-more-stable-27","v":2,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"Which nucleic acid, being more stable, is preferred for storage of genetic information?","back":"The above discussion indicate that both RNA and DNA can function as genetic material, but DNA being more stable is preferred for storage of genetic information. For the transmission of genetic information, RNA is better.","quote":"The above discussion indicate that both RNA and DNA can function as genetic material, but DNA being more stable is preferred for storage of genetic information. For the transmission of genetic information, RNA is better.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":88,"section":"5.2.2 Properties of Genetic Material (DNA versus RNA)"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":540},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-rna-was-the-first-genetic-28","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"Which was the first genetic material?","back":"RNA was the first genetic material.","quote":"RNA was the first genetic material.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":88,"section":"5.3 RNA World"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":550},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-rna-used-to-act-as-29","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"In the RNA world, what two roles did RNA play?","back":"RNA used to act as a genetic material as well as a catalyst","quote":"RNA used to act as a genetic material as well as a catalyst","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":88,"section":"5.3 RNA World"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":560},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-therefore-dna-has-evolved-from-30","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"How did DNA evolve, according to the RNA world idea?","back":"Therefore, DNA has evolved from RNA with chemical modifications that make it more stable.","quote":"Therefore, DNA has evolved from RNA with chemical modifications that make it more stable.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":88,"section":"5.3 RNA World"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":570},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-after-the-completion-of-replication-3","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"In semiconservative DNA replication, what does each DNA molecule have after replication is complete?","back":"After the completion of replication, each DNA molecule would have one parental and one newly synthesised strand.","quote":"After the completion of replication, each DNA molecule would have one parental and one newly synthesised strand.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":88,"section":"5.4 Replication"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":580},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-it-has-not-escaped-our-31","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"Who wrote: 'It has not escaped our notice ... a possible copying mechanism for the genetic material'?","back":"It has not escaped our notice that the specific pairing we have postulated immediately suggests a possible copying mechanism for the genetic material (Watson and Crick, 1953).","quote":"It has not escaped our notice that the specific pairing we have postulated immediately suggests a possible copying mechanism for the genetic material (Watson and Crick, 1953).","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":88,"section":"5.4 Replication"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":590},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-matthew-meselson-and-franklin-stahl-32","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"Who proved the semiconservative replication of DNA in E. coli, and when?","back":"Matthew Meselson and Franklin Stahl performed the following experiment in 1958","quote":"Matthew Meselson and Franklin Stahl performed the following experiment in 1958","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":89,"section":"5.4.1 The Experimental Proof"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":600},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-they-grew-e-coli-in-33","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"Meselson and Stahl: how was heavy DNA obtained in E. coli?","back":"They grew E. coli in a medium containing 15NH4Cl (15N is the heavy isotope of nitrogen) as the only nitrogen source for many generations.","quote":"They grew E. coli in a medium containing 15NH4Cl (15N is the heavy isotope of nitrogen) as the only nitrogen source for many generations.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":89,"section":"5.4.1 The Experimental Proof"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":610},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-this-heavy-dna-molecule-could-34","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"Meselson and Stahl: how was heavy DNA distinguished from normal DNA?","back":"This heavy DNA molecule could be distinguished from the normal DNA by centrifugation in a cesium chloride (CsCl) density gradient","quote":"This heavy DNA molecule could be distinguished from the normal DNA by centrifugation in a cesium chloride (CsCl) density gradient","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":89,"section":"5.4.1 The Experimental Proof"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":620},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-the-dna-that-was-extracted-35","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"Meselson and Stahl: density of DNA one generation after transfer from 15N to 14N medium?","back":"the DNA that was extracted from the culture one generation after the transfer from 15N to 14N medium [that is after 20 minutes; E. coli divides in 20 minutes] had a hybrid or intermediate density.","quote":"the DNA that was extracted from the culture one generation after the transfer from 15N to 14N medium [that is after 20 minutes; E. coli divides in 20 minutes] had a hybrid or intermediate density.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":89,"section":"5.4.1 The Experimental Proof"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":630},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-composed-of-equal-amounts-of-36","v":2,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"Meselson and Stahl: what was the DNA composition of E. coli after 40 minutes (II generation)?","back":"DNA extracted from the culture after another generation [that is after 40 minutes, II generation] was composed of equal amounts of this hybrid DNA and of 'light' DNA.","quote":"DNA extracted from the culture after another generation [that is after 40 minutes, II generation] was composed of equal amounts of this hybrid DNA and of 'light' DNA.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":90,"section":"5.4.1 The Experimental Proof"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":640},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-very-similar-experiments-involving-use-37","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"Who showed semiconservative replication in chromosomes of Vicia faba?","back":"Very similar experiments involving use of radioactive thymidine to detect distribution of newly synthesised DNA in the chromosomes was performed on Vicia faba (faba beans) by Taylor and colleagues in 1958. The experiments proved that the DNA in chromosomes also replicate semiconservatively.","quote":"Very similar experiments involving use of radioactive thymidine to detect distribution of newly synthesised DNA in the chromosomes was performed on Vicia faba (faba beans) by Taylor and colleagues in 1958. The experiments proved that the DNA in chromosomes also replicate semiconservatively.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":90,"section":"5.4.1 The Experimental Proof"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":650},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.term-the-main-enzyme-is-referred-5","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"term","front":"The main enzyme is referred to as ____, since it uses a DNA template","back":"The main enzyme is referred to as DNA-dependent DNA polymerase, since it uses a DNA template","quote":"The main enzyme is referred to as DNA-dependent DNA polymerase, since it uses a DNA template","blanks":["DNA-dependent DNA polymerase"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":90,"section":"5.4.2 The Machinery and the Enzymes"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":660},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-deoxyribonucleoside-triphosphates-serve-dual-purposes-38","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"What dual purpose do deoxyribonucleoside triphosphates serve in replication?","back":"Deoxyribonucleoside triphosphates serve dual purposes. In addition to acting as substrates, they provide energy for polymerisation reaction","quote":"Deoxyribonucleoside triphosphates serve dual purposes. In addition to acting as substrates, they provide energy for polymerisation reaction","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":90,"section":"5.4.2 The Machinery and the Enzymes"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":670},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.kl-the-replication-occur-within-a-17","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"key_line","front":"The replication occur within a small opening of the DNA helix, referred to as ____.","back":"The replication occur within a small opening of the DNA helix, referred to as replication fork.","quote":"The replication occur within a small opening of the DNA helix, referred to as replication fork.","blanks":["replication fork"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":90,"section":"5.4.2 The Machinery and the Enzymes"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":680},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.kl-the-dna-dependent-dna-polymerases-18","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"key_line","front":"The DNA-dependent DNA polymerases catalyse polymerisation only in one direction, that is ____.","back":"The DNA-dependent DNA polymerases catalyse polymerisation only in one direction, that is 5'→3'.","quote":"The DNA-dependent DNA polymerases catalyse polymerisation only in one direction, that is 5'→3'.","blanks":["5'→3'"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":90,"section":"5.4.2 The Machinery and the Enzymes"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":690},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-on-one-strand-the-template-39","v":2,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"In DNA replication, on which template strand is synthesis continuous and on which discontinuous?","back":"Consequently, on one strand (the template with polarity 3'→5'), the replication is continuous, while on the other (the template with polarity 5'→3'), it is discontinuous.","quote":"Consequently, on one strand (the template with polarity 3'→5'), the replication is continuous, while on the other (the template with polarity 5'→3'), it is discontinuous.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":90,"section":"5.4.2 The Machinery and the Enzymes"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":700},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.kl-the-discontinuously-synthesised-fragments-are-19","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"key_line","front":"The discontinuously synthesised fragments are later joined by the enzyme ____","back":"The discontinuously synthesised fragments are later joined by the enzyme DNA ligase","quote":"The discontinuously synthesised fragments are later joined by the enzyme DNA ligase","blanks":["DNA ligase"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":90,"section":"5.4.2 The Machinery and the Enzymes"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":710},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.term-there-is-a-definite-region-6","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"term","front":"There is a definite region in E. coli DNA where the replication originates. Such regions are termed as ____.","back":"There is a definite region in E. coli DNA where the replication originates. Such regions are termed as origin of replication.","quote":"There is a definite region in E. coli DNA where the replication originates. Such regions are termed as origin of replication.","blanks":["origin of replication"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":90,"section":"5.4.2 The Machinery and the Enzymes"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":720},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-recombinant-dna-procedures-requires-a-40","v":2,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"What do vectors provide for propagating a piece of DNA in recombinant DNA procedures?","back":"The vectors provide the origin of replication.","quote":"The vectors provide the origin of replication.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":91,"section":"5.4.2 The Machinery and the Enzymes"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":730},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.kl-in-eukaryotes-the-replication-of-20","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"key_line","front":"In eukaryotes, the replication of DNA takes place at ____ of the cell-cycle.","back":"In eukaryotes, the replication of DNA takes place at S-phase of the cell-cycle.","quote":"In eukaryotes, the replication of DNA takes place at S-phase of the cell-cycle.","blanks":["S-phase"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":91,"section":"5.4.2 The Machinery and the Enzymes"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":740},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.kl-a-failure-in-cell-division-21","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"key_line","front":"A failure in cell division after DNA replication results into ____ (a chromosomal anomaly).","back":"A failure in cell division after DNA replication results into polyploidy (a chromosomal anomaly).","quote":"A failure in cell division after DNA replication results into polyploidy (a chromosomal anomaly).","blanks":["polyploidy"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":91,"section":"5.4.2 The Machinery and the Enzymes"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":750},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-transcription-here-also-the-principle-41","v":2,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"What is the process of copying genetic information from one strand of DNA into RNA called?","back":"The process of copying genetic information from one strand of the DNA into RNA is termed as transcription.","quote":"The process of copying genetic information from one strand of the DNA into RNA is termed as transcription.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":91,"section":"5.5 Transcription"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":760},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-in-transcription-only-a-segment-42","v":2,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"Unlike replication, how much of the DNA is copied in transcription?","back":"However, unlike in the process of replication, which once set in, the total DNA of an organism gets duplicated, in transcription only a segment of DNA and only one of the strands is copied into RNA.","quote":"However, unlike in the process of replication, which once set in, the total DNA of an organism gets duplicated, in transcription only a segment of DNA and only one of the strands is copied into RNA.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":91,"section":"5.5 Transcription"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":770},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-second-the-two-rna-molecules-43","v":2,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"What would happen if both DNA strands were transcribed at once into two RNA molecules?","back":"Second, the two RNA molecules if produced simultaneously would be complementary to each other, hence would form a double stranded RNA. This would prevent RNA from being translated into protein and the exercise of transcription would become a futile one.","quote":"Second, the two RNA molecules if produced simultaneously would be complementary to each other, hence would form a double stranded RNA. This would prevent RNA from being translated into protein and the exercise of transcription would become a futile one.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":91,"section":"5.5 Transcription"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":780},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.list-a-transcription-unit-in-dna-3","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"list","front":"Name the three regions that define a transcription unit.","back":"A Promoter\nThe Structural gene\nA Terminator","quote":"A transcription unit in DNA is defined primarily by the three regions in the DNA : (i) A Promoter (ii) The Structural gene (iii) A Terminator","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":91,"section":"5.5.1 Transcription Unit"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":790},
