{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.def-hence-it-is-subjected-to-6","v":2,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"definition","front":"What is splicing?","back":"The second complexity is that the primary transcripts contain both the exons and the introns and are non-functional. Hence, it is subjected to a process called splicing where the introns are removed and exons are joined in a defined order.","quote":"The second complexity is that the primary transcripts contain both the exons and the introns and are non-functional. Hence, it is subjected to a process called splicing where the introns are removed and exons are joined in a defined order.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":95,"section":"5.5.3 Types of RNA and the process of Transcription"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":1010},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-hnrna-undergoes-additional-processing-called-53","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"What are capping and tailing of hnRNA?","back":"hnRNA undergoes additional processing called as capping and tailing. In capping an unusual nucleotide (methyl guanosine triphosphate) is added to the 5'-end of hnRNA. In tailing, adenylate residues (200-300) are added at 3'-end in a template independent manner.","quote":"hnRNA undergoes additional processing called as capping and tailing. In capping an unusual nucleotide (methyl guanosine triphosphate) is added to the 5'-end of hnRNA. In tailing, adenylate residues (200-300) are added at 3'-end in a template independent manner.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":95,"section":"5.5.3 Types of RNA and the process of Transcription"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":1020},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-it-is-the-fully-processed-54","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"What is transported out of the nucleus for translation in eukaryotes?","back":"It is the fully processed hnRNA, now called mRNA, that is transported out of the nucleus for translation","quote":"It is the fully processed hnRNA, now called mRNA, that is transported out of the nucleus for translation","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":95,"section":"5.5.3 Types of RNA and the process of Transcription"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":1030},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-it-was-george-gamow-a-55","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"Who argued that the code should be a combination of bases?","back":"It was George Gamow, a physicist, who argued that since there are only 4 bases and if they have to code for 20 amino acids, the code should constitute a combination of bases.","quote":"It was George Gamow, a physicist, who argued that since there are only 4 bases and if they have to code for 20 amino acids, the code should constitute a combination of bases.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":95,"section":"5.6 Genetic Code"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":1040},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-suggested-that-in-order-to-56","v":2,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"Gamow: how many nucleotides should make up the code for all 20 amino acids?","back":"It was George Gamow, a physicist, who argued that since there are only 4 bases and if they have to code for 20 amino acids, the code should constitute a combination of bases. He suggested that in order to code for all the 20 amino acids, the code should be made up of three nucleotides.","quote":"It was George Gamow, a physicist, who argued that since there are only 4 bases and if they have to code for 20 amino acids, the code should constitute a combination of bases. He suggested that in order to code for all the 20 amino acids, the code should be made up of three nucleotides.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":95,"section":"5.6 Genetic Code"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":1050},
