{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-a-classical-example-of-point-63","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"Name a classical point mutation and its effect.","back":"A classical example of point mutation is a change of single base pair in the gene for beta globin chain that results in the change of amino acid residue glutamate to valine. It results into a diseased condition called as sickle cell anemia.","quote":"A classical example of point mutation is a change of single base pair in the gene for beta globin chain that results in the change of amino acid residue glutamate to valine. It results into a diseased condition called as sickle cell anemia.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":97,"section":"5.6.1 Mutations and Genetic Code"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":1170},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.kl-insertion-or-deletion-of-one-32","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"key_line","front":"Insertion or deletion of one or two bases changes the ____ from the point of insertion or deletion.","back":"Insertion or deletion of one or two bases changes the reading frame from the point of insertion or deletion.","quote":"Insertion or deletion of one or two bases changes the reading frame from the point of insertion or deletion.","blanks":["reading frame"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":97,"section":"5.6.1 Mutations and Genetic Code"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":1180},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-frameshift-insertion-or-deletion-mutations-64","v":2,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"What are insertions or deletions of one or two bases called?","back":"Insertion or deletion of one or two bases changes the reading frame from the point of insertion or deletion. However, such mutations are referred to as frameshift insertion or deletion mutations.","quote":"Insertion or deletion of one or two bases changes the reading frame from the point of insertion or deletion. However, such mutations are referred to as frameshift insertion or deletion mutations.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":98,"section":"5.6.1 Mutations and Genetic Code"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":1190},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-insertion-or-deletion-of-three-65","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"What happens when three or multiples of three bases are inserted or deleted?","back":"Insertion or deletion of three or its multiple bases insert or delete in one or multiple codon hence one or multiple amino acids, and reading frame remains unaltered from that point onwards.","quote":"Insertion or deletion of three or its multiple bases insert or delete in one or multiple codon hence one or multiple amino acids, and reading frame remains unaltered from that point onwards.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":98,"section":"5.6.1 Mutations and Genetic Code"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":1200},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-he-postulated-the-presence-of-66","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"Crick: what did he postulate to read the genetic code?","back":"He postulated the presence of an adapter molecule that would on one hand read the code and on other hand would bind to specific amino acids.","quote":"He postulated the presence of an adapter molecule that would on one hand read the code and on other hand would bind to specific amino acids.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":98,"section":"5.6.2 tRNA - the Adapter Molecule"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":1210},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-trna-has-an-anticodon-loop-67","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"Structure of tRNA: what are its anticodon loop and amino acid acceptor end?","back":"tRNA has an anticodon loop that has bases complementary to the code, and it also has an amino acid acceptor end to which it binds to amino acids.","quote":"tRNA has an anticodon loop that has bases complementary to the code, and it also has an amino acid acceptor end to which it binds to amino acids.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":98,"section":"5.6.2 tRNA - the Adapter Molecule"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":1230},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-for-initiation-there-is-another-68","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"Which tRNA is used for initiation, and for which codons is there no tRNA?","back":"For initiation, there is another specific tRNA that is referred to as initiator tRNA. There are no tRNAs for stop codons.","quote":"For initiation, there is another specific tRNA that is referred to as initiator tRNA. There are no tRNAs for stop codons.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":98,"section":"5.6.2 tRNA - the Adapter Molecule"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":1240},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-in-figure-5-12-the-69","v":2,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"What shapes does tRNA show in secondary and actual structure?","back":"In figure 5.12, the secondary structure of tRNA has been depicted that looks like a clover-leaf. In actual structure, the tRNA is a compact molecule which looks like inverted L.","quote":"In figure 5.12, the secondary structure of tRNA has been depicted that looks like a clover-leaf. In actual structure, the tRNA is a compact molecule which looks like inverted L.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":98,"section":"5.6.2 tRNA - the Adapter Molecule"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":1250},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.def-translation-refers-to-the-process-7","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"definition","front":"Define translation.","back":"Translation refers to the process of polymerisation of amino acids to form a polypeptide","quote":"Translation refers to the process of polymerisation of amino acids to form a polypeptide","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":98,"section":"5.7 Translation"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":1260},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.kl-the-amino-acids-are-joined-34","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"key_line","front":"The amino acids are joined by a bond which is known as a ____.","back":"The amino acids are joined by a bond which is known as a peptide bond.","quote":"The amino acids are joined by a bond which is known as a peptide bond.","blanks":["peptide bond"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":98,"section":"5.7 Translation"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":1270},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.def-amino-acids-are-activated-in-8","v":2,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"definition","front":"What is charging of tRNA?","back":"Formation of a peptide bond requires energy. Therefore, in the first phase itself amino acids are activated in the presence of ATP and linked to their cognate tRNA– a process commonly called as charging of tRNA or aminoacylation of tRNA to be more specific.","quote":"Formation of a peptide bond requires energy. Therefore, in the first phase itself amino acids are activated in the presence of ATP and linked to their cognate tRNA– a process commonly called as charging of tRNA or aminoacylation of tRNA to be more specific.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":98,"section":"5.7 Translation"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":1280},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-the-cellular-factory-responsible-for-70","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"What is the cellular factory for protein synthesis and what does it consist of?","back":"The cellular factory responsible for synthesising proteins is the ribosome. The ribosome consists of structural RNAs and about 80 different proteins. In its inactive state, it exists as two subunits; a large subunit and a small subunit.","quote":"The cellular factory responsible for synthesising proteins is the ribosome. The ribosome consists of structural RNAs and about 80 different proteins. In its inactive state, it exists as two subunits; a large subunit and a small subunit.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":99,"section":"5.7 Translation"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":1290},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-when-the-small-subunit-encounters-71","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"When does the process of translation begin?","back":"When the small subunit encounters an mRNA, the process of translation of the mRNA to protein begins.","quote":"When the small subunit encounters an mRNA, the process of translation of the mRNA to protein begins.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":99,"section":"5.7 Translation"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":1300},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.kl-the-ribosome-also-acts-as-35","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"key_line","front":"The ribosome also acts as a catalyst (____ in bacteria is the enzyme-ribozyme) for the formation of peptide bond.","back":"The ribosome also acts as a catalyst (23S rRNA in bacteria is the enzyme-ribozyme) for the formation of peptide bond.","quote":"The ribosome also acts as a catalyst (23S rRNA in bacteria is the enzyme-ribozyme) for the formation of peptide bond.","blanks":["23S rRNA"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":99,"section":"5.7 Translation"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":1310},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.def-a-translational-unit-in-mrna-9","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"definition","front":"What is a translational unit in mRNA?","back":"A translational unit in mRNA is the sequence of RNA that is flanked by the start codon (AUG) and the stop codon and codes for a polypeptide.","quote":"A translational unit in mRNA is the sequence of RNA that is flanked by the start codon (AUG) and the stop codon and codes for a polypeptide.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":99,"section":"5.7 Translation"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":1320},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-an-mrna-also-has-some-72","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"What are UTRs in mRNA and where are they present?","back":"An mRNA also has some additional sequences that are not translated and are referred as untranslated regions (UTR). The UTRs are present at both 5'-end (before start codon) and at 3'-end (after stop codon). They are required for efficient translation process.","quote":"An mRNA also has some additional sequences that are not translated and are referred as untranslated regions (UTR). The UTRs are present at both 5'-end (before start codon) and at 3'-end (after stop codon). They are required for efficient translation process.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":99,"section":"5.7 Translation"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":1330},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.kl-for-initiation-the-ribosome-binds-36","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"key_line","front":"For initiation, the ribosome binds to the mRNA at the start codon (AUG) that is recognised only by the ____.","back":"For initiation, the ribosome binds to the mRNA at the start codon (AUG) that is recognised only by the initiator tRNA.","quote":"For initiation, the ribosome binds to the mRNA at the start codon (AUG) that is recognised only by the initiator tRNA.","blanks":["initiator tRNA"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":99,"section":"5.7 Translation"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":1340},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-during-this-stage-complexes-composed-73","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"What happens during the elongation phase of translation?","back":"During this stage, complexes composed of an amino acid linked to tRNA, sequentially bind to the appropriate codon in mRNA by forming complementary base pairs with the tRNA anticodon.","quote":"During this stage, complexes composed of an amino acid linked to tRNA, sequentially bind to the appropriate codon in mRNA by forming complementary base pairs with the tRNA anticodon.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":99,"section":"5.7 Translation"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":1350},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.kl-at-the-end-a-release-37","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"key_line","front":"At the end, a ____ binds to the stop codon, terminating translation and releasing the complete polypeptide from the ribosome.","back":"At the end, a release factor binds to the stop codon, terminating translation and releasing the complete polypeptide from the ribosome.","quote":"At the end, a release factor binds to the stop codon, terminating translation and releasing the complete polypeptide from the ribosome.","blanks":["release factor"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":99,"section":"5.7 Translation"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":1360},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.list-regulation-could-be-exerted-at-6","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"list","front":"Name the four levels at which gene expression can be regulated in eukaryotes.","back":"transcriptional level (formation of primary transcript)\nprocessing level (regulation of splicing)\ntransport of mRNA from nucleus to the cytoplasm\ntranslational level","quote":"regulation could be exerted at (i) transcriptional level (formation of primary transcript), (ii) processing level (regulation of splicing), (iii) transport of mRNA from nucleus to the cytoplasm, (iv) translational level.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":99,"section":"5.8 Regulation of Gene Expression"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":1370},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.kl-in-prokaryotes-control-of-the-38","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"key_line","front":"In prokaryotes, control of the rate of ____ is the predominant site for control of gene expression.","back":"In prokaryotes, control of the rate of transcriptional initiation is the predominant site for control of gene expression.","quote":"In prokaryotes, control of the rate of transcriptional initiation is the predominant site for control of gene expression.","blanks":["transcriptional initiation"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":100,"section":"5.8 Regulation of Gene Expression"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":1380},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-these-regulatory-proteins-can-act-74","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"How can regulatory proteins act on RNA polymerase activity in prokaryotes?","back":"These regulatory proteins can act both positively (activators) and negatively (repressors).","quote":"These regulatory proteins can act both positively (activators) and negatively (repressors).","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":100,"section":"5.8 Regulation of Gene Expression"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":1390},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-the-operator-region-is-adjacent-75","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"Where is the operator region located, and what binds to it?","back":"The operator region is adjacent to the promoter elements in most operons and in most cases the sequences of the operator bind a repressor protein.","quote":"The operator region is adjacent to the promoter elements in most operons and in most cases the sequences of the operator bind a repressor protein.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":100,"section":"5.8 Regulation of Gene Expression"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":1400},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-the-elucidation-of-the-lac-76","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"Who elucidated the lac operon?","back":"The elucidation of the lac operon was also a result of a close association between a geneticist, Francois Jacob and a biochemist, Jacque Monod.","quote":"The elucidation of the lac operon was also a result of a close association between a geneticist, Francois Jacob and a biochemist, Jacque Monod.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":100,"section":"5.8.1 The Lac operon"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":1410},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.def-in-lac-operon-here-lac-10","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"definition","front":"What is an operon?","back":"In lac operon (here lac refers to lactose), a polycistronic structural gene is regulated by a common promoter and regulatory genes. Such arrangement is very common in bacteria and is referred to as operon.","quote":"In lac operon (here lac refers to lactose), a polycistronic structural gene is regulated by a common promoter and regulatory genes. Such arrangement is very common in bacteria and is referred to as operon.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":100,"section":"5.8.1 The Lac operon"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":1420},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.list-three-structural-genes-z-y-7","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"list","front":"Name the three structural genes of the lac operon.","back":"z\ny\na","quote":"three structural genes (z, y, and a)","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":100,"section":"5.8.1 The Lac operon"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":1430},
