{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.types-the-methods-involved-two-major-3","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"types","front":"The two major approaches (methodologies) of the Human Genome Project.","back":"Expressed Sequence Tags (ESTs)\nSequence Annotation","quote":"The methods involved two major approaches. One approach focused on identifying all the genes that are expressed as RNA (referred to as Expressed Sequence Tags (ESTs). The other took the blind approach of simply sequencing the whole set of genome that contained all the coding and non-coding sequence, and later assigning different regions in the sequence with functions (a term referred to as Sequence Annotation).","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":103,"section":"5.9 Human Genome Project"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":1640},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-the-commonly-used-hosts-were-90","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"Which hosts and vectors were used to clone DNA fragments in the HGP?","back":"The commonly used hosts were bacteria and yeast, and the vectors were called as BAC (bacterial artificial chromosomes), and YAC (yeast artificial chromosomes).","quote":"The commonly used hosts were bacteria and yeast, and the vectors were called as BAC (bacterial artificial chromosomes), and YAC (yeast artificial chromosomes).","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":103,"section":"5.9 Human Genome Project"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":1670},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-the-fragments-were-sequenced-using-91","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"Whose method was the principle of the automated DNA sequencers used in the HGP?","back":"The fragments were sequenced using automated DNA sequencers that worked on the principle of a method developed by Frederick Sanger.","quote":"The fragments were sequenced using automated DNA sequencers that worked on the principle of a method developed by Frederick Sanger.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":103,"section":"5.9 Human Genome Project"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":1680},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-alignment-of-these-sequences-was-92","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"Why were specialised computer programs developed in the HGP?","back":"Alignment of these sequences was humanly not possible. Therefore, specialised computer based programs were developed","quote":"Alignment of these sequences was humanly not possible. Therefore, specialised computer based programs were developed","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":103,"section":"5.9 Human Genome Project"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":1690},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-this-was-generated-using-information-94","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"What information was used to generate the genetic and physical maps of the genome?","back":"This was generated using information on polymorphism of restriction endonuclease recognition sites, and some repetitive DNA sequences known as microsatellites","quote":"This was generated using information on polymorphism of restriction endonuclease recognition sites, and some repetitive DNA sequences known as microsatellites","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":104,"section":"5.9 Human Genome Project"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":1710},
