{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-repeated-sequences-make-up-very-95","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"What makes up a very large portion of the human genome?","back":"Repeated sequences make up very large portion of the human genome.","quote":"Repeated sequences make up very large portion of the human genome.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":104,"section":"5.9.1 Salient Features of Human Genome"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":1780},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.def-repetitive-sequences-are-stretches-of-13","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"definition","front":"What are repetitive sequences?","back":"Repetitive sequences are stretches of DNA sequences that are repeated many times, sometimes hundred to thousand times. They are thought to have no direct coding functions, but they shed light on chromosome structure, dynamics and evolution.","quote":"Repetitive sequences are stretches of DNA sequences that are repeated many times, sometimes hundred to thousand times. They are thought to have no direct coding functions, but they shed light on chromosome structure, dynamics and evolution.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":104,"section":"5.9.1 Salient Features of Human Genome"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":1790},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.kl-chromosome-1-has-most-genes-50","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"key_line","front":"Chromosome 1 has most genes (____), and the Y has the fewest (____).","back":"Chromosome 1 has most genes (2968), and the Y has the fewest (231).","quote":"Chromosome 1 has most genes (2968), and the Y has the fewest (231).","blanks":["2968","231"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":104,"section":"5.9.1 Salient Features of Human Genome"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":1800},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.def-scientists-have-identified-about-1-14","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"definition","front":"What are SNPs?","back":"Scientists have identified about 1.4 million locations where single-base DNA differences (SNPs – single nucleotide polymorphism, pronounced as 'snips') occur in humans.","quote":"Scientists have identified about 1.4 million locations where single-base DNA differences (SNPs – single nucleotide polymorphism, pronounced as 'snips') occur in humans.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":104,"section":"5.9.1 Salient Features of Human Genome"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":1810},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.def-dna-fingerprinting-is-a-very-15","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"definition","front":"What is DNA fingerprinting?","back":"DNA fingerprinting is a very quick way to compare the DNA sequences of any two individuals.","quote":"DNA fingerprinting is a very quick way to compare the DNA sequences of any two individuals.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":105,"section":"5.10 DNA Fingerprinting"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":1820},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-dna-fingerprinting-involves-identifying-differences-96","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"DNA fingerprinting involves identifying differences in which DNA regions?","back":"DNA fingerprinting involves identifying differences in some specific regions in DNA sequence called as repetitive DNA, because in these sequences, a small stretch of DNA is repeated many times.","quote":"DNA fingerprinting involves identifying differences in some specific regions in DNA sequence called as repetitive DNA, because in these sequences, a small stretch of DNA is repeated many times.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":105,"section":"5.10 DNA Fingerprinting"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":1830},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-these-repetitive-dna-are-separated-97","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"What is satellite DNA, and how is it separated from bulk genomic DNA?","back":"These repetitive DNA are separated from bulk genomic DNA as different peaks during density gradient centrifugation. The bulk DNA forms a major peak and the other small peaks are referred to as satellite DNA.","quote":"These repetitive DNA are separated from bulk genomic DNA as different peaks during density gradient centrifugation. The bulk DNA forms a major peak and the other small peaks are referred to as satellite DNA.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":105,"section":"5.10 DNA Fingerprinting"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":1840},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.types-depending-on-base-composition-a-4","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"types","front":"Types of satellite DNA.","back":"micro-satellites\nmini-satellites","quote":"Depending on base composition (A : T rich or G:C rich), length of segment, and number of repetitive units, the satellite DNA is classified into many categories, such as micro-satellites, mini-satellites etc.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":105,"section":"5.10 DNA Fingerprinting"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":1850},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-these-sequence-show-high-degree-98","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"What property of repetitive satellite DNA forms the basis of DNA fingerprinting?","back":"These sequence show high degree of polymorphism and form the basis of DNA fingerprinting.","quote":"These sequence show high degree of polymorphism and form the basis of DNA fingerprinting.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":105,"section":"5.10 DNA Fingerprinting"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":1860},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-since-dna-from-every-tissue-99","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"Why is DNA fingerprinting useful in forensics and paternity testing?","back":"Since DNA from every tissue (such as blood, hair-follicle, skin, bone, saliva, sperm etc.), from an individual show the same degree of polymorphism, they become very useful identification tool in forensic applications. Further, as the polymorphisms are inheritable from parents to children, DNA fingerprinting is the basis of paternity testing, in case of disputes.","quote":"Since DNA from every tissue (such as blood, hair-follicle, skin, bone, saliva, sperm etc.), from an individual show the same degree of polymorphism, they become very useful identification tool in forensic applications. Further, as the polymorphisms are inheritable from parents to children, DNA fingerprinting is the basis of paternity testing, in case of disputes.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":105,"section":"5.10 DNA Fingerprinting"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":1870},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.kl-polymorphism-variation-at-genetic-level-51","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"key_line","front":"Polymorphism (variation at genetic level) arises due to ____.","back":"Polymorphism (variation at genetic level) arises due to mutations.","quote":"Polymorphism (variation at genetic level) arises due to mutations.","blanks":["mutations"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":105,"section":"5.10 DNA Fingerprinting"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":1880},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.def-in-simple-terms-if-an-16","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"definition","front":"What is DNA polymorphism in simple terms?","back":"In simple terms, if an inheritable mutation is observed in a population at high frequency, it is referred to as DNA polymorphism.","quote":"In simple terms, if an inheritable mutation is observed in a population at high frequency, it is referred to as DNA polymorphism.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":106,"section":"5.10 DNA Fingerprinting"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":1890},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-sequence-variation-has-traditionally-been-100","v":2,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"Allelic variation is a DNA polymorphism if more than one allele at a locus occurs at what frequency?","back":"Allelic (again recall the definition of alleles from Chapter 4) sequence variation has traditionally been described as a DNA polymorphism if more than one variant (allele) at a locus occurs in human population with a frequency greater than 0.01.","quote":"Allelic (again recall the definition of alleles from Chapter 4) sequence variation has traditionally been described as a DNA polymorphism if more than one variant (allele) at a locus occurs in human population with a frequency greater than 0.01.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":106,"section":"5.10 DNA Fingerprinting"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":1900},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-the-probability-of-such-variation-101","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"Why is DNA polymorphism more likely in noncoding DNA sequences?","back":"The probability of such variation to be observed in noncoding DNA sequence would be higher as mutations in these sequences may not have any immediate effect/impact in an individual's reproductive ability.","quote":"The probability of such variation to be observed in noncoding DNA sequence would be higher as mutations in these sequences may not have any immediate effect/impact in an individual's reproductive ability.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":106,"section":"5.10 DNA Fingerprinting"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":1910},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-the-technique-of-dna-fingerprinting-102","v":2,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"Who developed DNA fingerprinting, and what probe did he use?","back":"The technique of DNA Fingerprinting was initially developed by Alec Jeffreys. He used a satellite DNA as probe that shows very high degree of polymorphism. It was called as Variable Number of Tandem Repeats (VNTR).","quote":"The technique of DNA Fingerprinting was initially developed by Alec Jeffreys. He used a satellite DNA as probe that shows very high degree of polymorphism. It was called as Variable Number of Tandem Repeats (VNTR).","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":106,"section":"5.10 DNA Fingerprinting"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":1920},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.kl-the-vntr-belongs-to-a-52","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"key_line","front":"The VNTR belongs to a class of satellite DNA referred to as ____.","back":"The VNTR belongs to a class of satellite DNA referred to as mini-satellite.","quote":"The VNTR belongs to a class of satellite DNA referred to as mini-satellite.","blanks":["mini-satellite"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":106,"section":"5.10 DNA Fingerprinting"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":1930},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.kl-the-technique-as-used-earlier-53","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"key_line","front":"The technique, as used earlier, involved ____ using radiolabelled VNTR as a probe.","back":"The technique, as used earlier, involved Southern blot hybridisation using radiolabelled VNTR as a probe.","quote":"The technique, as used earlier, involved Southern blot hybridisation using radiolabelled VNTR as a probe.","blanks":["Southern blot hybridisation"],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":106,"section":"5.10 DNA Fingerprinting"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":1940},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.steps-isolation-of-dna-ii-digestion-2","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"steps","front":"Steps of DNA fingerprinting, in order.","back":"isolation of DNA\ndigestion of DNA by restriction endonucleases\nseparation of DNA fragments by electrophoresis\ntransferring (blotting) of separated DNA fragments to synthetic membranes, such as nitrocellulose or nylon\nhybridisation using labelled VNTR probe\ndetection of hybridised DNA fragments by autoradiography","quote":"isolation of DNA, (ii) digestion of DNA by restriction endonucleases, (iii) separation of DNA fragments by electrophoresis, (iv) transferring (blotting) of separated DNA fragments to synthetic membranes, such as nitrocellulose or nylon, (v) hybridisation using labelled VNTR probe, and (vi) detection of hybridised DNA fragments by autoradiography.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":106,"section":"5.10 DNA Fingerprinting"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":1950},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-a-small-dna-sequence-is-103","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"How are VNTRs arranged, and how does their copy number vary?","back":"A small DNA sequence is arranged tandemly in many copy numbers. The copy number varies from chromosome to chromosome in an individual. The numbers of repeat show very high degree of polymorphism.","quote":"A small DNA sequence is arranged tandemly in many copy numbers. The copy number varies from chromosome to chromosome in an individual. The numbers of repeat show very high degree of polymorphism.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":106,"section":"5.10 DNA Fingerprinting"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":1960},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-it-differs-from-individual-to-104","v":1,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"In whom is the DNA fingerprint pattern the same?","back":"It differs from individual to individual in a population except in the case of monozygotic (identical) twins.","quote":"It differs from individual to individual in a population except in the case of monozygotic (identical) twins.","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":106,"section":"5.10 DNA Fingerprinting"},"importance":"must","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":1970},
{"id":"kc.biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance.fact-the-sensitivity-of-the-technique-105","v":2,"subject":"biology","chapterKey":"biology.molecular-basis-of-inheritance","chapter":"Molecular Basis of Inheritance","unit":"Genetics and Evolution","kind":"fact","front":"What increased the sensitivity of DNA fingerprinting?","back":"The sensitivity of the technique has been increased by use of polymerase chain reaction (PCR– you will study about it in Chapter 9).","quote":"The sensitivity of the technique has been increased by use of polymerase chain reaction (PCR– you will study about it in Chapter 9).","blanks":[],"source":{"kind":"ncert","book":"Biology","code":"lebo105","chapterNo":5,"page":106,"section":"5.10 DNA Fingerprinting"},"importance":"good","asked":0,"tags":[],"order":1980},
